            "claim": "The first uses error $\\varepsilon$ on the expert distribution. The second is DAgger’s conditional, infinite-sample Theorem 3.2: after $N=\\tilde{O}(uT)$ iterations there exists a policy $\\hat{\\pi}$ in the learned sequence satisfying the bound. Here $\\varepsilon_N$ is the true loss of the best policy in hindsight across the collected mixed-policy state distributions, not the expert-distribution error above. The factor $u$ bounds the increase in expert cost-to-go after one action. Linear-in-$T$ excess cost requires $u$ independent of $T$; in the worst case $u$ can be $O(T)$. The loss and mixing assumptions are stated in the <Term id=\"dagger\">DAgger</Term> section below <Cite id=\"dagger-2011\" />. Physical Intelligence restated the same point in operational terms in 2025: it is relatively easy to get a learned policy to succeed at a task some of the time, and much harder to make it succeed reliably <Cite id=\"pistar06-blog-2025\" />.",
