r the traditional supervised learning approach. However, in many cases uu is O⁡(1)O(1) or sub-linear in TT and the forward algorithm leads to improved performance. For instance if CC is the 0-1 loss with respect to the expert, then u≤1u\\\\leq 1. Additionally if π∗\\\\pi^{\\*} is able to recover from mistakes made by π\\\\pi, in the sense that within a few steps, π∗\\\\pi^{\\*} is back in a distribution of states that is close to what π∗\\\\pi^{\\*} would be in if π∗\\\\pi^{\\*} had been executed initially instead of π\\\\pi, then uu will be O⁡(1)O(1). 222<br><br><br><br>This is the case for instance in Markov Desision Processes (MDPs) when the Markov Chain defined by the system dynamics and policy π∗\\\\pi^{\\*} is rapidly mixing. In particular, if it is α\\\\alpha-mixing with exponential decay rate δ\\\\delta then uu is O⁡(11−exp⁡(−δ))O(\\\\frac{1}{1-\\\\exp(-\\\\delta)}). A drawback of the forward algorithm is that it is impractical when TT is large (or undefined) as we must train TT different policies sequentially and cannot stop the algorithm before we complete all TT iterations. Hence it can not be applied to most real-world applications.<br>[Separate literal excerpt from the same retained response]<br>The main result of our analysis in the next section is the following guarantee for DAgger. Let π1:N\\\\pi\\_{1:N} denote the sequence of policies π1,π2,…,πN\\\\pi\\_{1},\\\\pi\\_{2},\\\\dots,\\\\pi\\_{N}. Assume ℓ\\\\ell is strongly convex and bounded over Π\\\\Pi. Suppose βi≤(1−α)i−1\\\\beta\\_{i}\\\\leq(1-\\\\alpha)^{i-1} for all ii for some constant α\\\\alpha independent of TT. Let ϵN=minπ∈Π⁡1N​∑i=1N𝔼s∼dπi​\\[ℓ⁡(s,π)\\]\\\\epsilon\\_{N}=\\\\min\\_{\\\\pi\\\\in\\\\Pi}\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum\\_{i=1}^{N}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d\\_{\\\\pi\\_{i}}}\\[\\\\ell(s,\\\\pi)\\] be the true loss of the best policy in hindsight. Then the following holds in the infinite sample case (infinite number of sample trajectories at each iteration):<br><br>###### Theorem 3.1.<br><br>For DAgger, if NN is O~​(T)\\\\tilde{O}(T) there exists a policy π^∈π^1:N\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\\\in\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\_{1:N} s.t. 𝔼s∼dπ^​\\[ℓ⁡(s,π^)\\]≤ϵN+O⁡(1/T)\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d\\_{\\\\hat{\\\\pi}}}\\[\\\\ell(s,\\\\hat{\\\\pi})\\]\\\\leq\\\\epsilon\\_{N}+O(1/T)<br><br>In particular, this holds for the policy π^=arg​minπ∈π^1:N𝔼s∼dπ\\[ℓ(s,π)\\]\\\\hat{\\\\pi}=\\\\argmin\\_{\\\\pi\\\\in\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\_{1:N}}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d\\_{\\\\pi}}\\[\\\\ell(s,\\\\pi)\\]. 333<br><br><br><br>It is not necessary to find the best policy in the sequence that minimizes the loss under its distribution; the same guarantee holds for the policy which uniformly randomly picks one policy in the sequence π^1:N\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\_{1:N} and executes that policy for TT steps.<br>If the task cost function CC corresponds to (or is upper bounded by) the surrogate loss ℓ\\\\ell then this bound tells us directly that J⁡(π^)≤T​ϵN+O⁡(1)J(\\\\hat{\\\\pi})\\\\leq T\\\\epsilon\\_{N}+O(1). For arbitrary task cost function CC, then if ℓ\\\\ell is an upper bound on the 0-1 loss with respect to π∗\\\\pi^{\\*}, combining this result with Theorem [2.2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1011.0686#S2.Thmtheorem2 \"Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Forward Training ‣ 2 PRELIMINARIES\") yields that:<br><br>###### Theorem 3.2.<br><br>For DAgger, if NN is O~​(u​T)\\\\tilde{O}(uT) there exists a policy π^∈π^1:N\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\\\in\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\_{1:N} s.t. J⁡(π^)≤J⁡(π∗)+u​T​ϵN+O⁡(1)J(\\\\hat{\\\\pi})\\\\leq J(\\\\pi^{\\*})+uT\\\\epsilon\\_{N}+O(1).",
