            "supportingPassage": "In our implementation, we fix the chunk size to be kk: every kk steps, the agent receives an observation, generates the next kk actions, and executes the actions in sequence (Figure [3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F3 \"Fig. 3 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\")). This implies a kk-fold reduction in the effective horizon of the task. Concretely, the policy models πθ(at:t+k\\&#124;st)\\\\pi\\_{\\\\theta}(a\\_{t:t+k}\\&#124;s\\_{t}) instead of πθ​(at\\&#124;st)\\\\pi\\_{\\\\theta}(a\\_{t}\\&#124;s\\_{t}). Chunking can also help model non-Markovian behavior in human demonstrations. Specifically, a single-step policy would struggle with temporally correlated confounders, such as pauses in the middle of a demonstration \\[ [61](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib61 \"\")\\], since the behavior not only depends on the state, but also the timestep. Action chunking can mitigate this issue when the confounder is within a chunk, without introducing the causal confusion issue for history-conditioned policies \\[ [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib12 \"\")\\].",
