            "supportingPassage": "Section 2, bounded task cost and total cost: “We assume CC is bounded in \\[0,1\\]\\[0,1\\]. The total cost of executing policy π\\\\pi for TT-steps ( _i.e._, the cost-to-go) is denoted J⁡(π)=∑t=1T𝔼s∼dπt​\\[Cπ​(s)\\]=T​𝔼s∼dπ​\\[Cπ​(s)\\]J(\\\\pi)=\\\\sum\\_{t=1}^{T}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d^{t}\\_{\\\\pi}}\\[C\\_{\\\\pi}(s)\\]=T\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d\\_{\\\\pi}}\\[C\\_{\\\\pi}(s)\\].” Section 2.1, assumptions and Theorem 2.1: “Assuming ℓ⁡(s,π)\\\\ell(s,\\\\pi) is the 0-1 loss (or upper bound on the 0-1 loss) implies the following performance guarantee with respect to any task cost function CC bounded in \\[0,1\\]\\[0,1\\]:<br><br>###### Theorem 2.1.<br><br>( [Ross and Bagnell 2010](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1011.0686#bib.bib17 \"\")) Let 𝔼s∼dπ∗​\\[ℓ⁡(s,π)\\]=ϵ\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{s\\\\sim d\\_{\\\\pi^{\\*}}}\\[\\\\ell(s,\\\\pi)\\]=\\\\epsilon, then J⁡(π)≤J⁡(π∗)+T2​ϵJ(\\\\pi)\\\\leq J(\\\\pi^{\\*})+T^{2}\\\\epsilon.” Section 2.1, tightness and footnote 1: “Note that this bound is tight, i.e. there exist problems such that a policy π\\\\pi with ϵ\\\\epsilon 0-1 loss on dπ∗d\\_{\\\\pi^{\\*}} can incur extra cost that grows quadratically in TT. [Kääriäinen 2006](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1011.0686#bib.bib9 \"\") demonstrated this in a sequence prediction setting111<br><br><br><br>In their example, an error rate of ϵ>0\\\\epsilon>0 when trained to predict the next output in sequence with the previous correct output as input can lead to an expected number of mistakes of T2−1−(1−2​ϵ)T+14​ϵ+12\\\\frac{T}{2}-\\\\frac{1-(1-2\\\\epsilon)^{T+1}}{4\\\\epsilon}+\\\\frac{1}{2} over sequences of length TT at test time. This is bounded by T2​ϵT^{2}\\\\epsilon and behaves as Θ⁡(T2​ϵ)\\\\Theta(T^{2}\\\\epsilon) for small ϵ\\\\epsilon. and [Ross and Bagnell 2010](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1011.0686#bib.bib17 \"\") provided an imitation learning example where J⁡(π^s​u​p)=(1−ϵ​T)​J​(π∗)+T2​ϵJ(\\\\hat{\\\\pi}\\_{sup})=(1-\\\\epsilon T)J(\\\\pi^{\\*})+T^{2}\\\\epsilon. Hence the traditional supervised learning approach has poor performance guarantees due to the quadratic growth in TT. Instead we would prefer approaches that can guarantee growth linear or near-linear in TT and ϵ\\\\epsilon. The following two approaches from [Ross and Bagnell 2010](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1011.0686#bib.bib17 \"\") achieve this on some classes of imitation learning problems, including all those where surrogate loss ℓ\\\\ell upper bounds CC.”",
