            "supportingPassage": "### IV-CImplementing ACT We implement the CVAE encoder and decoder with transformers, as transformers are designed for both synthesizing information across a sequence and generating new sequences. The CVAE encoder is implemented with a BERT-like transformer encoder \\[ [13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib13 \"\")\\]. The inputs to the encoder are the current joint positions and the target action sequence of length kk from the demonstration dataset, prepended by a learned “\\[CLS\\]” token similar to BERT. This forms a k+2k+2 length input (Figure [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F2 \"Fig. 2 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") left). After passing through the transformer, the feature corresponding to “\\[CLS\\]” is used to predict the mean and variance of the “style variable” zz, which is then used as input to the decoder. The CVAE decoder (i.e. the policy) takes the current observations and zz as the input, and predicts the next kk actions (Figure [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F2 \"Fig. 2 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") right). We use ResNet image encoders, a transformer encoder, and a transformer decoder to implement the CVAE decoder. Intuitively, the transformer encoder synthesizes information from different camera viewpoints, the joint positions, and the style variable, and the transformer decoder generates a coherent action sequence. The observation includes 4 RGB images, each at 480×640480\\\\times 640 resolution, and joint positions for two robot arms (7+7=14 DoF in total). The action space is the absolute joint positions for two robots, a 14-dimensional vector. Thus with action chunking, the policy outputs a k×14k\\\\times 14 tensor given the current observation. The policy first process the images with ResNet18 backbones \\[ [22](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib22 \"\")\\], which convert 480×640×3480\\\\times 640\\\\times 3 RGB images into 15×20×51215\\\\times 20\\\\times 512 feature maps. We then flatten along the spatial dimension to obtain a sequence of 300×512300\\\\times 512. To preserve the spatial information, we add a 2D sinusoidal position embedding to the feature sequence \\[ [8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib8 \"\")\\]. Repeating this for all 4 images gives a feature sequence of 1200×5121200\\\\times 512 in dimension. We then append two more features: the current joint positions and the “style variable” zz. They are projected from their original dimensions to 512512 through linear layers respectively. Thus, the input to the transformer encoder is 1202×5121202\\\\times 512. The transformer decoder conditions on the encoder output through cross-attention, where the input sequence is a fixed position embedding, with dimensions k×512k\\\\times 512, and the keys and values are coming from the encoder. This gives the transformer decoder an output dimension of k×512k\\\\times 512, which is then down-projected with an MLP into k×14k\\\\times 14, corresponding to the predicted target joint positions for the next kk steps. We use L1 loss for reconstruction instead of the more common L2 loss: we noted that L1 loss leads to more precise modeling of the action sequence. We also noted degraded performance when using delta joint positions as actions instead of target joint positions. We include a detailed architecture diagram in Appendix [-C](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#A0.SS3 \"-C Detailed Architecture Diagram ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"). We summarize the training and inference of ACT in Algorithms [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg1 \"Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") and [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg2 \"Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"). The model has around 80M parameters, and we train from scratch for each task. The training takes around 5 hours on a single 11G RTX 2080 Ti GPU, and the inference time is around 0.01 seconds on the same machine.",
