          {
            "claim": "The paper ships two denoiser backbones <Cite id=\"diffusion-policy-2023\" />. Its CNN adopts a temporal backbone from Diffuser with modifications: it denoises action sequences conditioned on observations instead of a concatenated state-action trajectory <Cite id=\"diffusion-policy-2023\" />. Diffuser's backbone is a one-dimensional temporal U-Net over state-action trajectories <Cite id=\"diffuser-2022\" />: CNN (default): 1D temporal U-Net over the action sequence.",
            "line": 188,
            "verdict": "corrected",
            "outcome": "passing",
            "citationId": "",
            "sourceUrl": "",
            "supportingPassage": "",
            "evidenceFailures": [],
            "sourceChecked": "diffusion-policy-2023: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137; diffuser-2022: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.09991",
            "note": "Default means the DP recommendation, not universal superiority. Historical Diffuser six-block locator corrected from Appendix A to Appendix C item 1; no exact DP residual count or diagram operator is inferred. Original tuple and identity archived in current-claim-history-20260907; source excerpts/provenance in the current closeout receipt.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "dp-current-10-20260907",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "dp-backbone",
                  "citationId": "diffusion-policy-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137",
                  "supportingPassage": "We adopt the 1D temporal CNN from [Janner et al. 2022b](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib24 \"\") with a few modifications:\nFirst, we only model the conditional distribution p⁡(𝐀t\\|𝐎t)p(\\\\mathbf{A}\\_{t}\\|\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t}) by conditioning the action generation process on observation features 𝐎t\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t} with Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) [Perez et al. 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib39 \"\") as well as denoising iteration kk, shown in Fig [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#S1.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion\") (b).\nSecond, we only predict the action trajectory instead of the concatenated observation action trajectory.\nThird, we removed inpainting-based goal state conditioning due to incompatibility with our framework utilizing a receding prediction horizon. However, goal conditioning is still possible with the same FiLM conditioning method used for observations.\n\n[Separate excerpt from this same retained response]\n\nIn general, we recommend starting with the CNN-based diffusion policy implementation as the first attempt at a new task. If performance is low due to task complexity or high-rate action changes, then the Time-series Diffusion Transformer formulation can be used to potentially improve performance at the cost of additional tuning.\n\n[Separate excerpt from this same retained response]\n\nTo reduce the over-smoothing effect in CNN models [Tancik et al. 2020](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib57 \"\"), we introduce a novel transformer-based DDPM which adopts the transformer architecture from minGPT [Shafiullah et al. 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib48 \"\") for action prediction.\nActions with noise AtkA\\_{t}^{k} are passed in as input tokens for the transformer decoder blocks, with the sinusoidal embedding for diffusion iteration kk prepended as the first token.\nThe observation 𝐎t\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t} is transformed into observation embedding sequence by a shared MLP, which is then passed into the transformer decoder stack as input features.\nThe “gradient” ϵθ​(𝐎𝐭,𝐀𝐭k,k)\\\\epsilon\\_{\\\\theta}(\\\\mathbf{O\\_{t}},\\\\mathbf{A\\_{t}}^{k},k) is predicted by each corresponding output token of the decoder stack."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "diffuser-backbone",
                  "citationId": "diffuser-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.09991",
                  "supportingPassage": "and (3) the trajectory representation should allow for\nequivariance along one dimension (the planning horizon)\nbut not the other (the state and action features). We\nsatisfy these criteria with a model consisting of repeated\n(temporal) convolutional residual blocks. The overall\narchitecture resembles the types of U-Nets that have\nfound success in image-based diffusion models, but with\ntwo-dimensional spatial convolutions replaced by onedimensional temporal convolutions (FigureA1). Because\nthe model is fully convolutional, the horizon of the\npredictions is determined not by the model architecture,\nbut by the input dimensionality; it can change dynamically\nduring planning if desired.\n\n[Separate excerpt from this same retained response]\n\nTrajectory representation. Diffuser is a model of\ntrajectories designed for planning, meaning that the\neffectiveness of the controller derived from the model is\njust as important as the quality of the state predictions.\nAs a result, states and actions in a trajectory are predicted\njointly; for the purposes of prediction the actions are simply\nadditional dimensions of the state. Specifically, we represent\ninputs (and outputs) of Diffuser as a two-dimensional array:\n\n[Separate excerpt from this same retained response]\n\n1. The architecture of Diffuser (FigureA1) consists of a U-Net structure with 6 repeated residual blocks. Each block consisted of two temporal convolutions, each followed by group norm (Wu & He,2018), and a final Mish nonlinearity (Misra,2019). Timestep embeddings are produced by a single fully-connected layer and added to the activations of the first temporal convolution within each block."
                }
              ],
