            "sourceChecked": "rt1-2022: https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
            "note": "VLA-GENERALIST-CLOSEOUT-vla-models-1. RT-1 separates ImageNet/pretrained language encoders from transformer training. Six images, 81 to eight tokens each, 48 total, eight layers, 19M transformer/35M system, eleven action coordinates and categorical cross-entropy are source-specific. Keep the 130k/700+/17-month/13-robot training population separate from 3000 evaluation trials. Reported 3Hz control and 280ms observed component latency are not universal token-policy limits. Retained-source review 2026-09-08T03:08:27.674Z; zero new retrievals. Rebound to actual9764820. Original retrieval status/time, literal contexts and hashes: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-vla-generalist-closeouts-integration-20260908/source-proof.json. Exact prior/current tuple and plan archive: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-vla-generalist-closeouts-integration-20260908/row-history.json and correction history below. Routine correction authority is not owner source certification or independent acceptance.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "vla-generalist-closeout-vla-models-1-20260908",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "r1-pretrained",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "One of the main contributions of our system is the network architecture, Robotics Transformer 1 (RT-1), an efficient model that can absorb large amounts of data, effectively generalize, and output actions at real-time rates for practical robotic control. RT-1 takes a short sequence of images and a natural language instruction as input and outputs an action for the robot at each time step.\nTo this end, the architecture (shown in Figure [1(a)](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#S1.F1.sf1 \"In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ RT-1: Robotics Transformer for Real-World Control at Scale\")) leverages several elements: first the images and text are processed via an ImageNet pretrained convolutional network ( [Tan & Le, 2019](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib52 \"\")) conditioned on a pretrained embedding of the instruction via FiLM ( [Perez et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib36 \"\")), followed by a Token Learner ( [Ryoo et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib43 \"\")) to compute a compact set of tokens, and finally a Transformer ( [Vaswani et al., 2017](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib54 \"\")) to attend over these tokens and produce discretized action tokens.\nThe actions consist of seven dimensions for the arm movement (x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw, opening of the gripper), three dimensions for base movement (x, y, yaw) and a discrete dimension to switch between three modes: controlling the arm, the base, or terminating the episode.\nRT-1 performs closed-loop control and commands actions at 33 Hz until it either yields a “terminate” action or hits a pre-set time step limit."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-six",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "Instruction and image tokenization. The RT-1 architecture relies on a data-efficient and compact tokenization of images and language instruction.\nRT-1 tokenizes a history of 6 images by passing images through an ImageNet pretrained EfficientNet-B3 ( [Tan & Le, 2019](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib52 \"\")) model, which takes 6 images of resolution 300×300300\\\\times 300 as input and outputs a spatial feature map of shape 9×9×5129\\\\times 9\\\\times 512 from the final convolutional layer.\nUnlike [Reed et al. (2022)](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib42 \"\"), we do not patchify the images into visual tokens prior to feeding them to our Transformer backbone.\nWe instead flatten the output feature map from the EfficientNet into 8181 visual tokens which are passed on to the later layers of the network."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-tokenlearner",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "TokenLearner.\nTo further compress the number of tokens that RT-1 needs to attend over and thus speed up inference, RT-1 uses TokenLearner ( [Ryoo et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib43 \"\")).\nTokenLearner is an element-wise attention module that learns to map a large number of tokens into a much smaller number of tokens. This allows us to soft-select image tokens based on their information, passing only the important token combinations to the subsequent Transformer layers.\nThe inclusion of TokenLearner subsamples the 8181 visual tokens that come out of the pre-trained FiLM-EfficientNet layers to just 88 final tokens that are then passed on to our Transformer layers."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-transformer",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "Transformer.\nThese 8 tokens per-image are then concatenated with the other images in the history, forming 48 total tokens (with added position encoding) to be fed into the Transformer backbone of RT-1.\nThe Transformer is a decoder-only sequence model with 8 self-attention layers and 19M total parameters that outputs action tokens."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-size-rate",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "(a) RT-1 takes images and natural language instructions and outputs discretized base and arm actions. Despite its size (35M parameters), it does this at 3 Hz, due to its efficient yet high-capacity architecture: a FiLM ( [Perez et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib36 \"\")) conditioned EfficientNet ( [Tan & Le, 2019](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib52 \"\")), a TokenLearner ( [Ryoo et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib43 \"\")), and a Transformer ( [Vaswani et al., 2017](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib54 \"\"))."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-coordinates",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "Action tokenization.\nTo tokenize actions, each action dimension in RT-1 is discretized into 256256 bins. As mentioned previously, the action dimensions we consider include seven variables for the arm movement (xx, yy, zz, roll, pitch, yaw, opening of the gripper), three variables for base movement (xx, yy, yaw) and a discrete variable to switch between three modes: controlling arm, base or terminating the episode.\nFor each variable, we map the target to one of the 256 bins, where the bins are uniformly distributed within the bounds of each variable."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-loss",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "Loss.\nWe use a standard categorical cross-entropy entropy objective and causal masking that was utilized in prior Transformer-based controllers ( [Reed et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib42 \"\"); [Lee et al., 2022a](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib27 \"\"))."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-language-encoder",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "To include the language instruction, we condition the image tokenizer on the natural language instruction in the form of a pretrained language embedding, allowing extraction of task-relevant image features early on and improving performance of RT-1.\nThe instruction is first embedded via Universal Sentence Encoder ( [Cer et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib2 \"\")).\nThis embedding is then used as input to identity-initialized FiLM layers ( [Perez et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib36 \"\")) added to the pretrained EfficientNet to condition the image encoder. Normally, inserting a FiLM layer into the interior of a pretrained network would disrupt the intermediate activations and negate the benefit of using pretrained weights. To overcome this, we initialize the weights of the dense layers (fcf\\_{c} and hCh\\_{C}) which produce the FiLM affine transformation to zero, allowing the FiLM layer to initially act as an identity and preserve the function of the pretrained weights. We find that identity-initialized FiLM also produces better results when training with an EfficientNet initialized from scratch, without ImageNet pretraining, but it does not surpass the initialization described above. The architecture of the image tokenizer is presented in Fig. [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#S5.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 5 RT-1: Robotics Transformer ‣ RT-1: Robotics Transformer for Real-World Control at Scale\")."
                }
              ],
