                  "supportingPassage": "(a) RT-1 takes images and natural language instructions and outputs discretized base and arm actions. Despite its size (35M parameters), it does this at 3 Hz, due to its efficient yet high-capacity architecture: a FiLM ( [Perez et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib36 \"\")) conditioned EfficientNet ( [Tan & Le, 2019](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib52 \"\")), a TokenLearner ( [Ryoo et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib43 \"\")), and a Transformer ( [Vaswani et al., 2017](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib54 \"\"))."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r1-language-encoder",
                  "citationId": "rt1-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "To include the language instruction, we condition the image tokenizer on the natural language instruction in the form of a pretrained language embedding, allowing extraction of task-relevant image features early on and improving performance of RT-1.\nThe instruction is first embedded via Universal Sentence Encoder ( [Cer et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib2 \"\")).\nThis embedding is then used as input to identity-initialized FiLM layers ( [Perez et al., 2018](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#bib.bib36 \"\")) added to the pretrained EfficientNet to condition the image encoder. Normally, inserting a FiLM layer into the interior of a pretrained network would disrupt the intermediate activations and negate the benefit of using pretrained weights. To overcome this, we initialize the weights of the dense layers (fcf\\_{c} and hCh\\_{C}) which produce the FiLM affine transformation to zero, allowing the FiLM layer to initially act as an identity and preserve the function of the pretrained weights. We find that identity-initialized FiLM also produces better results when training with an EfficientNet initialized from scratch, without ImageNet pretraining, but it does not surpass the initialization described above. The architecture of the image tokenizer is presented in Fig. [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.06817v2#S5.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 5 RT-1: Robotics Transformer ‣ RT-1: Robotics Transformer for Real-World Control at Scale\")."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "ov-quantiles",
                  "citationId": "openvla-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "To train OpenVLA, we fine-tune a pretrained Prismatic-7B VLM backbone for robot action prediction (see [Fig.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S3.F2 \"In 3 The OpenVLA Model ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")).\nWe formulate the action prediction problem as a “vision-language” task, where an input observation image and a natural language task instruction are mapped to a string of predicted robot actions \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\")\\]. To enable the VLM’s language model backbone to predict robot actions, we represent the actions in the output space of the LLM by mapping continuous robot actions to discrete tokens used by the language model’s tokenizer. Following [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\"), we discretize each dimension of the robot actions separately into one of 256 bins. For each action dimension, we set the bin width to uniformly divide the interval between the 1st and 99th quantile of the actions in the training data. Using quantiles instead of the min-max bounds [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\") used allows us to ignore outlier actions in the data that could otherwise drastically expand the discretization interval and reduce the effective granularity of our action discretization."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "ov-vocabulary",
                  "citationId": "openvla-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "Using this discretization, we obtain N discrete integers ∈\\[0​…​255\\]\\\\in\\[0\\\\dots 255\\]\nfor an NN-dimensional robot action. Unfortunately, the tokenizer used by OpenVLA’s language backbone, the Llama tokenizer \\[ [10](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib10 \"\")\\], only reserves 100 “special tokens” for tokens newly introduced during fine-tuning, which is too few for the 256 tokens of our action discretization. Instead, we again opt for simplicity and follow [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\")’s approach by simply overwriting the 256 _least used_ tokens in the Llama tokenizer’s vocabulary (which corresponds to the last 256 tokens) with our action tokens.\nOnce the actions are processed into a sequence of tokens, OpenVLA is trained with a standard next-token prediction objective, evaluating the cross-entropy loss on the predicted action tokens only. We discuss key design decisions for implementing this training procedure in [Section3.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S3.SS4 \"3.4 OpenVLA Design Decisions ‣ 3 The OpenVLA Model ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"). Next, we describe the robot dataset we use for OpenVLA training."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "r2-vocabulary",
                  "citationId": "rt2-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2307.15818v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "A possible instantiation of such a target could be: “1 128 91 241 5 101 127”. The two VLMs that we finetune in our experiments, PaLI-X ( [Chen et al., 2023a](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.15818v1#bib.bib8 \"\")) and PaLM-E ( [Driess et al., 2023](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.15818v1#bib.bib15 \"\")), use different tokenizations. For PaLI-X, integers up to 1000 each have a unique token, so we simply associate the action bins to the token representing the corresponding integer. For the PaLM-E model, which does not provide this convenient representation of numbers, we simply overwrite the 256 least frequently used tokens to represent the action vocabulary.\nIt is worth noting that training VLMs to override existing tokens with action tokens is a form of symbol tuning ( [Wei et al., 2023](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.15818v1#bib.bib79 \"\")), which has been shown to work well for VLMs in prior work."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "oft-coordinates",
                  "citationId": "openvla-oft-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19645",
                  "supportingPassage": "Original OpenVLA formulation. We use OpenVLA \\[ [23](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19645#bib.bib23 \"\")\\] as our representative base VLA, a 7B-parameter manipulation policy created by fine-tuning the Prismatic VLM \\[ [21](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19645#bib.bib21 \"\")\\] on 1M episodes from the Open X-Embodiment dataset \\[ [34](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19645#bib.bib34 \"\")\\]. See Appendix [A-A](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.19645#A1.SS1 \"A-A Model Architecture Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Fine-Tuning Vision-Language-Action Models: Optimizing Speed and Success\") for architecture details. OpenVLA’s original training formulation uses autoregressive prediction of 7 discrete robot action tokens per timestep: 3 for position control, 3 for orientation control, and 1 for gripper control. It employs next-token prediction with cross-entropy loss as its learning objective, similar to language models. We explore alternative formulations including parallel decoding, continuous action representations, and learning objectives like L1 regression and diffusion modeling in the next few sections."
                }
              ],
