                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "Robot setups and tasks. We test a simple fine-tuning recipe for the OpenVLA model: full fine-tuning of all model parameters, using small datasets with 10–150 demonstrations of a target task (see [Fig.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.F5 \"In 5.2 Data-Efficient Adaptation to New Robot Setups ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"); we explore parameter-efficient fine-tuning approaches in [Section5.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.SS3 \"5.3 Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")).\nWe test OpenVLA in two setups: Franka-Tabletop, a stationary, table-mounted Franka Emika Panda 7-DoF robot arm; and Franka-DROID, the Franka robot arm setup from the recently released DROID dataset \\[ [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib11 \"\")\\], mounted on a movable standing desk. The setups use 5Hz and 15 Hz non-blocking controllers, respectively. We choose Franka robot arms as the target embodiment for our fine-tuning experiments since they are widely used in the robot learning community and thus a likely “target” of OpenVLA fine-tuning. We test on setups with different control frequencies to test OpenVLA’s applicability to a range of use cases."
