s://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Jain,+V), [Vikash Kumar](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kumar,+V), [Vincent Vanhoucke](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Vanhoucke,+V), [Vitor Guizilini](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Guizilini,+V), [Wei Zhan](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhan,+W), [Wenxuan Zhou](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhou,+W), [Wolfram Burgard](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Burgard,+W), [Xi Chen](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen,+X), [Xiangyu Chen](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen,+X), [Xiaolong Wang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wang,+X), [Xinghao Zhu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhu,+X), [Xinyang Geng](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Geng,+X), [Xiyuan Liu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Liu,+X), [Xu Liangwei](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Liangwei,+X), [Xuanlin Li](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Li,+X), [Yansong Pang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Pang,+Y), [Yao Lu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lu,+Y), [Yecheng Jason Ma](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ma,+Y+J), [Yejin Kim](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kim,+Y), [Yevgen Chebotar](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chebotar,+Y), [Yifan Zhou](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhou,+Y), [Yifeng Zhu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhu,+Y), [Yilin Wu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wu,+Y), [Ying Xu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Xu,+Y), [Yixuan Wang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wang,+Y), [Yonatan Bisk](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bisk,+Y), [Yongqiang Dou](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Dou,+Y), [Yoonyoung Cho](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Cho,+Y), [Youngwoon Lee](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lee,+Y), [Yuchen Cui](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Cui,+Y), [Yue Cao](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Cao,+Y), [Yueh-Hua Wu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wu,+Y), [Yujin Tang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Tang,+Y), [Yuke Zhu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhu,+Y), [Yunchu Zhang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhang,+Y), [Yunfan Jiang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Jiang,+Y), [Yunshuang Li](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Li,+Y), [Yunzhu Li](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Li,+Y), [Yusuke Iwasawa](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Iwasawa,+Y), [Yutaka Matsuo](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Matsuo,+Y), [Zehan Ma](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ma,+Z), [Zhuo Xu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Xu,+Z), [Zichen Jeff Cui](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Cui,+Z+J), [Zichen Zhang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhang,+Z), [Zipeng Fu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Fu,+Z), [Zipeng Lin](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lin,+Z)\n\net al. (194 additional authors not shown)\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models, by Open X-Embodiment Collaboration and 293 other authors\n\n[View PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.08864) [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2310.08864v9)\n\n> Abstract:Large, high-capacity models trained on diverse datasets have shown remarkable successes on efficiently tackling downstream applications. In domains from NLP to Computer Vision, this has led to a consolidation of pretrained models, with general pretrained backbones serving as a starting point for many applications. Can such a consolidation happen in robotics? Conventionally, robotic learning methods train a separate model for every application, every robot, and even every environment. Can we instead train generalist X-robot policy that can be adapted efficiently to new robots, tasks, and environments? In this paper, we provide datasets in standardized data formats and models to make it possible to explore this possibility in the context of robotic manipulation, alongside experimental results that provide an example of effective X-robot policies. We assemble a dataset from 22 different robots collected through a collaboration between 21 institutions, demonstrating 527 skills (160266 tasks). We show that a high-capacity model trained on this data, which we call RT-X, exhibits positive transfer and improves the capabilities of multiple robots by leveraging experience from other platforms. More details can be found on the project website [this https URL](https://robotics-transformer-x.github.io/).\n\n|     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n| Comments: | Project website: [this https URL](https://robotics-transformer-x.github.io/) |\n| Subjects: | Robotics (cs.RO) |\n| Cite as: | [arXiv:2310.08864](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08864) \\[cs.RO\\] |\n|  | (or [arXiv:2310.08864v9](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08864v9) \\[cs.RO\\] for this version) |\n|  | [https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.08864](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.08864)<br>Focus to learn more<br>arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite |"
