            "compound": {
              "planId": "cross-closeout-comparison-matrix-3-20260907",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "identity",
                  "citationId": "act-aloha-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13705",
                  "supportingPassage": "# Title:Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\n\nAuthors: [Tony Z. Zhao](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhao,+T+Z), [Vikash Kumar](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kumar,+V), [Sergey Levine](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Levine,+S), [Chelsea Finn](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Finn,+C)\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Tony Zhao \\[ [view email](https://arxiv.org/show-email/55dcc006/2304.13705)\\]\n\n**\\[v1\\]**\nSun, 23 Apr 2023 19:10:53 UTC (4,495 KB)\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "architecture",
                  "citationId": "act-aloha-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705",
                  "supportingPassage": "We implement the CVAE encoder and decoder with transformers, as transformers are designed for both synthesizing information across a sequence and generating new sequences.\nThe CVAE encoder is implemented with a BERT-like transformer encoder \\[ [13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib13 \"\")\\].\nThe inputs to the encoder are the current joint positions and the target action sequence of length kk from the demonstration dataset, prepended by a learned “\\[CLS\\]” token similar to BERT. This forms a k+2k+2 length input (Figure [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F2 \"Fig. 2 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") left).\nAfter passing through the transformer, the feature corresponding to “\\[CLS\\]” is used to predict the mean and variance of the “style variable” zz, which is then used as input to the decoder.\nThe CVAE decoder (i.e. the policy) takes the current observations and zz as the input, and predicts the next kk actions (Figure [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F2 \"Fig. 2 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") right). We use ResNet image encoders, a transformer encoder, and a transformer decoder to implement the CVAE decoder. Intuitively, the transformer encoder synthesizes information from different camera viewpoints, the joint positions, and the style variable, and the transformer decoder generates a coherent action sequence.\nThe observation includes 4 RGB images, each at 480×640480\\\\times 640 resolution, and joint positions for two robot arms (7+7=14 DoF in total).\nThe action space is the absolute joint positions for two robots, a 14-dimensional vector.\nThus with action chunking, the policy outputs a k×14k\\\\times 14 tensor given the current observation.\nThe policy first process the images with ResNet18 backbones \\[ [22](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib22 \"\")\\], which convert 480×640×3480\\\\times 640\\\\times 3 RGB images into 15×20×51215\\\\times 20\\\\times 512 feature maps. We then flatten along the spatial dimension to obtain a sequence of 300×512300\\\\times 512. To preserve the spatial information, we add a 2D sinusoidal position embedding to the feature sequence \\[ [8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib8 \"\")\\].\nRepeating this for all 4 images gives a feature sequence of 1200×5121200\\\\times 512 in dimension.\nWe then append two more features: the current joint positions and the “style variable” zz. They are projected from their original dimensions to 512512 through linear layers respectively.\nThus, the input to the transformer encoder is 1202×5121202\\\\times 512.\nThe transformer decoder conditions on the encoder output through cross-attention,\nwhere the input sequence is a fixed position embedding, with dimensions k×512k\\\\times 512, and the keys and values are coming from the encoder.\nThis gives the transformer decoder an output dimension of k×512k\\\\times 512, which is then down-projected with an MLP into k×14k\\\\times 14, corresponding to the predicted target joint positions for the next kk steps.\nWe use L1 loss for reconstruction instead of the more common L2 loss: we noted that L1 loss leads to more precise modeling of the action sequence.\nWe also noted degraded performance when using delta joint positions as actions instead of target joint positions.\nWe include a detailed architecture diagram in Appendix [-C](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#A0.SS3 \"-C Detailed Architecture Diagram ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\").\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nWe summarize the training and inference of ACT in Algorithms [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg1 \"Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") and [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg2 \"Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"). The model has around 80M parameters, and we train from scratch for each task. The training takes around 5 hours on a single 11G RTX 2080 Ti GPU, and the inference time is around 0.01 seconds on the same machine."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "reference-horizon-rate",
                  "citationId": "act-aloha-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705",
                  "supportingPassage": "In Subsection [V-C](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S5.SS3 \"V-C Experiment Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"),\nwe observed that ACT significantly outperforms previous methods that only predict single-step actions, with the hypothesis that action chunking is the key design choice.\nSince kk dictates how long the sequence in each “chunk” is, we can analyze this hypothesis by varying kk. k=1k=1 corresponds to no action chunking, and k=e​p​i​s​o​d​e​\\_​l​e​n​g​t​hk=episode\\\\\\_length corresponds to fully open-loop control, where the robot outputs the entire episode’s action sequence based on the first observation. We disable temporal ensembling in these experiments to only measure the effect of chunking, and trained separate policies for each kk.\nIn Figure [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S5.F6 \"Fig. 6 ‣ V-C Experiment Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") (a), we plot the success rate averaged across 4 settings, corresponding to 2 simulated tasks with either human or scripted data, with the blue line representing ACT without the temporal ensemble.\nWe observe that performance improves drastically from 1% at k=1k=1 to 44% at k=100k=100, then slightly tapers down with higher kk.\nThis illustrates that more chunking and a lower effective horizon generally improve performance. We attribute the slight dip at k=200,400k=200,400 (i.e., close to open-loop control) to the lack of reactive behavior and the difficulty in modeling long action sequences.\nTo further evaluate the effectiveness and generality of action chunking, we augment two baseline methods with action chunking. For BC-ConvMLP, we simply increase the output dimension to k∗a​c​t​i​o​n​\\_​d​i​mk\\*action\\\\\\_dim, and for VINN, we retrieve the next kk actions.\nWe visualize their performance in Figure [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S5.F6 \"Fig. 6 ‣ V-C Experiment Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") (a) with different kk, showing trends consistent with ACT, where more action chunking improves performance.\nWhile ACT still outperforms both augmented baselines with sizable gains, these results suggest that action chunking is generally beneficial for imitation learning in these settings.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nA naïve implementation of action chunking can be sub-optimal: a new environment observation is incorporated abruptly every kk steps and can result in jerky robot motion.\nTo improve smoothness and avoid discrete switching between executing and observing, we query the policy at every timestep.\nThis makes different action chunks overlap with each other, and at a given timestep there will be more than one predicted action. We illustrate this in Figure [3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S3.F3 \"Fig. 3 ‣ III ALOHA: A Low-cost Open-source Hardware System for Bimanual Teleoperation ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") and propose a temporal ensemble to combine these predictions.\nOur temporal ensemble performs a weighted average over these predictions with an exponential weighting scheme wi=exp(−m∗i)w\\_{i}=\\\\exp(-m\\*i), where w0w\\_{0} is the weight for the oldest action.\nThe speed for incorporating new observation is governed by mm, where a smaller mm means faster incorporation.\nWe note that unlike typical smoothing, where the current action is aggregated with actions in adjacent timesteps, which leads to bias, we aggregate actions predicted for the same timestep.\nThis procedure also incurs no additional training cost, only extra inference-time computation.\nIn practice, we find both action chunking and temporal ensembling to be important for the success of ACT, which produces precise and smooth motion. We discuss these components in more detail in the ablation studies in Subsection [VI-A](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S6.SS1 \"VI-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensembling ‣ VI Ablations ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\").\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nThe rest of the setup includes a robot cage with 20×\\\\times20mm aluminum extrusions, reinforced by crossing steel cables. There is a total of four Logitech C922x webcams, each streaming 480×\\\\times640 RGB images. Two of the webcams are mounted on the wrist of the follower robots, allowing for a close-up view of the grippers. The remaining two cameras are mounted on the front and at the top respectively (Fig [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#S2.F1 \"Fig. 1 ‣ II Related Work ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\")).\nBoth the teleoperation and data recording happen at 50Hz."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "release-and-transfer-scope",
                  "citationId": "act-aloha-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705",
                  "supportingPassage": "We summarize the training and inference of ACT in Algorithms [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg1 \"Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\") and [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#alg2 \"Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-A Action Chunking and Temporal Ensemble ‣ IV Action Chunking with Transformers ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"). The model has around 80M parameters, and we train from scratch for each task. The training takes around 5 hours on a single 11G RTX 2080 Ti GPU, and the inference time is around 0.01 seconds on the same machine.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nSkills such as threading a zip tie, inserting RAM, and juggling ping pong ball, to our knowledge, are not available for existing teleoperation systems with 5-10x the budget \\[ [21](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib21 \"\"), [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#bib.bib5 \"\")\\]. We include a more detailed price & capability comparison in Appendix [-A](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#A0.SS1 \"-A Comparing ALOHA with Prior Teleoperation Setups ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\"), as well as more skills that ALOHA is capable of in Figure [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13705#A0.F7 \"Fig. 7 ‣ -A Comparing ALOHA with Prior Teleoperation Setups ‣ Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware\").\nTo make ALOHA more accessible, we open-source all software and hardware with a detailed tutorial covering 3D printing, assembling the frame to software installations. You can find the tutorial on the [project website](https://tonyzhaozh.github.io/aloha/ \"\")."
                }
