                  "partId": "architecture",
                  "citationId": "diffusion-policy-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 3 Key Design Decisions\n\nIn this section, we describe key design decisions for Diffusion Policy as well as its concrete implementation of ϵθ\\\\epsilon\\_{\\\\theta} with neural network architectures.\n\n### 3.1 Network Architecture Options\n\nThe first design decision is the choice of neural network architectures for ϵθ\\\\epsilon\\_{\\\\theta}.\nIn this work, we examine two common network architecture types, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) [Ronneberger et al. 2015](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib46 \"\") and Transformers [Vaswani et al. 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib60 \"\"), and compare their performance and training characteristics. Note that the choice of noise prediction network ϵθ\\\\epsilon\\_{\\\\theta} is independent of visual encoders, which will be described in Sec. [3.2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#S3.SS2 \"3.2 Visual Encoder ‣ 3 Key Design Decisions ‣ Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion\").\n\nCNN-based Diffusion Policy\nWe adopt the 1D temporal CNN from [Janner et al. 2022b](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib24 \"\") with a few modifications:\nFirst, we only model the conditional distribution p⁡(𝐀t\\|𝐎t)p(\\\\mathbf{A}\\_{t}\\|\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t}) by conditioning the action generation process on observation features 𝐎t\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t} with Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) [Perez et al. 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib39 \"\") as well as denoising iteration kk, shown in Fig [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#S1.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion\") (b).\nSecond, we only predict the action trajectory instead of the concatenated observation action trajectory.\nThird, we removed inpainting-based goal state conditioning due to incompatibility with our framework utilizing a receding prediction horizon. However, goal conditioning is still possible with the same FiLM conditioning method used for observations.\n\nIn practice, we found the CNN-based backbone to work well on most tasks out of the box without the need for much hyperparameter tuning. However, it performs poorly when the desired action sequence changes quickly and sharply through time (such as velocity command action space), likely due to the inductive bias of temporal convolutions to prefer low-frequency signals [Tancik et al. 2020](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib57 \"\").\n\nTime-series diffusion transformer\nTo reduce the over-smoothing effect in CNN models [Tancik et al. 2020](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib57 \"\"), we introduce a novel transformer-based DDPM which adopts the transformer architecture from minGPT [Shafiullah et al. 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib48 \"\") for action prediction.\nActions with noise AtkA\\_{t}^{k} are passed in as input tokens for the transformer decoder blocks, with the sinusoidal embedding for diffusion iteration kk prepended as the first token.\nThe observation 𝐎t\\\\mathbf{O}\\_{t} is transformed into observation embedding sequence by a shared MLP, which is then passed into the transformer decoder stack as input features.\nThe “gradient” ϵθ​(𝐎𝐭,𝐀𝐭k,k)\\\\epsilon\\_{\\\\theta}(\\\\mathbf{O\\_{t}},\\\\mathbf{A\\_{t}}^{k},k) is predicted by each corresponding output token of the decoder stack.\n\nIn our state-based experiments, most of the best-performing policies are achieved with the transformer backbone, especially when the task complexity and rate of action change are high. However, we found the transformer to be more sensitive to hyperparameters. The difficulty of transformer training [Liu et al. 2020](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib29 \"\") is not unique to Diffusion Policy and could potentially be resolved in the future with improved transformer training techniques or increased data scale.\n\nRecommendations.\nIn general, we recommend starting with the CNN-based diffusion policy implementation as the first attempt at a new task. If performance is low due to task complexity or high-rate action changes, then the Time-series Diffusion Transformer formulation can be used to potentially improve performance at the cost of additional tuning.\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nThe visual encoder maps the raw image sequence into a latent embedding OtO\\_{t} and is trained end-to-end with the diffusion policy.\nDifferent camera views use separate encoders, and images in each timestep are encoded independently and then concatenated to form OtO\\_{t}.\nWe used a standard ResNet-18 (without pretraining) as the encoder with the following modifications:\n1) Replace the global average pooling with a spatial softmax pooling to maintain spatial information [Mandlekar et al. 2021](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib33 \"\").\n2) Replace BatchNorm with GroupNorm [Wu and He 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib65 \"\") for stable training. This is important when the normalization layer is used in conjunction with Exponential Moving Average [He et al. 2020](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2303.04137#bib.bib19 \"\") (commonly used in DDPMs)."
