                  "supportingPassage": "# Title:OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\n\nAuthors: [Moo Jin Kim](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kim,+M+J), [Karl Pertsch](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Pertsch,+K), [Siddharth Karamcheti](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Karamcheti,+S), [Ted Xiao](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Xiao,+T), [Ashwin Balakrishna](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Balakrishna,+A), [Suraj Nair](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Nair,+S), [Rafael Rafailov](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Rafailov,+R), [Ethan Foster](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Foster,+E), [Grace Lam](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lam,+G), [Pannag Sanketi](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Sanketi,+P), [Quan Vuong](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Vuong,+Q), [Thomas Kollar](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kollar,+T), [Benjamin Burchfiel](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Burchfiel,+B), [Russ Tedrake](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Tedrake,+R), [Dorsa Sadigh](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Sadigh,+D), [Sergey Levine](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Levine,+S), [Percy Liang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Liang,+P), [Chelsea Finn](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Finn,+C)\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Moo Jin Kim \\[ [view email](https://arxiv.org/show-email/82761e0a/2406.09246)\\]\n\n**[\\[v1\\]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246v1)**\nThu, 13 Jun 2024 15:46:55 UTC (11,299 KB)\n\n**[\\[v2\\]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246v2)**\nWed, 4 Sep 2024 02:14:57 UTC (11,361 KB)\n\n**\\[v3\\]**\nThu, 5 Sep 2024 19:46:34 UTC (11,362 KB)\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "architecture-action",
                  "citationId": "openvla-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 3.1 Preliminaries: Vision-Language Models\n\nThe architecture of most recent VLMs \\[ [42](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib42 \"\"), [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib43 \"\"), [20](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib20 \"\"), [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib44 \"\")\\] consists of three main parts (see [Fig.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S3.F2 \"In 3 The OpenVLA Model ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")): (1) a visual encoder that maps image inputs to a number of “image patch embeddings”, (2) a projector that takes the output embeddings of the visual encoder and maps them into the input space of a language model, and (3) a large language model (LLM) backbone. During VLM training, the model is trained end-to-end with a next text token prediction objective on paired or interleaved vision and language data curated from various Internet sources.\n\nIn this work, we build on the Prismatic-7B VLM \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib44 \"\")\\]. Prismatic follows the same standard architecture described above, with a 600M-parameter visual encoder, a small 2-layer MLP projector, and a 7B-parameter Llama 2 language model backbone\\[ [10](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib10 \"\")\\]. Notably, Prismatic uses a _two-part_ visual encoder, consisting of pretrained SigLIP \\[ [79](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib79 \"\")\\] and DinoV2 \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib25 \"\")\\] models. Input image patches are passed separately through both encoders and the resulting feature vectors are concatenated channel-wise. In contrast to the more commonly used vision encoders such as CLIP- \\[ [80](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib80 \"\")\\] or SigLIP-only encoders, the addition of DinoV2 features has been shown to be helpful for improved spatial reasoning \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib44 \"\")\\], which can be particularly helpful for robot control.\n\nSigLIP, DinoV2, and Llama 2 do not release details about their training data, which likely consists of trillions of tokens of Internet-sourced image-text, image-only, and text-only data respectively. The Prismatic VLM is fine-tuned on top of these components using the LLaVA 1.5 data mixture \\[ [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib43 \"\")\\], which contains a total of approximately 1M image-text and text-only data samples from open-source datasets \\[ [81](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib81 \"\"), [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib82 \"\"), [29](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib29 \"\"), [83](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib83 \"\"), [42](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib42 \"\")\\].\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n### 3.2 OpenVLA Training Procedure\n\nTo train OpenVLA, we fine-tune a pretrained Prismatic-7B VLM backbone for robot action prediction (see [Fig.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S3.F2 \"In 3 The OpenVLA Model ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")).\nWe formulate the action prediction problem as a “vision-language” task, where an input observation image and a natural language task instruction are mapped to a string of predicted robot actions \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\")\\]. To enable the VLM’s language model backbone to predict robot actions, we represent the actions in the output space of the LLM by mapping continuous robot actions to discrete tokens used by the language model’s tokenizer. Following [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\"), we discretize each dimension of the robot actions separately into one of 256 bins. For each action dimension, we set the bin width to uniformly divide the interval between the 1st and 99th quantile of the actions in the training data. Using quantiles instead of the min-max bounds [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\") used allows us to ignore outlier actions in the data that could otherwise drastically expand the discretization interval and reduce the effective granularity of our action discretization.\n\nUsing this discretization, we obtain N discrete integers ∈\\[0​…​255\\]\\\\in\\[0\\\\dots 255\\]\nfor an NN-dimensional robot action. Unfortunately, the tokenizer used by OpenVLA’s language backbone, the Llama tokenizer \\[ [10](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib10 \"\")\\], only reserves 100 “special tokens” for tokens newly introduced during fine-tuning, which is too few for the 256 tokens of our action discretization. Instead, we again opt for simplicity and follow [Brohan et al. \\[7\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib7 \"\")’s approach by simply overwriting the 256 _least used_ tokens in the Llama tokenizer’s vocabulary (which corresponds to the last 256 tokens) with our action tokens.\nOnce the actions are processed into a sequence of tokens, OpenVLA is trained with a standard next-token prediction objective, evaluating the cross-entropy loss on the predicted action tokens only. We discuss key design decisions for implementing this training procedure in [Section3.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S3.SS4 \"3.4 OpenVLA Design Decisions ‣ 3 The OpenVLA Model ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"). Next, we describe the robot dataset we use for OpenVLA training.\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nWe filter out all “no-op” actions from the dataset, i.e., actions that have near-zero magnitude in the translation and rotation components and do not change the state of the robot’s gripper. We find that this simple data cleaning step is crucial for highly expressive single-step policies such as OpenVLA, which otherwise learn to imitate these no-op actions and consequently freeze indefinitely at certain states during evaluation."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "data-transfer-release",
                  "citationId": "openvla-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "Figure 1: We present OpenVLA, a 7B-parameter open-source vision-language-action model (VLA), trained\non 970k robot episodes from the Open X-Embodiment dataset \\[ [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib1 \"\")\\]. OpenVLA sets a new state of the art for generalist robot manipulation policies. It supports controlling multiple robots out of the box and can be quickly adapted to new robot domains via parameter-efficient fine-tuning. The OpenVLA checkpoints and PyTorch training pipeline are fully open-source and models can be downloaded and fine-tuned from HuggingFace.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n### 3.3 Training Data\n\nThe goal in constructing the OpenVLA training dataset is to capture a large diversity of robot embodiments, scenes, and tasks. This enables the final model to control various robots out of the box _and_ admits efficient fine-tuning to new robot setups. We leverage the Open X-Embodiment dataset \\[ [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib1 \"\")\\] (OpenX) as a base to curate our training dataset. The full OpenX dataset, at the time of writing, consists of more than 70 individual robot datasets, with more than 2M robot trajectories, that were pooled into a coherent and easy-to-use data format in a large community effort. To make training on this data practical, we apply multiple steps of data curation to the raw dataset.\n\nThe goals of this curation are to ensure (1) a coherent input and output space across all training datasets, and (2) a balanced mix of embodiments, tasks, and scenes in the final training mixture.222\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOcto \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] demonstrated training across datasets with heterogeneous sensory inputs. While very promising, we leave an investigation of VLA training across heterogeneous sensor modalities and action spaces to future work. To address (1), we follow \\[ [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib1 \"\"), [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] and restrict our training dataset to contain only manipulation datasets with at least one 3rd person camera and use single-arm end-effector control. For (2), we leverage the data mixture weights of Octo \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] for all datasets that pass the first round of filtering. Octo heuristically down-weights or removes less diverse datasets and up-weights datasets with larger task and scene diversity; see [Octo Model Team et al. \\[5\\]](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib5 \"\") for details.\n\nWe also experimented with incorporating a few additional datasets into our training mixture that were added to the OpenX dataset since the release of Octo, including the DROID dataset \\[ [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib11 \"\")\\], although at a conservative mixture weight of 10%. In practice, we found that the action token accuracy on DROID remained low throughout training, suggesting a larger mixture weight or model\nmay be required to fit its diversity in the future. To not jeopardize the quality of the final model, we removed DROID from the data mixture for the final third of training. We provide a complete overview of the used datasets and mixture weights in [AppendixA](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#A1 \"Appendix A Data Mixture Details ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\").\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "rate-settings",
                  "citationId": "openvla-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 3.5 Infrastructure for Training and Inference\n\nThe final OpenVLA model is trained on a cluster of 64 A100 GPUs for 14 days, or a total of 21,500 A100-hours, using a batch size of 2048. During inference, OpenVLA requires 15GB of GPU memory when loaded in bfloat16 precision (i.e., without quantization) and runs at approximately 6Hz on one NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU (without compilation, speculative decoding, or other inference speed-up tricks). We can further reduce the memory footprint of OpenVLA during inference via quantization, without compromising performance in real-world robotics tasks, as shown in [Section5.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.SS4 \"5.4 Memory-Efficient Inference via Quantization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"). We report inference speed on various consumer- and server-grade GPUs in [Fig.6](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.F6 \"In 5.4 Memory-Efficient Inference via Quantization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"). For convenience, we implement a remote VLA inference server to allow real-time remote streaming of action predictions to the robot – removing the requirement of having access to a powerful local compute device to control the robot. We release this remote inference solution as part of our open-source code release ( [Section4](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S4 \"4 The OpenVLA Codebase ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")).\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nRobot setups and tasks. We test a simple fine-tuning recipe for the OpenVLA model: full fine-tuning of all model parameters, using small datasets with 10–150 demonstrations of a target task (see [Fig.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.F5 \"In 5.2 Data-Efficient Adaptation to New Robot Setups ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\"); we explore parameter-efficient fine-tuning approaches in [Section5.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#S5.SS3 \"5.3 Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\")).\nWe test OpenVLA in two setups: Franka-Tabletop, a stationary, table-mounted Franka Emika Panda 7-DoF robot arm; and Franka-DROID, the Franka robot arm setup from the recently released DROID dataset \\[ [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09246v3#bib.bib11 \"\")\\], mounted on a movable standing desk. The setups use 5Hz and 15 Hz non-blocking controllers, respectively. We choose Franka robot arms as the target embodiment for our fine-tuning experiments since they are widely used in the robot learning community and thus a likely “target” of OpenVLA fine-tuning. We test on setups with different control frequencies to test OpenVLA’s applicability to a range of use cases."
                }
