                  "supportingPassage": "Problem Statement . Our system receives a user-provided natural language instruction i i that describes a task that the robot should execute. The instruction can be long, abstract, or ambiguous. We also assume that we are given a set of skills Π \\Pi , where each skill π ∈ Π \\pi\\in\\Pi performs a short task, such as picking up a particular object, and comes with a short language description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} (e.g., “find a sponge”) and an affordance function p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , which indicates the probability of c c -ompleting the skill with description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} successfully from state s s . Intuitively, p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) means “if I ask the robot to do ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} , will it do it?”. In RL terminology, p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) is the value function for the skill if we take the reward to be 1 1 for successful completion and 0 0 otherwise.\n\nAs mentioned above, ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} denotes the textual label of skill π \\pi and p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) denotes the probability that skill π \\pi with textual label ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} successfully completes if executed from state s s , where c π c_{\\pi} is a Bernoulli random variable. The LLM provides us with p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) , the probability that a skill’s textual label is a valid next step for the user’s instruction. However, what we are interested in is the probability that a given skill successfully makes progress toward actually completing the instruction, which we denote as p ⁡ ( c i | i , s , ℓ π ) p(c_{i}|i,s,\\ell_{\\pi}) . Assuming that a skill that succeeds makes progress on i i with probability p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) (i.e., its probability of being the right skill), and a skill that fails makes progress with probability zero, we can factorize this as p ⁡ ( c i | i , s , ℓ π ) ∝ p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) ​ p ​ ( ℓ π | i ) p(c_{i}|i,s,\\ell_{\\pi})\\propto p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi})p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) . This corresponds to multiplying the probability of the language description of the skill given the instruction p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) , which we refer to as task-grounding, and the probability of the skill being possible in the current state of the world p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , which we refer to as world-grounding."
