                  "supportingPassage": "Problem Statement . Our system receives a user-provided natural language instruction i i that describes a task that the robot should execute. The instruction can be long, abstract, or ambiguous. We also assume that we are given a set of skills Π \\Pi , where each skill π ∈ Π \\pi\\in\\Pi performs a short task, such as picking up a particular object, and comes with a short language description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} (e.g., “find a sponge”) and an affordance function p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , which indicates the probability of c c -ompleting the skill with description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} successfully from state s s . Intuitively, p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) means “if I ask the robot to do ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} , will it do it?”. In RL terminology, p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) is the value function for the skill if we take the reward to be 1 1 for successful completion and 0 0 otherwise.\n\nRecall that we wish to find the affordance function p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , which indicates the probability of c c -ompleting the skill with description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} successfully from state s s . Our learned policies produce a Q-function, Q π ​ ( s , a ) Q^{\\pi}(s,a) . Given Q π ​ ( s , a ) Q^{\\pi}(s,a) with action a a and state s s , value v ⁡ ( s ) = max a ⁡ Q π ​ ( s , a ) v(s)=\\max_{a}Q^{\\pi}(s,a) is found through optimization via the cross entropy method, similar to MT-Opt. For brevity below we refer to the value functions by their skill-text description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} as v ℓ π v^{\\ell_{\\pi}} and the affordance function as p ℓ π affordance p^{\\mathrm{affordance}}_{\\ell_{\\pi}} . Due to artifacts of training and each implementation, the value functions require calibration to be directly applied as a probability. The parameters used for calibration are determined empirically. Furthermore, SayCan enforces logic that if a skill that has already been completed and the reward received (e.g., navigating to the table the robot is already in front of) then it should not be performed.\n\np pick affordance = clamp ⁡ ( v pick − v min pick v max pick − v min pick , 0 , 1 ) , where ​ v max pick = 0.5 , v min pick = 0.2 . p_{\\mathrm{pick}}^{\\mathrm{affordance}}=\\mathrm{clamp}(\\frac{v^{\\mathrm{pick}}-v^{\\mathrm{pick}}_{\\mathrm{min}}}{v^{\\mathrm{pick}}_{\\mathrm{max}}-v^{\\mathrm{pick}}_{\\mathrm{min}}},0,1),\\mathrm{where}\\;v^{\\mathrm{pick}}_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.5,v^{\\mathrm{pick}}_{\\mathrm{min}}=0.2."
