                  "supportingPassage": "As mentioned above, ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} denotes the textual label of skill π \\pi and p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) denotes the probability that skill π \\pi with textual label ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} successfully completes if executed from state s s , where c π c_{\\pi} is a Bernoulli random variable. The LLM provides us with p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) , the probability that a skill’s textual label is a valid next step for the user’s instruction. However, what we are interested in is the probability that a given skill successfully makes progress toward actually completing the instruction, which we denote as p ⁡ ( c i | i , s , ℓ π ) p(c_{i}|i,s,\\ell_{\\pi}) . Assuming that a skill that succeeds makes progress on i i with probability p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) (i.e., its probability of being the right skill), and a skill that fails makes progress with probability zero, we can factorize this as p ⁡ ( c i | i , s , ℓ π ) ∝ p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) ​ p ​ ( ℓ π | i ) p(c_{i}|i,s,\\ell_{\\pi})\\propto p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi})p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) . This corresponds to multiplying the probability of the language description of the skill given the instruction p ⁡ ( ℓ π | i ) p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) , which we refer to as task-grounding, and the probability of the skill being possible in the current state of the world p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , which we refer to as world-grounding.\n\nSayCan. The key idea of SayCan is to ground large language models through value functions – affordance functions that capture the log likelihood that a particular skill will be able to succeed in the current state. Given a skill π ∈ Π \\pi\\in\\Pi , its language description ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} and its corresponding value function, which provides p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi}) , the probability of c c -ompletion for the skill described by ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} in state s s , we form an affordance space { p ⁡ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) } π ∈ Π \\{p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi})\\}_{\\pi\\in\\Pi} . This value function space captures affordances across all skills [ 12 ] (see Figure 2 ). For each skill, the affordance function and the LLM probability are then multiplied together and ultimately the most probable skill is selected, i.e. π = arg ​ max π ∈ Π ⁡ p ​ ( c π | s , ℓ π ) ​ p ​ ( ℓ π | i ) \\pi=\\argmax_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}p(c_{\\pi}|s,\\ell_{\\pi})p(\\ell_{\\pi}|i) . Once the skill is selected, the corresponding policy is executed by the agent and the LLM query is amended to include ℓ π \\ell_{\\pi} and the process is run again until a termination token (e.g., “done”) is chosen. This process is shown in Figure 5 and described in Algorithm 1 . These two mirrored processes together lead to a probabilistic interpretation of SayCan, where the LLM provides probabilities of a skill being useful for the high-level instruction and the affordances provide probabilities of successfully executing each skill. Combining these two probabilities together provides a probability that this skill furthers the execution of the high-level instruction commanded by the user."
