                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Our method also uses a two-stage inference procedure, where we first infer a high-level semantic subtask (e.g., “pick up the plate”), and then predict the action based on this subtask. Many prior methods have employed two separate models for this purpose, with a VLM predicting semantic steps and a separate low-level policy executing those steps \\[ [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib2 \"\"), [71](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib71 \"\"), [13](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib13 \"\"), [24](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib24 \"\"), [70](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib70 \"\"), [72](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib72 \"\"), [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib47 \"\")\\]. Our method uses the same exact model for both high-level and low-level inference, in a recipe that more closely resembles chain-of-thought \\[ [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib82 \"\")\\] or test-time compute \\[ [39](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib39 \"\")\\] methods, though unlike embodied chain-of-thought methods \\[ [88](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib88 \"\"), [46](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib46 \"\"), [61](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib61 \"\")\\], the high-level inference process still runs at a lower frequency than low-level action inference."
