            "supportingPassage": "Excerpt 1 (Section IV-C, High-Level subtask prediction):<br>_H_ igh- _L_ evel subtask prediction (HL). Breaking down high-level task commands such as “clean the bedroom” into shorter subtasks like “adjust the blanket” and “pick up pillow”, similar to chain-of-thought prompting for language models, can help a trained policy reason about the current scene and better determine the next action. For robot data in MM, ME, and CE where the task involves multiple subtasks, we manually annotate all data with semantic descriptions of the subtasks and train π0.5\\\\pi\\_{0.5} to jointly predict the subtask labels (as text) as well as the actions (conditioned on the subtask label) based on the current observation and high-level command. This naturally leads to a model that can act both as a high-level policy (outputting subtasks) and low-level policy that executes actions for these subtasks. We also label relevant bounding boxes shown in the current observation and train π0.5\\\\pi\\_{0.5} to predict them before predicting the subtask.<br><br>Excerpt 2 (Section IV-C, Multi-modal Web Data):<br>Multi-modal _W_ eb _D_ ata (WD). Finally we include a diverse set of web data involving image captioning (CapsFusion \\[ [87](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib87 \"\")\\], COCO \\[ [12](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib12 \"\")\\]),<br>question answering (Cambrian-7M \\[ [77](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib77 \"\")\\], PixMo \\[ [19](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib19 \"\")\\], VQAv2 \\[ [32](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib32 \"\")\\]), and object localization in pre-training. For object localization, we further extend the standard datasets with additional web data of indoor scenes and household objects with bounding box annotations.",
