                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "The control system is very simple: the π0.5\\\\pi\\_{0.5} model directly commands target poses for the arms, gripper, and torso lift, and the target base velocities at 50 Hz (with action chunking). These targets are tracked with simple PD controllers, without any additional trajectory planning or collision detection. All manipulation and navigation control is fully end-to-end.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nThe action expert is a smaller transformer that takes in a sequence of noisy action tokens 𝐚t:t+Hτ,ω\\\\mathbf{a}\\_{t:t+H}^{\\\\tau,\\\\omega} for an action horizon of 50, i.e. H=49H=49, and is trained with the flow matching objective. The noisy action chunk (with action dimension dd) is first projected to the transformer embedding dimension using a single linear layer. Unlike π0\\\\pi\\_{0} that fuses the flow-matching timestep τ\\\\tau with the noisy action before being fed into the transformer, π0.5\\\\pi\\_{0.5} uses a separate MLP for projecting τ\\\\tau only and then applies adaptive RMSNorm to inject the timestep information to each layer of the action expert.\nThe timestep MLP takes in the form of swish⁡(W2⋅swish⁡(W1⋅ϕ⁡(τ)))\\\\mathrm{swish}(W\\_{2}\\\\cdot\\\\mathrm{swish}(W\\_{1}\\\\cdot\\\\phi(\\\\tau))), where ϕ:ℝ→ℝw\\\\phi:\\\\mathbb{R}\\\\to\\\\mathbb{R}^{w} is a sinusoidal positional encoding function \\[ [79](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib79 \"\")\\] and W1,W2∈ℝw×wW\\_{1},W\\_{2}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{w\\\\times w}. The action expert outputs action tokens ya1:Hy^{a}\\_{1:H}, which are then decoded into the target vector field using a final linear projection.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nRobot reasoning and planning with language.\nA number of prior works have shown that augmenting end-to-end policies with high-level reasoning can significantly improve performance for long-horizon tasks \\[ [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib2 \"\"), [36](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib36 \"\"), [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib44 \"\"), [74](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib74 \"\"), [71](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib71 \"\"), [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib4 \"\"), [16](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib16 \"\"), [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib11 \"\"), [53](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib53 \"\"), [88](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib88 \"\"), [51](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib51 \"\"), [59](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib59 \"\"), [13](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib13 \"\"), [70](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib70 \"\"), [91](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib91 \"\"), [65](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib65 \"\"), [72](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib72 \"\"), [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib47 \"\"), [76](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib76 \"\"), [89](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib89 \"\")\\], particularly when high-level subtask inference can benefit from large pre-trained LLMs and VLMs. Our method also uses a two-stage inference procedure, where we first infer a high-level semantic subtask (e.g., “pick up the plate”), and then predict the action based on this subtask. Many prior methods have employed two separate models for this purpose, with a VLM predicting semantic steps and a separate low-level policy executing those steps \\[ [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib2 \"\"), [71](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib71 \"\"), [13](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib13 \"\"), [24](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib24 \"\"), [70](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib70 \"\"), [72](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib72 \"\"), [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib47 \"\")\\]. Our method uses the same exact model for both high-level and low-level inference, in a recipe that more closely resembles chain-of-thought \\[ [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib82 \"\")\\] or test-time compute \\[ [39](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib39 \"\")\\] methods, though unlike embodied chain-of-thought methods \\[ [88](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib88 \"\"), [46](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib46 \"\"), [61](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib61 \"\")\\], the high-level inference process still runs at a lower frequency than low-level action inference.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1/Figure_3.png)Fig. 3: Model overview.π0.5\\\\pi\\_{0.5} is trained in two stages. First, a pre-training stage combines all of the different data sources to produce an initial VLA with discrete tokens. This stage uses data from diverse robotic platforms, high-level semantic action prediction, and data from the web. Robotic data uses the FAST action tokenizer to represent actions as discrete tokens \\[ [64](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.16054v1#bib.bib64 \"\")\\]. Second, a post-training stage specializes the model for low-level and high-level inferences for mobile manipulation, leveraging the most task-relevant data, including verbal instructions from human supervisors. This stage uses flow matching to represent the action distribution, enabling efficient real-time inference and the ability to represent fine-grained continuous action sequences. At inference time, the model first infers a high-level subtask, and then predicts the actions based on this subtask."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "helix-stage-specific-rates",
                  "citationId": "helix-02-2026",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://www.figure.ai/news/helix-02",
                  "supportingPassage": "Each system operates at its natural timescale. **System 2 (S2)** reasons slowly about goals: interpreting scenes, understanding language, and sequencing behaviors. **System 1 (S1)** thinks fast, translating perception into full‑body joint targets at 200 Hz. **System 0 (S0)** executes at 1 kHz, handling balance, contact, and coordination across the entire body. Together, they form a tightly integrated hierarchy from pixels to torque.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n**Architecture:** A 10M‑parameter neural network that takes full‑body joint state and base motion as input and outputs joint‑level actuator commands at 1 kHz.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nSystem 2 remains the semantic reasoning layer: processing scenes, understanding language, and producing latent goals for S1. Helix 02 dramatically expands the _scope_ of behaviors S2 can specify. Previously: _“Pick up the ketchup.”_ Now:\n\n- \"Walk to the dishwasher and open it\"\n\n- \"Carry the bowls to the counter\"\n\n- \"Go back to the top rack and pick up the cups\"\n\n\nS2 doesn't need to plan low-level footsteps or specify how to coordinate arms and legs. It produces a sequence of semantic latents that S1 interprets into motor commands, which S0 executes."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "go2-module-versus-drawing-lanes",
                  "citationId": "agibot-go2-2026",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://www.agibot.com/article/231/detail/56.html",
                  "supportingPassage": "● Semantic Planning Module (System 2): Operates at a lower frequency. Acting as a \"General Commander,\" it generates structured high-level action sequences. These are presented through Progressive Refinement, ensuring that the reasoning itself is inherently \"executable,\" providing stable geometric anchors for control.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n● Action Following Module (System 1): Operates at a higher frequency. Acting as an \"Agile Executor,\" it continuously receives high-level intents and combines them with real-time observations to generate specific control signals, performing Residual Refinement to compensate for environmental noise.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nCrucially, these two systems are deeply aligned. To ensure execution strictly adheres to reasoning, GO-2 utilizes a Teacher Forcing mechanism during training, teaching the model to perform robustly even under \"approximately correct but imperfect\" reasoning conditions."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "gemini-roles-not-measured-schedule",
                  "citationId": "gemini-robotics-15-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342",
                  "supportingPassage": "In this section, we focus on the Thinking VLA model (GR 1.5 with thinking mode ON during inference). A detailed analysis of the higher-level reasoning enabled by GR-ER 1.5 is deferred to Section [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#S5 \"5 Gemini Robotics 1.5: A Physical Agent ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\").\nThe advantage of interleaving robot actions with explicit thinking steps is particularly evident in the context of longer multi-step tasks, such as \"sorting clothes by colors\" (see Appendix [B.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#A2.SS4 \"B.4 Multi-step benchmark ‣ Appendix B Gemini Robotics 1.5 is a general multi-embodiment Vision-Language-Action Model ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\") for our multi-step benchmark).\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n\nOrchestrator:\nThe orchestrator processes user input and environmental feedback and controls the overall task flow. It breaks complex tasks into simpler steps that can be executed by the VLA, and it performs success detection to decide when to switch to the next step. To accomplish a user-specified task, it can leverage digital tools to access external information or perform additional reasoning steps. We use GR-ER 1.5 as the orchestrator.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nFigure 16: (Left) GR-ER 1.5 uses inference-time compute to improve performance. (Center) GR-ER 1.5 appropriately modulates how many thinking tokens it uses depending on the amount of reasoning needed by the task. Given the same thinking budget, GR-ER 1.5 uses fewest tokens for pointing tasks, and most for video QA. (Right) GR-ER 1.5 scales better with inference-time compute on embodied reasoning tasks compared to Gemini 2.5 Flash. All data points are the average of 3 evaluation runs over the same benchmark sets."
