                  "supportingPassage": "\\[Submitted on 8 Feb 2025 ( [v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05450v1)), last revised 14 Apr 2025 (this version, v2)\\]\n\n# Title:ConRFT: A Reinforced Fine-tuning Method for VLA Models via Consistency Policy\n\nAuthors: [Yuhui Chen](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen,+Y), [Shuai Tian](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Tian,+S), [Shugao Liu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Liu,+S), [Yingting Zhou](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhou,+Y), [Haoran Li](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Li,+H), [Dongbin Zhao](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhao,+D)"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "conrft-openness",
                  "citationId": "conrft-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05450",
                  "supportingPassage": "> Abstract:Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have shown substantial potential in real-world robotic manipulation. However, fine-tuning these models through supervised learning struggles to achieve robust performance due to limited, inconsistent demonstrations, especially in contact-rich environments. In this paper, we propose a reinforced fine-tuning approach for VLA models, named ConRFT, which consists of offline and online fine-tuning with a unified consistency-based training objective, to address these challenges. In the offline stage, our method integrates behavior cloning and Q-learning to effectively extract policy from a small set of demonstrations and stabilize value estimating. In the online stage, the VLA model is further fine-tuned via consistency policy, with human interventions to ensure safe exploration and high sample efficiency. We evaluate our approach on eight diverse real-world manipulation tasks. It achieves an average success rate of 96.3% within 45-90 minutes of online fine-tuning, outperforming prior supervised methods with a 144% improvement in success rate and 1.9x shorter episode length. This work highlights the potential of integrating reinforcement learning to enhance the performance of VLA models for real-world robotic applications. Videos and code are available at our project website [this https URL](https://cccedric.github.io/conrft/)."
                },
