                  "supportingPassage": "Figure 2: GR 1.5 can control three different robots with the same checkpoint to accomplish a variety of tasks out-of-the-box.\n\nGR 1.5 can control robots with dramatically different form factors to complete a large variety of tasks out-of-the-box, without the need for post-training to specialize the model to a particular embodiment or task. [Fig.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#S3.F2 \"In 3 Gemini Robotics 1.5 is a general multi-embodiment Vision-Language-Action Model ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\") shows example tasks on ALOHA, Bi-arm Franka and Apollo humanoid robots.\n\n\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\n## 5 Gemini Robotics 1.5: A Physical Agent\n\nIn this section, we combine GR-ER 1.5, our embodied reasoning model, with GR 1.5, our VLA model, into a full agentic system, and demonstrate how the synergy between these two models enables the execution of complex, long-horizon tasks ( [Ahn et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#bib.bib22 \"\"); [Huang et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#bib.bib12 \"\"); [Shi et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#bib.bib32 \"\")) in out-of-distribution environments.\nThe test scenarios require advanced real-world understanding, tool use, long-horizon task planning, execution, and error recovery. To understand the contribution of different components of the agentic system, we conduct the following ablation study:\n\n- •\n\n\nGR 1.5 (with Thinking On): The Thinking VLA model that thinks before acting ( [Section3.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#S3.SS3 \"3.3 Thinking Helps Acting ‣ 3 Gemini Robotics 1.5 is a general multi-embodiment Vision-Language-Action Model ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\")).\n\n- •\n\n\nAgentic (Gemini 2.5 Flash + GR 1.5): The baseline agentic system, utilizing the Gemini 2.5 Flash model as orchestrator, and our VLA model for execution.\n\n- •\n\n\nAgentic (GR-ER 1.5 + GR 1.5): Our agentic system, utilizing our ER model as orchestrator, and our VLA model for execution.\n\n\nWe choose 8 tasks across the ALOHA and Bi-arm Franka222\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhile we are using the pre-training checkpoint for ALOHA, we performed additional post-training on the bi-arm Franka platform to improve its success rate for long-horizon tasks. platforms to test different aspects of an agent, including tool use, memory, planning, and dexterous manipulation skills. For example, in “Sort Trash”, “Nut Allergy”, and “Mushroom Risotto” the agent needs to perform web search to understand how the objects fit the requirements of the prompt. In “Desk Organization” and “Swap”, the agent is asked to memorize the state of the scene and objects, and then recover the original states. “Pack Suitcase” and “Top shelf to the table” test the GR 1.5 Agent’s 3-D reasoning and dexterity, manipulating soft items on shelving or hangers. The “Blocks in Drawer” task has 9 distinct steps, testing the agent’s planning capability. Details of the benchmark and the progress score definition can be found in Appendix [D.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#A4.SS1 \"D.1 Long-horizon benchmarks ‣ Appendix D Gemini Robotics 1.5: A Physical Agent ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\"). For completeness, we also report success rate results in [D.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.03342#A4.SS2 \"D.2 Success rate ‣ Appendix D Gemini Robotics 1.5: A Physical Agent ‣ Gemini Robotics 1.5: Pushing the Frontier of Generalist Robots with Advanced Embodied Reasoning, Thinking, and Motion Transfer\").\n\n"
