                  "supportingPassage": "Transfer from VLM data into robotics.\nOne of the main motivations for VLAs is transferring knowledge from non-robot data sources to robot policies.\nWe perform an experiment with a mobile manipulation robot, where we investigate semantic generalization to new objects.\nThe robot is tasked with moving objects from a kitchen counter into an (already open) drawer.\nThe objects are not seen during training.\nAs one can see in Fig. [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F8 \"Figure 8 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\") under “OOD Follow Rate”, co-training on VLM data is particularly important for this generalization."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "web-data-result",
                  "citationId": "knowledge-insulation-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://www.pi.website/research/knowledge_insulation",
                  "supportingPassage": "Results on object generalization with mobile manipulator. The robot is asked to grasp unseen objects (OOD) and seen objects (in-distribution) in unseen environments. Co-training on web-data boosts generalization performance most. Insulating the knowledge of the VLM through stopping the gradient from the action expert also helps significantly if no web-data is present."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "items-language",
                  "citationId": "knowledge-insulation-paper-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Figure 4: Comparison to baselines for the “items in drawer” task. Our method outperforms all other baselines both in terms of performance and the ability of the model to follow language instructions. Allowing gradients from the action expert to the backbone (joint-training or π0\\\\pi\\_{0}) harms language following. While π0\\\\pi\\_{0}-FAST maintains good language following, its performance is worse than our method. Neither HybridVLA nor freezing the backbone is viable for this task.![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1/figures/ur5_all.png)Figure 5: Comparison of multiple models/architectures on “table bussing” task with specialist models trained on a single robot embodiment. Our model has the highest performance, low inference time, and follows language instructions well. π0\\\\pi\\_{0}-FAST also follows language well and has good performance, but requires twice the amount of time (wall clock) to solve the task due to slow inference. π0\\\\pi\\_{0} struggles with following language instructions. OpenVLA-OFT follows language well and has low inference time, but has the lowest overall performance."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "no-stop-gradient-qualifier",
                  "citationId": "knowledge-insulation-paper-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Language following.\nIn any scene, a robot can typically execute many sensible actions, for example grasping different objects.\nHere we evaluate whether different models, when given a specific task (provided via natural language instructions), produces actions that attempt to achieve this task.\nThis is particularly important for tasks which have a long-horizon goals such as cleaning a kitchen counter where models could easily overfit on provided data to solving the long-horizon task by focussing on the image inputs alone.\nWe hypothesize that if a VLA maintains more of its VLM pre-training knowledge, it should be more likely to pay attention to the actual language input.\nAs one can see in Fig. [4(b)](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F4.sf2 \"In Figure 4 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\"), stopping the gradient flow from the action expert is an effective way of improving language following compared to π0\\\\pi\\_{0} and joint-training without stop-gradient and without VLM data co-training.\nAs mentioned above already, if the model is co-trained with VLM data, as shown in Fig. [6(a)](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F6.sf1 \"In Figure 6 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\"), Fig. [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F5 \"Figure 5 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\"), and Fig. [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F8 \"Figure 8 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\"), then joint-training without stop-gradient can also achieve good language following.\nFurther, transfusion (Fig. [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1#S6.F5 \"Figure 5 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better\")) follows language better than π0\\\\pi\\_{0} with the action expert, which can be explained by the fact that it reuses the backbone weights for continuous action generation and the only newly initialized parameters are the action projections.\nThese results strongly support the hypothesis that gradients from randomly initialized robotics specific adapters unfavorably interact with the pre-trained VLM weights.\nOur proposed knowledge insulation techniques of stoping gradient flow, and/or co-training with VLM data are able to achieve better language following."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "imperfection",
                  "citationId": "knowledge-insulation-paper-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2505.23705v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Our method provides an effective recipe for training continuous-action VLAs, but does have limitations. Training with both continuous and discrete outputs increases computational cost by about 20% during training. However, due to the increased convergence speed, this cost is offset such that our model still trains much faster (in wall-clock time) relative to purely diffusion based VLAs such as π0\\\\pi\\_{0}. Additionally, while our method improves language following it is still far from perfect, likely because correlations in the training data still cause the model to sometimes ignore language instructions."
                }
