            "supportingPassage": "“In this work, we present and study a training set-up that achieves fast policy generation for real-world robotic tasks by using massive parallelism on a single workstation GPU.<br><br>The parallel approach allows training policies for flat terrain in under four minutes, and in twenty minutes for uneven terrain.<br><br>For our simulation and deployment experiments, we use a policy trained with 4096 robots and a batch size of 98304, which we train for 1500 policy updates in under 20 minutes<br><br>Trained on: i9-11900k CPU, NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU.” Scope: 2021 identifies the preprint/conference, not an independently established experiment date. Preserve the abstract's uneven-terrain twenty-minute headline and Section 4.2's under-twenty-minute tested-run distinction.  Rudin, Hoeller, Reist and Hutter, Learning to Walk in Minutes Using Massively Parallel Deep Reinforcement Learning (arXiv 2109.11978; preprint/CoRL 2021 identity). Retained unversioned ar5iv full text; its revision is not independently labelled. The landing page reports latest v3 (2022-08-19), but this is not a byte-pinned v3 or v2 body. web fetch result observed 2026-09-06T19:18:21.832Z, reported HTTP 200; no new fetch in this normalization.",
