            "sourceChecked": "reality-gap-survey-2026, https://arxiv.org/html/2510.20808 (HTML v1, curl GET 200, 2026-09-16T11:45:14Z, 347,792 bytes, sha256 05d3d5b5956688b10dbb843896047f31e003f8f0ac42f6bd74bb5c28982c406c); identity via https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20808 (curl GET 200, same fetch session)",
            "note": "Verbatim (sec. 3.1.1 Dynamics, Contact Dynamics item): 'To maintain computational efficiency, simulators typically rely on simplified models such as point contacts, linearized friction cones, or compliant spring-damper systems. ... Such mismatches are particularly problematic in contact-rich tasks like robotic manipulation, where precise interaction modeling is crucial for successful control and planning.' The phrase 'not cheaply simulatable' is the article's paraphrase (the survey prints no form of 'cheap'); it is a faithful rendering of the survey's efficiency-vs-fidelity statement. 'Authoritative 2026 statement' matches the survey's acceptance in the Annual Review of Control, Robotics, and Autonomous Systems 2026 (abs Comments, fetched live).",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "why-rl-locomotion-2-reality-gap-20260916g",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "w2-contact-gap-paraphrase",
                  "citationId": "reality-gap-survey-2026",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2510.20808",
                  "supportingPassage": "Contact Dynamics. A key contributor to the dynamics gap in robotics is the inaccurate modeling of physical contact. Real-world contact interactions are inherently complex and nonlinear. Materials deform under pressure, friction varies with relative velocity, and contact states can alternate between sticking, slipping, and separation. To maintain computational efficiency, simulators typically rely on simplified models such as point contacts, linearized friction cones, or compliant spring-damper systems. While these approximations enable faster simulation, they often fail to capture critical physical behaviors. This can result in non-physical artifacts such as spurious internal forces, unstable grasps, or unrealistic motion patterns. Such mismatches are particularly problematic in contact-rich tasks like robotic manipulation, where precise interaction modeling is crucial for successful control and planning. [...] These proxy shapes are evaluated at discrete time steps, which further limits the accuracy of contact modeling, especially in tasks involving fine-grained manipulation or dense contact."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "w2-venue-gloss",
                  "citationId": "reality-gap-survey-2026",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20808",
