            "note": "Current source-backed correction and retained-body review on 2026-09-08 by integrator review source-review integrator 18d95639; not independent acceptance. Original tuple (history, not proof): [\"Isaac Lab on dexterous manipulation: \\\"remains challenging compared to standard parallel-jaw grasping due to the high-dimensional action space and fine-grained control required\\\"\",\"Isaac Lab paper, arXiv 2511.04831v1 HTML, Sec. 5.1.1\",\"verified\",\"Verbatim quote; the paper's answer for DextrAH is SDF collision, accurate contact modeling, and domain randomization, as the article states.\"]. Immediate-before tuple (history, not proof): [\"Isaac Lab on dexterous manipulation: \\\"remains challenging compared to standard parallel-jaw grasping due to the high-dimensional action space and fine-grained control required\\\"\",\"Isaac Lab paper, arXiv 2511.04831v1 HTML, Sec. 5.1.1\",\"verified\",\"Verbatim quote; the paper's answer for DextrAH is SDF collision, accurate contact modeling, and domain randomization, as the article states.\"]. Producer GET 2026-09-08T00:55:54.021238+00:00 to 00:55:54.267675+00:00, observed 200/no redirects; body SHA256 9e3fba6a5031507b29d5ecc529c8dfba329f9f2c25582184b7d067f8aac43a39. Current review 2026-09-08T09:53:04.838899+00:00; no fresh fetch. Every required part/source pair, body context and original tool event checked in validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-isaac-only-integration-20260908/source-proof.json. Complete correction includes source-local reader, mounted CPU disclosure where applicable, and NVIDIA plus 105 unique Appendix A credits. Source-only proposal d4909e105a4855d4b3ecaa19a51463d24714808a7522d5db0f7b5e03acabb650 reviewed rather than accepted prospectively. No whole-article P1/P5 certification or review-date bump.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "isaac-v1-rl-sim2real-why-rl-locomotion-3-20260908",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "challenge",
                  "citationId": "isaac-lab-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2511.04831v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Dexterous manipulation with multi-fingered hands remains challenging compared to standard parallel-jaw grasping due to the high-dimensional action space and fine-grained control required for coordinated finger movements and in-hand object manipulation. While most grasping systems operate open-loop and predict gripper poses for simple contact, they often fall short in dynamic tasks or tasks with high precision requirements. Isaac Lab enables the development of advanced dexterous manipulation policies by offering high-fidelity simulation of multi-DOF hands, accurate contact modeling, and support for rich sensory inputs, including vision and proprioception. It allows for scalable policy training and deployment of learned dexterous manipulation policies on real-world robotic platforms through features such as domain randomization and tiled rendering."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "framework-response",
                  "citationId": "isaac-lab-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2511.04831v1",
                  "supportingPassage": "Dexterous manipulation with multi-fingered hands remains challenging compared to standard parallel-jaw grasping due to the high-dimensional action space and fine-grained control required for coordinated finger movements and in-hand object manipulation. While most grasping systems operate open-loop and predict gripper poses for simple contact, they often fall short in dynamic tasks or tasks with high precision requirements. Isaac Lab enables the development of advanced dexterous manipulation policies by offering high-fidelity simulation of multi-DOF hands, accurate contact modeling, and support for rich sensory inputs, including vision and proprioception. It allows for scalable policy training and deployment of learned dexterous manipulation policies on real-world robotic platforms through features such as domain randomization and tiled rendering."
                }
