                  "citationId": "peng-2018",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/1710.06537v3",
                  "supportingPassage": "### IV-DAdaptive Policy\n\nManipulation tasks, such as pushing, have a strong dependency on the physical properties of the system (e.g. mass, friction, and characteristics of the actuators). In order to determine the appropriate actions, a policy requires some means of inferring the underlying dynamics of its environment. While the dynamics parameters are readily available in simulation, the same does not hold once a policy has been deployed in the real world. In the absence of direct knowledge of the parameters, the dynamics can be inferred from a history of past states and actions. System identification using a history of past trajectories has been previously explored by Yu et al. \\[ [24](https://arxiv.org/html/1710.06537v3#bib.bib24 \"\")\\]. Their system incorporates an online system identification module ϕ⁡(st,ht)=μ^\\\\phi(s\\_{t},h\\_{t})=\\\\hat{\\\\mu}, which utilizes a history of past states and actions ht=\\[at−1,st−1,at−2,st−2,…\\]h\\_{t}=\\[a\\_{t-1},s\\_{t-1},a\\_{t-2},s\\_{t-2},...\\] to predict the dynamics parameters μ\\\\mu. The predicted parameters are then used as inputs to a universal policy that samples an action according to the current state and inferred dynamics π⁡(at\\|st,μ^)\\\\pi(a\\_{t}\\|s\\_{t},\\\\hat{\\\\mu}). However, this decomposition requires identifying the dynamics parameters of interest to be predicted at runtime, which may be difficult for more complex systems. Constructing such a set of parameters necessarily requires some structural assumptions about the dynamics of a system, which may not hold in the real world. Alternatively, SysID can be implicitly embedded into a policy by using a recurrent model π⁡(at\\|st,zt,g)\\\\pi(a\\_{t}\\|s\\_{t},z\\_{t},g), where the internal memory zt=z⁡(ht)z\\_{t}=z(h\\_{t}) acts as a summary of past states and actions, thereby providing a mechanism with which the policy can use to infer the dynamics of the system. This model can then be trained end-to-end and the representation of the internal memory can be learned without requiring manual identification of a set of dynamics parameters to be inferred at runtime."
                }
