            "compound": {
              "planId": "rma-writer-11-20260909",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "base-and-latent",
                  "citationId": "rma-2021",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.04034",
                  "supportingPassage": "We learn a base policy π\\pi which takes as input the current state xt∈ℝ30x_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{30}, previous action at−1∈ℝ12a_{t-1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{12} and the extrinsics vector zt∈ℝ8z_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{8} to predict the next action ata_{t}. The predicted action ata_{t} is the desired joint position for the 1212 robot joints which is converted to torque using a PD controller. The extrinsics vector ztz_{t} is a low dimensional encoding of the environment vector et∈ℝ17e_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{17} generated by μ\\mu.\nzt\\displaystyle z_{t} =μ⁡(et)\\displaystyle=\\mu(e_{t})\n(1)\nat\\displaystyle a_{t} =π⁡(xt,at−1,zt)\\displaystyle=\\pi(x_{t},a_{t-1},z_{t})\n(2)\nWe implement μ\\mu and π\\pi as MLPs (details in Section IV-B). We jointly train the base policy π\\pi and the environmental factor encoder μ\\mu end to end using model-free reinforcement learning. At time step tt, π\\pi takes the current state xtx_{t}, previous action at−1a_{t-1} and the extrinsics zt=μ⁡(et)z_{t}=\\mu(e_{t}), to predict an action ata_{t}. RL maximizes the following expected return of the policy π\\pi:\n\nAbstract\nSuccessful real-world deployment of legged robots would require them to adapt in real-time to unseen scenarios like changing terrains, changing payloads, wear and tear. This paper presents Rapid Motor Adaptation (RMA) algorithm to solve this problem of real-time online adaptation in quadruped robots. RMA consists of two components: a base policy and an adaptation module. The combination of these components enables the robot to adapt to novel situations in fractions of a second. RMA is trained completely in simulation without using any domain knowledge like reference trajectories or predefined foot trajectory generators and is deployed on the A1 robot without any fine-tuning. We train RMA on a varied terrain generator using bioenergetics-inspired rewards and deploy it on a variety of difficult terrains including rocky, slippery, deformable surfaces in environments with grass, long vegetation, concrete, pebbles, stairs, sand, etc. RMA shows state-of-the-art performance across diverse real-world as well as simulation experiments. Video results at https://ashish-kmr.github.io/rma-legged-robots/.\nFig. 0: RMA consists of two subsystems - the base policy π\\pi and the adaptation module ϕ\\phi. Top: RMA is trained in two phases. In the first phase, the base policy π\\pi takes as input the current state xtx_{t}, previous action at−1a_{t-1} and the privileged environmental factors ete_{t} which is encoded into the latent extrinsics vector ztz_{t} using the environmental factor encoder μ\\mu. The base policy is trained in simulation using model-free RL. In the second phase, the adaptation module ϕ\\phi is trained to predict the extrinsics zt^\\hat{z_{t}} from the history of state and actions via supervised learning with on-policy data. Bottom: At deployment, the adaptation module ϕ\\phi generates the extrinsics z^t\\hat{z}_{t} at 10Hz, and the base policy generates the desired joint positions at 100Hz which are converted to torques using A1’s PD controller. Since the adaptation module runs at a lower frequency, the base policy consumes the most recent extrinsics vector z^t\\hat{z}_{t} predicted by the adaptation module to predict ata_{t}. This asynchronous design was critical for seamless deployment on low-cost robots like A1 with limited on-board compute. Videos at: https://ashish-kmr.github.io/rma-legged-robots/"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "adaptation-training-history",
                  "citationId": "rma-2021",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.04034",
                  "supportingPassage": "The knowledge of privileged environment configuration ete_{t} and its encoded extrinsics vector ztz_{t} are not accessible during deployment in the real-world. Hence, we propose to estimate the extrinsics online using the adaptation module ϕ\\phi. Instead of ete_{t}, the adaptation module uses the recent history of robot’s states xt−k:t−1x_{t-k:t-1} and actions at−k:t−1a_{t-k:t-1} to generate zt^\\hat{z_{t}} which is an estimate of the true extrinsics vector ztz_{t}. In our experiments, we use k=50k=50 which corresponds to 0.50.5s.\nzt^=ϕ(xt−k:t−1,at−k:t−1)\\hat{z_{t}}=\\phi\\big(x_{t-k:t-1},a_{t-k:t-1}\\big)\nNote that instead of predicting ete_{t}, which is the case in typical system identification, we directly estimate the extrinsics ztz_{t} that only encodes how the behavior should change to correct for the given environment vector ete_{t}.\nTo train the adaptation module, we just need the state-action history and the target value of ztz_{t} (given by the environmental factor encoder μ\\mu). Both of these are available in simulation, and hence, ϕ\\phi can be trained via supervised learning to minimize: MSE​(zt^,zt)=‖zt^−zt‖2,\\text{MSE}(\\hat{z_{t}},z_{t})=\\|\\hat{z_{t}}-z_{t}\\|^{2}, where zt=μ⁡(et)z_{t}=\\mu(e_{t}). We model ϕ\\phi as a 11-D CNN to capture temporal correlations (Section IV-B).\nOne way to collect the state-action history is to unroll the trained base policy π\\pi with the ground truth ztz_{t}. However, such a dataset will contain examples of only good trajectories where the robot walks seamlessly. Adaptation module ϕ\\phi trained on this data would not be robust to deviations from the expert trajectory, which will happen often during deployment.\nWe resolve this problem by training ϕ\\phi with on-policy data (similar to Ross et al. 2011). We unroll the base policy π\\pi with the zt^\\hat{z_{t}} predicted by the randomly initialized policy ϕ\\phi. We then use this state action history, paired with the ground truth ztz_{t} to train ϕ\\phi. We iteratively repeat this until convergence. This training procedure ensures that RMA sees enough exploration trajectories during training due to a) randomly initialized ϕ\\phi, and b) imperfect prediction of zt^\\hat{z_{t}}. This adds robustness to the performance of RMA during deployment.\nIII-C Asynchronous Deployment"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "deployment",
                  "citationId": "rma-2021",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.04034",
                  "supportingPassage": "We train RMA completely in simulation and then deploy it in the real world without any modification or fine-tuning. The two subsystems of RMA run asynchronously and at substantially different frequencies, and hence, can easily run using little on-board compute. The adaptation policy is slow because it operates on the state-action history of 5050 time steps, roughly updating the extrinsic vector zt^\\hat{z_{t}} once every 0.10.1s (10 Hz). The base policy runs at 100 Hz and uses the most recent zt^\\hat{z_{t}} generated by the adaptation module, along with the current state and the previous action, to predict ata_{t}. This asynchronous execution doesn’t hurt performance in practice because zt^\\hat{z_{t}} changes relatively infrequently in the real world.\nAlternately, we could have trained a base policy which directly takes the state and action history as input without decoupling them into the two modules. We found that this (a) leads to unnatural gaits and poor performance in simulation, (b) can only run at 10Hz on the on-board compute, and (c) lacks the asynchronous design which is critical for a seamless deployment of RMA on the real robot without the need for any synchronization or calibration of the two subsystems. This asynchronous design is fundamentally enabled by the decoupling of the relatively infrequently changing extrinsics vector with the quickly changing robot state.\n\nProcess 1 operating at 100 Hz;\nt←0t\\leftarrow 0;\nwhile not fall do\nat←π⁡(xt,at−1,z^async)a_{t}\\leftarrow\\pi(x_{t},a_{t-1},\\hat{z}_{\\mathrm{async}});\nxt+1←x_{t+1}\\leftarrow env.step(ata_{t});\nt←t+1t\\leftarrow t+1;\nend while\nProcess 2 operating at 10 Hz;\nwhile not fall do\nz^async←ϕ(xt−k:k,at−k−1:k−1)\\hat{z}_{\\mathrm{async}}\\leftarrow\\phi(x_{t-k:k},a_{t-k-1:k-1});\nend while\nAlgorithm 2 Rapid Motor AdaptationDeployment"
                },
