                  "supportingPassage": "### II-AData Generation: Retargeting Human Video Data\n\nTo track expressive and agile motions, we collect a video dataset of human movements and retarget it to robot motions, creating imitation goals for motion-tracking policies, as shown in [Figure3](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S1.F3 \"In I Introduction ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") and [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (a).\n\n#### Transforming Human Video to SMPL Motions\n\nWe begin by recording videos (see [Figure3](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S1.F3 \"In I Introduction ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (a) and [Figure12](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S5.F12 \"In V-B Different Usage of Delta Action Model ‣ V EXTENSIVE STUDIES AND ANALYSES ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\")) of humans performing expressive and agile motions. Using TRAM \\[ [93](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib93 \"\")\\], we reconstruct 3D motions from videos. TRAM estimates the global trajectory of the human motions in SMPL parameter format \\[ [52](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib52 \"\")\\], which includes global root translation, orientation, body poses, and shape parameters, as shown in [Figure3](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S1.F3 \"In I Introduction ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (b). The resulting motions are denoted as 𝒟SMPL{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\_{\\\\text{SMPL}}.\n\n#### Simulation-based Data Cleaning\n\nSince the reconstruction process can introduce noise and errors \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib25 \"\")\\], some estimated motions may not be physically feasible, making them unsuitable for motion tracking in the real world. To address this, we employ a “sim-to-data” cleaning procedure. Specifically, we use MaskedMimic \\[ [86](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib86 \"\")\\], a physics-based motion tracker, to imitate the SMPL motions from TRAM in IsaacGym simulator \\[ [58](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib58 \"\")\\]. The motions ( [Figure3](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S1.F3 \"In I Introduction ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (c)) that pass this simulation-based validation are saved as the cleaned dataset 𝒟SMPLCleaned{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\_{\\\\text{SMPL}}^{\\\\text{Cleaned}}.\n\n#### Retargeting SMPL Motions to Robot Motions\n\nWith the cleaned dataset 𝒟SMPLCleaned{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\_{\\\\text{SMPL}}^{\\\\text{Cleaned}} in SMPL format, we retarget the motions into robot motions following the shape-and-motion two-stage retargeting process \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib25 \"\")\\]. Since the SMPL parameters estimated by TRAM represent various human body shapes, we first optimize the shape parameter 𝜷′\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\beta}^{\\\\prime} to approximate a humanoid shape. By selecting 12 body links with correspondences between humans and humanoids, we perform gradient descent on 𝜷′\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\beta}^{\\\\prime} to minimize joint distances in the rest pose. Using the optimized shape 𝜷′\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\beta}^{\\\\prime} along with the original translation 𝒑\\\\boldsymbol{p} and pose 𝜽\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\theta}, we apply gradient descent to further minimize the distances of the body links. This process ensures accurate motion retargeting and produces the cleaned robot trajectory dataset 𝒟RobotCleaned{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\_{\\\\text{Robot}}^{\\\\text{Cleaned}}, as shown in [Figure3](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S1.F3 \"In I Introduction ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (d).\n\n### II-BPhase-based Motion Tracking Policy Training\n\nWe formulate the motion-tracking problem as a goal-conditioned reinforcement learning (RL) task, where the policy π\\\\pi is trained to track the retargeted robot movement trajectories in the dataset 𝒟RobotCleaned{\\\\mathcal{D}}\\_{\\\\text{Robot}}^{\\\\text{Cleaned}}. Inspired by  \\[ [67](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib67 \"\")\\], the state sts\\_{t} includes the robot’s proprioception stps\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{p}} and a time phase variable ϕ∈\\[0,1\\]\\\\phi\\\\in\\[0,1\\], where ϕ=0\\\\phi=0 represents the start of a motion and ϕ=1\\\\phi=1 represents the end. This time phase variable alone ϕ\\\\phi is proven to be sufficient to serve as the goal state 𝒔tg\\\\boldsymbol{s}\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{g}} for single-motion tracking \\[ [67](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib67 \"\")\\].\nThe proprioception stps\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{p}} is defined as stp≜\\[𝒒t−4:t,𝒒˙t−4:t,𝝎t−4:tr​o​o​t,𝒈t−4:t,𝒂t−5:t−1\\]s\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{p}}\\\\triangleq\\\\left\\[{\\\\boldsymbol{{q}}\\_{t-4:t}},{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\dot{q}}\\_{t-4:t}},{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\omega}^{root}\\_{t-4:t}},{\\\\boldsymbol{g}\\_{t-4:t}},{\\\\boldsymbol{a}\\_{t-5:t-1}}\\\\right\\], with 5-step history of joint position 𝒒t∈ℝ23{\\\\boldsymbol{{q}}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23}, joint velocity 𝒒˙t∈ℝ23{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\dot{q}}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23}, root angular velocity 𝝎tr​o​o​t∈ℝ3{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\omega}^{root}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3}, root projected gravity 𝒈t∈ℝ3{\\\\boldsymbol{g}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3}, and last action 𝒂t−1∈ℝ23{\\\\boldsymbol{a}\\_{t-1}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23}.\nUsing the agent’s proprioception stps\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{p}} and the goal state 𝒔tg\\\\boldsymbol{s}\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{g}}, we define the reward as rt=ℛ⁡(stp,stg)r\\_{t}=\\\\mathcal{R}\\\\left(s\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{p}},s\\_{t}^{\\\\mathrm{g}}\\\\right), which is used for policy optimization. The specific reward terms can be found in [TableI](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S2.T1 \"In Reward Terms ‣ II-B Phase-based Motion Tracking Policy Training ‣ II Pre-training: Learning Agile Humanoid Skills ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). The action 𝒂t∈ℝ23\\\\boldsymbol{a}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23} corresponds to the target joint positions and is passed to a PD controller that actuates the robot’s degrees of freedom. To optimize the policy, we use the proximal policy optimization (PPO) \\[ [80](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib80 \"\")\\], aiming to maximize the cumulative discounted reward 𝔼⁡\\[∑t=1Tγt−1​rt\\]\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\left\\[\\\\sum\\_{t=1}^{T}\\\\gamma^{t-1}r\\_{t}\\\\right\\]. We identify several design choices that are crucial for achieving stable policy training:\n\n#### Asymmetric Actor-Critic Training\n\nReal-world humanoid control is inherently a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP), where certain task-relevant properties that are readily available in simulation become unobservable in real-world scenarios. However, these missing properties can significantly facilitate policy training in simulation. To bridge this gap, we employ an asymmetric actor-critic framework, where the critic network has access to privileged information such as the global positions of the reference motion and the root linear velocity, while the actor network relies solely on proprioceptive inputs and a time-phase variable. This design not only enhances phase-based motion tracking during training but also enables a simple, phase-driven motion goal for sim-to-real transfer. Crucially, because the actor does not depend on position-based motion targets, our approach eliminates the need for odometry during real-world deployment—overcoming a well-documented challenge in prior work on humanoid robots \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib25 \"\"), [24](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib24 \"\")\\]."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "rollouts",
                  "citationId": "asap-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143",
                  "supportingPassage": "### III-AData Collection\n\nWe deploy the pretrained policy in the real world to perform whole-body motion tracking tasks (as depicted in [Figure9](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4.F9 \"In IV-C Real-World Evaluations ‣ IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\")) and record the resulting trajectories, denoted as 𝒟r={s0r,a0r,…,sTr,aTr}\\\\mathcal{D}^{\\\\text{r}}=\\\\{s^{\\\\text{r}}\\_{0},a^{\\\\text{r}}\\_{0},\\\\dots,s^{\\\\text{r}}\\_{T},a^{\\\\text{r}}\\_{T}\\\\}, as illustrated in [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (a). At each timestep tt, we use a motion capture device and onboard sensors to record the state:\nst=\\[ptbase,vtbase,αtbase,ωtbase,qt,q˙t\\],s\\_{t}=\\[p^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t},v\\_{t}^{\\\\text{base}},\\\\alpha^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t},\\\\omega^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t},q\\_{t},\\\\dot{q}\\_{t}\\],\nwhere ptbase∈ℝ3p^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3} represents the robot base 3D position, vtbase∈ℝ3v\\_{t}^{\\\\text{base}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3} is base linear velocity, αtbase∈ℝ4\\\\alpha^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{4} is the robot base orientation represented as a quaternion, ωtbase∈ℝ3\\\\omega^{\\\\text{base}}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3} is the base angular velocity, qt∈ℝ23q\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23} is the vector of joint positions, and q˙t∈ℝ23\\\\dot{q}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{23} represents joint velocities.\n\nFig. 4: Baselines of ASAP. (a) Model-free RL training. (b) System ID from real to sim using real-world data. (c) Learning delta dynamics model using real-world data. (d) Our proposed method, learning delta action model using real-world data.\n\n### IV-CReal-World Evaluations\n\nTo answer Q3 (Does ASAP work for sim-to-real transfer?). We validate ASAP on real-world Unitree G1 robot.\n\nReal-World Data.\nIn the real-world experiments, we prioritize both motion safety and representativeness by selecting five motion-tracking tasks, including (i) kick, (ii) jump forward, (iii) step forward and back, (iv) single foot balance and (v) single foot jump.\nHowever, collecting over 400 real-world motion clips— the minimum required to train the full 23-DoF delta action model in simulation, as discussed in [SectionIII-B](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S3.SS2 \"III-B Training Delta Action Model ‣ III Post-training: Training Delta Action Model and Fine-tuning Motion Tracking Policy ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\")—poses significant challenges. Our experiments involve highly dynamic motions that cause rapid overheating of joint motors, leading to hardware failures (two Unitree G1 robots broke during data collection).\nGiven these constraints, we adopt a more sample-efficient approach by focusing exclusively on learning a 4-DoF ankle delta action model rather than the full-body 23-DoF model. This decision is motivated by two key factors: (1) the limited availability of real-world data makes training the full 23-DoF delta action model infeasible, and (2) the Unitree G1 robot \\[ [77](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib77 \"\")\\] features a mechanical linkage design in the ankle, which introduces a significant sim-to-real gap that is difficult to bridge with conventional modeling techniques \\[ [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib37 \"\")\\].\nUnder this setting, the original 23 DoF delta action model reduces to 4 DoF delta action model, which needs much less data to be trainable. In practice, we collect 100 motion clips, which prove sufficient to train an effective 4-DoF delta action model for real-world scenarios.\n\nWe execute the tracking policy 30 times for each task. In addition to these motion-tracking tasks, we also collect 10 minutes of locomotion data. The locomotion policy will be addressed in the next section, which is also utilized to bridge different tracking policies.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143v3/ASAP-RW-DeltaA-crop.png)Fig. 8: Visualization of LeBron James’ “Silencer” motion on the G1 robot before (upper figure enclosed in blue) and after (bottom figure enclosed in red) ASAP policy finetuning. The left half shows the policy finetuning for the in-distribution motions while the right half shows the out-of-distribution ones. After ASAP finetuning, the robot behaves more smoothly and reduces jerky lower-body motions.![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143v3/ASAP-MoreReal-crop.png)Fig. 9: We deploy the pretrained policy of a forward jump motion tracking task, challenging the 1.35m-tall Unitree G1 robot for a forward leap over 1m.\n\nPolicy Transition.\nIn the real world, we cannot easily reset the robot as in simulators, and therefore we train a robust locomotion policy for the policy transition between different motion-tracking tasks. Our locomotion command contains (v,ω,Π)(v,\\\\omega,\\\\Pi), where vv and ω\\\\omega indicate the linear and angular velocities while Π\\\\Pi indicates the command to walk or stand still. After each motion-tracking task is done, our locomotion policy will take over to keep the robot balance until the next motion-tracking task begins. In this way, the robot is able to execute multiple tasks without manually resetting.\n\nReal-World Results.\nThe sim-to-real gap is more pronounced than simulator-to-simulator discrepancies due to factors such as noisy sensor input, inaccuracies in robot modeling, and actuator differences.\nTo evaluate the effectiveness of ASAP in addressing these gaps, we compare the closed-loop performance of ASAP with the Vanilla baseline on two representative motion tracking tasks (kicking and “Silencer”) in which observe obvious sim-to-real gaps.\nTo show the generalizability of the learned delta action model for out-of-distribution motions, we also fine-tune the policy for LeBron James’ “Silencer” motion as shown in ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics\n\nfor Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills and [Figure8](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4.F8 \"In IV-C Real-World Evaluations ‣ IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). The experiment data is summarized in [TableV](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4.T5 \"In IV-C Real-World Evaluations ‣ IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). It shows that ASAP outperforms the baseline on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution humanoid motion tracking tasks, achieving a considerable reduction of the tracking errors across all key metrics (Eg-mpjpe,Empjpe,Ea​c​cE\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}},E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}},E\\_{acc} and Ev​e​lE\\_{vel}). These findings highlight the effectiveness of ASAP in improving sim-to-real transfer for agile humanoid motion tracking."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "delta-action",
                  "citationId": "asap-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143",
                  "supportingPassage": "### III-BTraining Delta Action Model\n\nDue to the sim-to-real gap, when we replay the real-world trajectories in simulation, the resulting simulated trajectory will likely deviate significantly from real-world recorded trajectories. This discrepancy is a valuable learning signal for learning the mismatch between simulation and real-world physics. We leverage an RL-based delta/residual action model to compensate for the sim-to-real physics gap.\n\nAs illustrated in [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (b), the delta action model is defined as Δ​at=πθΔ​(st,at)\\\\Delta a\\_{t}=\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t}), where the policy πθΔ\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta} learns to output corrective actions based on the current state sts\\_{t} and the action ata\\_{t}. These corrective actions (Δ​at\\\\Delta a\\_{t}) are added to the real-world recorded actions (atra^{r}\\_{t}) to account for discrepancies between simulation and real-world dynamics.\n\nThe RL environment incorporates this delta action model by modifying the simulator dynamics as follows: st+1=fsim​(st,atr+Δ​at)s\\_{t+1}=f^{\\\\text{sim}}(s\\_{t},a^{r}\\_{t}+\\\\Delta a\\_{t}) where fsimf^{\\\\text{sim}} represents the simulator’s dynamics, atra^{r}\\_{t} is the reference action recorded from real-world rollouts, and Δ​at\\\\Delta a\\_{t} introduces corrections learned by the delta action model.\n\nTABLE II: Reward Terms for Delta Action Learning\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Term | Weight | Term | Weight |\n| Penalty |\n| DoF position limits | −10.0-10.0 | DoF velocity limits | −5.0-5.0 |\n| Torque limits | −0.1-0.1 | Termination | −200.0-200.0 |\n| Regularization |\n| Action rate | −0.01-0.01 | Action norm | −0.2-0.2 |\n| Task Reward |\n| Body position | 1.01.0 | VR 3-point | 1.01.0 |\n| Body position (feet) | 1.01.0 | Body rotation | 0.50.5 |\n| Body angular velocity | 0.50.5 | Body velocity | 0.50.5 |\n| DoF position | 0.50.5 | DoF velocity | 0.50.5 |\n\nDuring each RL step:\n\n1. 1.\n\n\nThe robot is initialized at the real-world state strs^{r}\\_{t}.\n\n2. 2.\n\n\nA reward signal is computed to minimize the discrepancy between the simulated state st+1s\\_{t+1} and the recorded real-world state st+1rs^{r}\\_{t+1}, with an additional action magnitude regularization term OPENexp⁡(−∥at∥)−1)\\\\exp(-\\\\lVert a\\_{t}\\\\rVert)-1), as specified in [TableII](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S3.T2 \"In III-B Training Delta Action Model ‣ III Post-training: Training Delta Action Model and Fine-tuning Motion Tracking Policy ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). The workflow is illustrated in [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (b).\n\n3. 3.\n\n\nPPO is used to train the delta action policy πθΔ\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta}, learning corrected Δ​at\\\\Delta a\\_{t} to match simulation and the real world.\n\n\nBy learning the delta action model, the simulator can accurately reproduce real-world failures. For example, consider a scenario where the simulated robot can jump because its motor strength is overestimated, but the real-world robot cannot jump due to weaker motors. The delta action model πθΔ\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta} will learn to reduce the intensity of lower-body actions, simulating the motor limitations of the real-world robot. This allows the simulator to replicate the real-world dynamics and enables the policy to be fine-tuned to handle these limitations effectively.\n\n### VIII-CImplementation of Delta Dynamics Learning\n\nUsing the collected real-world trajectory, we replay the action sequence {a0real,…,aTreal}\\\\{a^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{0},\\\\dots,a^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{T}\\\\} in simulation and record the resulting trajectory {s0sim,…,sTsim}\\\\{s^{\\\\text{sim}}\\_{0},\\\\dots,s^{\\\\text{sim}}\\_{T}\\\\}. The neural dynamics model fθΔf^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta} is trained to predict the difference:\n\n|     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | st+1real−st+1sim=fθΔ​(streal,atreal),∀t.s^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{t+1}-s^{\\\\text{sim}}\\_{t+1}=f^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta}(s^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{t},a^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{t}),\\\\quad\\\\forall t. |  |\n\nIn practice, we compute the mean squared error (MSE) loss in an autoregressive setting, where the model predicts forward for KK steps and uses gradient descent to minimize the loss. To balance learning efficiency and stability over long horizons, we implement a schedule that gradually increases KK during training. Formally, the optimization objective is:\n\n|     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ℒ=∥st+Kreal−fsim​(…​fsimCLOSE⏟K(st,at)+fθΔ(st,at),…,at+K)∥.\\\\mathcal{L}=\\\\bigg\\\\lVert s^{\\\\text{real}}\\_{t+K}-\\\\underbrace{f^{\\\\text{sim}}\\\\big(\\\\dots f^{\\\\text{sim}}}\\_{K}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t})+f^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t}),\\\\dots,a\\_{t+K}\\\\big)\\\\bigg\\\\rVert. |  |\n\nAfter training, we freeze the residual dynamics model fθΔf^{\\\\Delta}\\_{\\\\theta} and integrate it into the simulator. During each simulation step, the robot’s state is updated by incorporating the delta predicted by the dynamics model. In this augmented simulation environment, we finetune the previously pretrained policy to adapt to the corrected dynamics, ensuring improved alignment with real-world behavior."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "fine-tune-deploy",
                  "citationId": "asap-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143",
                  "supportingPassage": "### III-CFine-tuning Motion Tracking Policy under New Dynamics\n\nWith the learned delta action model πΔ​(st,at)\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t}), we can reconstruct the simulation environment with\n\n|     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | st+1=fASAP​(st,at)=fsim​(st,at+πΔ​(st,at)),s\\_{t+1}=f^{\\\\text{{\\\\color\\[rgb\\]{0.75,0.0117,0.0117}ASAP} }}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t})=f^{\\\\text{sim}}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t}+\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t})), |  |\n\nAs shown in [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (c), we keep the πΔ\\\\pi^{\\\\Delta} model parameters frozen, and fine-tune the pretrained policy with the same reward summarized in [TableI](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S2.T1 \"In Reward Terms ‣ II-B Phase-based Motion Tracking Policy Training ‣ II Pre-training: Learning Agile Humanoid Skills ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\").\n\n### III-DPolicy Deployment\n\nFinally, we deploy the fine-tuned policy without delta action model in the real world as shown in [Figure2](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S0.F2 \"In Abstract ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\") (d). The fine-tuned policy shows enhanced real-world motion tracking performance compared to the pre-trained policy. Quantitative improvements will be discussed in [SectionIV](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4 \"IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\")."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "hardware-dimension",
                  "citationId": "asap-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143",
                  "supportingPassage": "### IV-CReal-World Evaluations\n\nTo answer Q3 (Does ASAP work for sim-to-real transfer?). We validate ASAP on real-world Unitree G1 robot.\n\nReal-World Data.\nIn the real-world experiments, we prioritize both motion safety and representativeness by selecting five motion-tracking tasks, including (i) kick, (ii) jump forward, (iii) step forward and back, (iv) single foot balance and (v) single foot jump.\nHowever, collecting over 400 real-world motion clips— the minimum required to train the full 23-DoF delta action model in simulation, as discussed in [SectionIII-B](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S3.SS2 \"III-B Training Delta Action Model ‣ III Post-training: Training Delta Action Model and Fine-tuning Motion Tracking Policy ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\")—poses significant challenges. Our experiments involve highly dynamic motions that cause rapid overheating of joint motors, leading to hardware failures (two Unitree G1 robots broke during data collection).\nGiven these constraints, we adopt a more sample-efficient approach by focusing exclusively on learning a 4-DoF ankle delta action model rather than the full-body 23-DoF model. This decision is motivated by two key factors: (1) the limited availability of real-world data makes training the full 23-DoF delta action model infeasible, and (2) the Unitree G1 robot \\[ [77](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib77 \"\")\\] features a mechanical linkage design in the ankle, which introduces a significant sim-to-real gap that is difficult to bridge with conventional modeling techniques \\[ [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#bib.bib37 \"\")\\].\nUnder this setting, the original 23 DoF delta action model reduces to 4 DoF delta action model, which needs much less data to be trainable. In practice, we collect 100 motion clips, which prove sufficient to train an effective 4-DoF delta action model for real-world scenarios.\n\nWe execute the tracking policy 30 times for each task. In addition to these motion-tracking tasks, we also collect 10 minutes of locomotion data. The locomotion policy will be addressed in the next section, which is also utilized to bridge different tracking policies.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143v3/ASAP-RW-DeltaA-crop.png)Fig. 8: Visualization of LeBron James’ “Silencer” motion on the G1 robot before (upper figure enclosed in blue) and after (bottom figure enclosed in red) ASAP policy finetuning. The left half shows the policy finetuning for the in-distribution motions while the right half shows the out-of-distribution ones. After ASAP finetuning, the robot behaves more smoothly and reduces jerky lower-body motions.![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143v3/ASAP-MoreReal-crop.png)Fig. 9: We deploy the pretrained policy of a forward jump motion tracking task, challenging the 1.35m-tall Unitree G1 robot for a forward leap over 1m.\n\nPolicy Transition.\nIn the real world, we cannot easily reset the robot as in simulators, and therefore we train a robust locomotion policy for the policy transition between different motion-tracking tasks. Our locomotion command contains (v,ω,Π)(v,\\\\omega,\\\\Pi), where vv and ω\\\\omega indicate the linear and angular velocities while Π\\\\Pi indicates the command to walk or stand still. After each motion-tracking task is done, our locomotion policy will take over to keep the robot balance until the next motion-tracking task begins. In this way, the robot is able to execute multiple tasks without manually resetting.\n\nReal-World Results.\nThe sim-to-real gap is more pronounced than simulator-to-simulator discrepancies due to factors such as noisy sensor input, inaccuracies in robot modeling, and actuator differences.\nTo evaluate the effectiveness of ASAP in addressing these gaps, we compare the closed-loop performance of ASAP with the Vanilla baseline on two representative motion tracking tasks (kicking and “Silencer”) in which observe obvious sim-to-real gaps.\nTo show the generalizability of the learned delta action model for out-of-distribution motions, we also fine-tune the policy for LeBron James’ “Silencer” motion as shown in ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics\n\nfor Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills and [Figure8](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4.F8 \"In IV-C Real-World Evaluations ‣ IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). The experiment data is summarized in [TableV](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01143#S4.T5 \"In IV-C Real-World Evaluations ‣ IV Performance Evaluation of ASAP ‣ ASAP: Aligning Simulation and Real-World Physics for Learning Agile Humanoid Whole-Body Skills\"). It shows that ASAP outperforms the baseline on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution humanoid motion tracking tasks, achieving a considerable reduction of the tracking errors across all key metrics (Eg-mpjpe,Empjpe,Ea​c​cE\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}},E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}},E\\_{acc} and Ev​e​lE\\_{vel}). These findings highlight the effectiveness of ASAP in improving sim-to-real transfer for agile humanoid motion tracking."
                }
