                  "supportingPassage": "### VI-BReal-world Demonstrations\n\n#### Deployment Details\n\nFor real-world deployment tests, we use a standard 1080P webcam as the RGB camera, and use HybrIK \\[ [27](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436#bib.bibx27 \"\")\\] as the 3D human pose estimator running at 30Hz. For the linear velocity estimation of the robot, we leverage the motion capture system (50Hz), and all the other proprioception is obtained from built-in sensors (200Hz) of Unitree H1 humanoid. Linear velocity state estimation could be replaced by onboard visual/LiDAR odometry methods, though we opt in to MoCap for this work due to its simplicity.\n\n#### Real-world Teleoperation Results\n\nFor real-time teleoperation, the 3D pose estimation from the RGB camera is noisy and can suffer from perspective bias, but our H2O policy shows a strong generalization ability to real-world estimated motion goals in real-time. The real-world teleoperation is shown in [Figure1](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436#S0.F1 \"In Learning Human-to-Humanoid Real-Time Whole-Body Teleoperation\"), [Figure5](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436#S6.F5 \"In Real-world Teleoperation Results ‣ VI-B Real-world Demonstrations ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Learning Human-to-Humanoid Real-Time Whole-Body Teleoperation\") and [Figure6](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436#S6.F6 \"In Real-world Teleoperation Results ‣ VI-B Real-world Demonstrations ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Learning Human-to-Humanoid Real-Time Whole-Body Teleoperation\"), where H2O enables precise real-time teleoperation of humanoids to do whole-body dynamic motions like ball kicking, walking, and back jumping. More demonstrations can be found on our [website](https://human2humanoid.com/ \"\").\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436v1/H2O-realexperiment-leftrightkick-crop.png)Fig. 5: The humanoid robot is able to track the precise lower-body movements of the human teleoperator.![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436v1/H2O-realexperiment-walking-backjumping-crop.png)Fig. 6: The humanoid robot is able to track walking motions of human-style pace and imitate continuous back jumping.\n\n#### Robustness\n\nOur H2O system can keep balance under external force disturbances, as shown in [Figure7](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436#S6.F7 \"In Robustness ‣ VI-B Real-world Demonstrations ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Learning Human-to-Humanoid Real-Time Whole-Body Teleoperation\"). These tests demonstrate the robustness of our system.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.04436v1/H2O-robustness-crop.png)Fig. 7: Robustness Tests of our H2O system under powerful kicking. The policy is able to maintain balance for both stable and dynamic teleoperated motions.\n\n### V-AState Space\n\nTo achieve real-time teleoperation of humanoid robots, the state space of RL policy must contain only quantities available in the real world. This differs from the simulation-only approaches, where all the physics information (\\\\eg, foot contact force) is available. For example, in the real world, we have no access to each joint’s precise global angular velocity due to the lack of IMUs, but the privileged policy πprivileged{\\\\pi\\_{\\\\text{privileged}}} requires them.\n\nIn our state space design, proprioception is defined by 𝒔tp≜\\[𝒒t,𝒒˙t,𝒗t,𝝎t,𝒈t,𝒂t−1\\]{\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{p}}\\_{t}}\\\\triangleq\\\\left\\[{\\\\bm{{q}}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{\\\\dot{q}}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{{v}}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{\\\\omega}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{g}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{a}\\_{t-1}}\\\\right\\], with joint position 𝒒t∈ℝ19{\\\\bm{{q}}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{19} (DoF position), joint velocity 𝒒˙t∈ℝ19{\\\\bm{\\\\dot{q}}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{19} (DoF velocity), root linear velocity 𝒗t∈ℝ3{\\\\bm{{v}}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3}, root angular velocity 𝝎t∈ℝ3{\\\\bm{\\\\omega}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3}, root projected gravity 𝒈t∈ℝ3{\\\\bm{g}\\_{t}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{3}, and last action 𝒂t−1∈ℝ19{\\\\bm{a}\\_{t-1}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{19}. The goal state is 𝒔tg≜\\[𝒑^tkp,𝒑^tkp−𝒑tkp,𝒑˙^tkp\\]{\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g}}\\_{t}}\\\\triangleq\\[{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}},{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}}-{\\\\bm{{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}},{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\dot{p}}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}}\\]. 𝒑^tkp∈ℝ8×3{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{8\\\\times 3} is the position of eight selected reference body positions (shoulders, elbows, hands, ankles); 𝒑^tkp−𝒑tkp{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}}-{\\\\bm{{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}} is the position difference between the reference joints and humanoid’s own joints; 𝒑˙^tkp{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\dot{p}}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}} is the linear velocity of the reference joints. All values are normalized to the humanoid’s own coordinate system.\nAs a comparison, we also consider a reduced goal state 𝒔tg-reduced≜(𝒑^tkp){\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g-reduced}}\\_{t}}\\\\triangleq({\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}}), where only the reference position 𝒑^tkp{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\text{kp}} but not the position difference. The action space of the agile policy consists of 19-dim joint targets. A PD controller tracks these joint targets by converting them to joint torques: τ=Kp​(𝒂t−𝒒t)−Kd​𝒒˙t\\\\tau=K\\_{p}({\\\\bm{a}\\_{t}}-{\\\\bm{{q}}\\_{t}})-K\\_{d}{\\\\bm{\\\\dot{q}}\\_{t}}."
