                  "supportingPassage": "# Title:Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\n\nAuthors: [Se Hwan Jeon](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Jeon,+S+H), [Steve Heim](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Heim,+S), [Charles Khazoom](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Khazoom,+C), [Sangbae Kim](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kim,+S)\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion, by Se Hwan Jeon and 3 other authors\n\n[View PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.10142) [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.10142v1)\n\n> Abstract:The main challenge in developing effective reinforcement learning (RL) pipelines is often the design and tuning the reward functions. Well-designed shaping reward can lead to significantly faster learning. Naively formulated rewards, however, can conflict with the desired behavior and result in overfitting or even erratic performance if not properly tuned. In theory, the broad class of potential based reward shaping (PBRS) can help guide the learning process without affecting the optimal policy. Although several studies have explored the use of potential based reward shaping to accelerate learning convergence, most have been limited to grid-worlds and low-dimensional systems, and RL in robotics has predominantly relied on standard forms of reward shaping. In this paper, we benchmark standard forms of shaping with PBRS for a humanoid robot. We find that in this high-dimensional system, PBRS has only marginal benefits in convergence speed. However, the PBRS reward terms are significantly more robust to scaling than typical reward shaping approaches, and thus easier to tune.\n\n|     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n| Subjects: | Robotics (cs.RO); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG) |\n| Cite as: | [arXiv:2307.10142](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.10142) \\[cs.RO\\] |\n|  | (or [arXiv:2307.10142v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.10142v1) \\[cs.RO\\] for this version) |\n|  | [https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.10142](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.10142)<br>Focus to learn more<br>arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite |\n| Journal reference: | 2023 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA), London, United Kingdom, 2023, pp. 9204-9210 |\n| Related DOI: | [https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10160885](https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10160885)<br>Focus to learn more<br>DOI(s) linking to related resources |"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "fixed-experiment",
                  "citationId": "mit-humanoid-rewards-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142",
                  "supportingPassage": "### III-ASystem Overview\n\nThe MIT Humanoid is an 18 degree-of-freedom robotic platform designed by the Biomimetic Robotics Lab \\[ [23](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx23 \"\")\\].\nFor learning running locomotion, we fix the arm joints at nominal angles and reduce control of the system to only the legs, a total of 10 degrees of freedom.\n\nThe policy network consists of a single neural network that outputs joint position targets 𝐚∈ℝ10\\\\mathbf{a}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{10} to the system, similar to prior work \\[ [24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx24 \"\"), [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx11 \"\")\\].\nThe torques are calculated as\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 𝝉=Kp​(𝐚−𝐪)+Kd​(𝐪˙),\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}=K\\_{p}(\\\\mathbf{a}-\\\\mathbf{q})+K\\_{d}(\\\\dot{\\\\mathbf{q}}), |  | (6) |\n\nwhere Kp=30K\\_{p}=30 Nm/rad and Kd=5K\\_{d}=5 Nms/rad are fixed proportional and damping gains respectively, and 𝐪\\\\mathbf{q} are the joint angles.\n\nThe observations 𝐬∈ℝ38\\\\mathbf{s}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{38} are listed in Table [I](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S2.T1 \"TABLE I ‣ II-C Potential-based Shaping ‣ II Preliminaries and Problem Statement ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\"), and are affected by uniformly sampled noise.\nThe phase ϕ\\\\phi is a simple clock with constant growth at one Hz, which we found helpful for the policy to settle into a periodic gait, although the final gaits observed are not limited to this frequency.\n\n### III-CShaping Rewards\n\nWe choose three shaping rewards commonly used as costs in the humanoid locomotion literature \\[ [25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx25 \"\"), [26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx26 \"\"), [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx12 \"\")\\]:\nwe regularize the _orientation_ (RoriR\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}}), _height_ (RhR\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}}), and _joints_ (RjR\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}}), with their respective reward terms defined in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nA nominal desired height zd​e​s=0.6z\\_{des}=0.6 m is a hand-chosen height target, g^x\\\\hat{g}\\_{x}, g^y\\\\hat{g}\\_{y} are the components of the gravity vector in the body frame, and qijq\\_{i}^{j} is the it​hi^{th} joint type on leg jj.\nThe subscript denotes the specific joint, with qaq\\_{a}, qpq\\_{p}, and qyq\\_{y} representing the abduction/adduction, pitch, and yaw joints specifically, and the superscript refers to the leg (left/right) the joint is part of.\nThe reward Rj​o​i​n​tR\\_{joint} serves to regularize the yaw joints about zero and encourage symmetry between the ab/ad and pitch joints of the legs.\nWe use squared-exponential functions to define our reward functions, as is common in RL literature \\[ [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx11 \"\"), [22](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx22 \"\"), [15](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx15 \"\"), [24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx24 \"\")\\].\n\nWe can put these ”direct” shaping rewards in their potential based forms trivially as\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Ps​(𝐬k,𝐬k+1)\\\\displaystyle P\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k},\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1}) | =γ​Rs​(𝐬k+1)−Rs​(𝐬k),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}), |  | (7) |\n\nfor some arbitrary shaping reward RsR\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}} and potential discounting γ\\\\gamma.\n\n### III-DImplementation Details\n\nThe locomotion policy is trained in the NVIDIA IsaacGym framework open-sourced by \\[ [21](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx21 \"\")\\] with the PPO-Clip algorithm \\[ [27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx27 \"\")\\] and the hyperparameters shown in Table [III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T3 \"TABLE III ‣ III-B Baseline Rewards ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nThe agents are trained on a computer equipped with an Intel i9-10850K processor and NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU.\nThe simulation is run at 1000 Hz, with a control frequency of 100 Hz. Each training run includes 4096 agents and is run for 1000 policy iterations, and we see convergence within around 30 minutes of wall-clock time."
