                  "supportingPassage": "TABLE II: Training rewards\n\n|     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Baseline | Weight | Function |\n| Linear velocity | 10 | exp(−\\|vx,y−cx,y\\|2/σx​y)\\\\exp(-\\\\lvert v\\_{x,y}-c\\_{x,y}\\\\rvert^{2}/\\\\sigma\\_{xy}) |\n| Angular velocity | 5 | exp(−(ωz−cω)2/σω)\\\\exp(-(\\\\omega\\_{z}-c\\_{\\\\omega})^{2}/\\\\sigma\\_{\\\\omega}) |\n| 1st order action rate | -1e-3 | \\|(qπk−qπk−1)/Δ​t\\|2\\\\lvert(q\\_{\\\\pi}^{k}-q\\_{\\\\pi}^{k-1})/\\\\Delta t\\\\rvert^{2} |\n| 2nd order action rate | -1e-4 | \\|(qπk−2​qπk−1+qπk−2)/Δ​t\\|2\\\\lvert(q\\_{\\\\pi}^{k}-2q\\_{\\\\pi}^{k-1}+q\\_{\\\\pi}^{k-2})/\\\\Delta t\\\\rvert^{2} |\n| Torques | -1e-4 | \\|𝝉\\|2\\\\lvert\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\\\rvert^{2} |\n| Torque limits | -0.01 | max⁡(\\|𝝉\\|−βτ​𝝉m​a​x,0)\\\\operatorname{max}(\\\\lvert\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\\\rvert-\\\\beta\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\_{max},0) |\n| Joint limits | -10 | max⁡(\\|𝝉\\|−βq​𝝉m​a​x,0)\\\\operatorname{max}(\\\\lvert\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\\\rvert-\\\\beta\\_{q}\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\_{max},0) |\n| Termination | -100 | {1,\\|𝐯b\\|≥10​ \\[m/s\\],1,\\|𝝎b\\|≥5​ \\[rad/s\\],1,g^x,g^y≥0.7,1,self−collision,0,otherwise.\\\\begin{cases}1,\\\\;\\\\lvert\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{b}\\\\rvert\\\\geq 10\\\\text{ \\[m/s\\]},\\\\\\<br>1,\\\\;\\\\lvert\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\omega}\\_{b}\\\\rvert\\\\geq 5\\\\text{ \\[rad/s\\]},\\\\\\<br>1,\\\\;\\\\hat{g}\\_{x},\\\\hat{g}\\_{y}\\\\geq 0.7,\\\\\\<br>1,\\\\;\\\\operatorname{self-collision},\\\\\\<br>0,\\\\;\\\\operatorname{otherwise}.\\\\end{cases} |\n| Direct Shaping | Weight | Function |\n| Orientation RoriR\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}} | 5.0 | exp(−(g^x2+g^y2)/σθ)\\\\exp({{-(\\\\hat{g}\\_{x}^{2}+\\\\hat{g}\\_{y}^{2})}/{\\\\sigma\\_{\\\\theta}}}) |\n| Height RhR\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}} | 2.0 | exp(−(zb−zd​e​s)2/σh)\\\\exp({{-(z\\_{b}-z\\_{des})^{2}}/{\\\\sigma\\_{h}}}) |\n| Joint regularization RjR\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}} | 1.0 | exp(−(qaL−qaR)2/σq)\\\\exp({{-(q\\_{a}^{L}-q\\_{a}^{R})^{2}}/{\\\\sigma\\_{q}}}) |\n|  |  | +exp(−(qpL−qpR)2/σq)+\\\\exp({{-(q\\_{p}^{L}-q\\_{p}^{R})^{2}}/{\\\\sigma\\_{q}}}) |\n|  |  | +exp(−(qyL)2/σq)+\\\\exp({{-(q\\_{y}^{L})^{2}}/{\\\\sigma\\_{q}}}) |\n|  |  | +exp(−(qyR)2/σq)+\\\\exp({{-(q\\_{y}^{R})^{2}}/{\\\\sigma\\_{q}}}) |\n| Potential Shaping | Weight | Function |\n| Orientation | 1.0 | γ​Rori​(𝐬k+1)−Rori​(𝐬k)\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}) |\n| Height | 1.0 | γ​Rh​(𝐬k+1)−Rh​(𝐬k)\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}) |\n| Joint regularization | 1.0 | γ​Rj​(𝐬k+1)−Rj​(𝐬k)\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}) |\n\n### III-BBaseline Rewards\n\nFor general locomotion, we define a set of baseline rewards as in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nThe first two, linear velocity and angular velocity tracking, are the only task-related rewards; all other terms are generic regularization terms to encourage smoothness, efficiency, and discourage joint limit violations.\n\nFor the baseline rewards, Δ​t{\\\\Delta t} is the controller timestep, σ=0.5{\\\\sigma=0.5} is a scaling parameter, βτ=0.8{\\\\beta\\_{\\\\tau}=0.8} and βq=0.9{\\\\beta\\_{q}=0.9} act as soft-stop limits to discourage reaching the joint and actuator constraints, and 𝝉m​a​x{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\tau}\\_{max}} and qm​a​xq\\_{max} are torque and joint limits of the system respectively.\n\n### III-CShaping Rewards\n\nWe choose three shaping rewards commonly used as costs in the humanoid locomotion literature \\[ [25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx25 \"\"), [26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx26 \"\"), [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx12 \"\")\\]:\nwe regularize the _orientation_ (RoriR\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}}), _height_ (RhR\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}}), and _joints_ (RjR\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}}), with their respective reward terms defined in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nA nominal desired height zd​e​s=0.6z\\_{des}=0.6 m is a hand-chosen height target, g^x\\\\hat{g}\\_{x}, g^y\\\\hat{g}\\_{y} are the components of the gravity vector in the body frame, and qijq\\_{i}^{j} is the it​hi^{th} joint type on leg jj.\nThe subscript denotes the specific joint, with qaq\\_{a}, qpq\\_{p}, and qyq\\_{y} representing the abduction/adduction, pitch, and yaw joints specifically, and the superscript refers to the leg (left/right) the joint is part of.\nThe reward Rj​o​i​n​tR\\_{joint} serves to regularize the yaw joints about zero and encourage symmetry between the ab/ad and pitch joints of the legs.\nWe use squared-exponential functions to define our reward functions, as is common in RL literature \\[ [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx11 \"\"), [22](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx22 \"\"), [15](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx15 \"\"), [24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx24 \"\")\\].\n\nWe can put these ”direct” shaping rewards in their potential based forms trivially as\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Ps​(𝐬k,𝐬k+1)\\\\displaystyle P\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k},\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1}) | =γ​Rs​(𝐬k+1)−Rs​(𝐬k),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}), |  | (7) |\n\nfor some arbitrary shaping reward RsR\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}} and potential discounting γ\\\\gamma.\n\n### III-DImplementation Details\n\nThe locomotion policy is trained in the NVIDIA IsaacGym framework open-sourced by \\[ [21](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx21 \"\")\\] with the PPO-Clip algorithm \\[ [27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx27 \"\")\\] and the hyperparameters shown in Table [III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T3 \"TABLE III ‣ III-B Baseline Rewards ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nThe agents are trained on a computer equipped with an Intel i9-10850K processor and NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU.\nThe simulation is run at 1000 Hz, with a control frequency of 100 Hz. Each training run includes 4096 agents and is run for 1000 policy iterations, and we see convergence within around 30 minutes of wall-clock time.\n\nWe first tune the baseline reward weights until reasonable running performance is achieved (see Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\")), then keep those weights fixed for all experiments.\nThe weights of the DRS and PBRS rewards are then tuned until a reasonable locomotion policy is found, with the weights set to the tuned values in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nCases are compared with accumulated baseline rewards and not the total rewards, such that the comparisons are not affected by the scaling of shaping rewards.\nWe also visually inspect policies to evaluate the resulting behavior for qualitative differences.\n\nTrajectories are collected over 24 timesteps, equating to roughly 0.2 s of simulation time, and the agents are subjected to randomized impulses, friction, and velocity commands during training."
