                  "partId": "practical-not-theorem",
                  "citationId": "mit-humanoid-rewards-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142",
                  "supportingPassage": "### IV-ADiscounting of Potential-Based Rewards\n\nWe find that in practice, using discounting for PBRS in ( [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S2.E4 \"In II-C Potential-based Shaping ‣ II Preliminaries and Problem Statement ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\")) can lead to learning instability \\[ [28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx28 \"\")\\].\nTo overcome this issue, we set γ=1\\\\gamma=1 in the calculation of ( [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S2.E4 \"In II-C Potential-based Shaping ‣ II Preliminaries and Problem Statement ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\")) (though not in calculating the advantage for PPO).\nWhile policy invariance is technically sacrificed by doing so, we find that training converges far more stably and quickly with this modification.\nWe do not discuss it further as our findings to this regard closely match those of \\[ [28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx28 \"\")\\], who studied in detail how the discount factor in potential-based rewards can affect both the magnitude and sign of the returned value.\nWe confirm their finding, as other studies on PBRS do not report any modifications of the discounting \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx9 \"\"), [20](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx20 \"\"), [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx7 \"\")\\], yet we found this to make a significant difference in the effectiveness of PBRS terms.\n\nIn practice, however, RL algorithms are affected by a myriad of hyperparameters, such as function approximation choices and exploration heuristics, that prevent the agent from converging to the true optimal policy in reasonable time.\nWe focus on comparing the performance of shaping with DRS and PBRS in a practical setting for training a humanoid robot to run.\n\n### III-CShaping Rewards\n\nWe choose three shaping rewards commonly used as costs in the humanoid locomotion literature \\[ [25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx25 \"\"), [26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx26 \"\"), [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx12 \"\")\\]:\nwe regularize the _orientation_ (RoriR\\_{\\\\mathrm{ori}}), _height_ (RhR\\_{\\\\mathrm{h}}), and _joints_ (RjR\\_{\\\\mathrm{j}}), with their respective reward terms defined in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ III-A System Overview ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nA nominal desired height zd​e​s=0.6z\\_{des}=0.6 m is a hand-chosen height target, g^x\\\\hat{g}\\_{x}, g^y\\\\hat{g}\\_{y} are the components of the gravity vector in the body frame, and qijq\\_{i}^{j} is the it​hi^{th} joint type on leg jj.\nThe subscript denotes the specific joint, with qaq\\_{a}, qpq\\_{p}, and qyq\\_{y} representing the abduction/adduction, pitch, and yaw joints specifically, and the superscript refers to the leg (left/right) the joint is part of.\nThe reward Rj​o​i​n​tR\\_{joint} serves to regularize the yaw joints about zero and encourage symmetry between the ab/ad and pitch joints of the legs.\nWe use squared-exponential functions to define our reward functions, as is common in RL literature \\[ [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx11 \"\"), [22](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx22 \"\"), [15](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx15 \"\"), [24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx24 \"\")\\].\n\nWe can put these ”direct” shaping rewards in their potential based forms trivially as\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Ps​(𝐬k,𝐬k+1)\\\\displaystyle P\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k},\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1}) | =γ​Rs​(𝐬k+1)−Rs​(𝐬k),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\gamma R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k+1})-R\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}}(\\\\mathbf{s}\\_{k}), |  | (7) |\n\nfor some arbitrary shaping reward RsR\\_{\\\\mathrm{s}} and potential discounting γ\\\\gamma.\n\n### III-DImplementation Details\n\nThe locomotion policy is trained in the NVIDIA IsaacGym framework open-sourced by \\[ [21](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx21 \"\")\\] with the PPO-Clip algorithm \\[ [27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx27 \"\")\\] and the hyperparameters shown in Table [III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S3.T3 \"TABLE III ‣ III-B Baseline Rewards ‣ III Humanoid Locomotion Case Study ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\").\nThe agents are trained on a computer equipped with an Intel i9-10850K processor and NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU.\nThe simulation is run at 1000 Hz, with a control frequency of 100 Hz. Each training run includes 4096 agents and is run for 1000 policy iterations, and we see convergence within around 30 minutes of wall-clock time.\n\n### IV-CSensitivity Analysis\n\nWe perform a sensitivity analysis of both DRS terms and PBRS terms by sweeping from 0.1 to 10 times the nominal weights.\nAs shown in Fig. [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#S4.F6 \"Fig. 6 ‣ IV-B Training Benchmark ‣ IV Results ‣ Benchmarking Potential Based Rewards for Learning Humanoid Locomotion\"), learning with PBRS terms is substantially more consistent across the weights and individual shaping rewards chosen.\nWhen the weights of the DRS terms are increased, the policy overfits to the specified reward and sacrifices the performance of the baseline rewards to do so.\nIn particular, rewarding a fixed, upright orientation is particularly detrimental to the baseline rewards.\nThis is unsurprising, given the significant banking and oscillations of the torso that naturally occur during running motions.\n\nBy placing shaping rewards in potential based form, the range of weights that can produce desirable behavior is much larger.\nThis significantly eases the burden of iterating on sets of reward weights for an acceptable policy.\n\n## V Conclusion and Outlook\n\nWe find that PBRS terms are beneficial for learning on high-dimensional, continuous systems such as legged robots; unlike previous studies \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx9 \"\"), [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx7 \"\"), [10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx10 \"\")\\], which have mostly focused on gridworld or low-dimensional systems, we find that the main benefit is not in accelerated convergence (which in our case is only marginal) but rather on ease of tuning.\nWe note that RL implementations in robotics often only use rewards of the form r⁡(sk)r(s\\_{k}); this type of reward can be trivially converted into PBRS form, and from our findings, we advocate using the PBRS form of rewards when possible.\n\nWhile we found that PBRS terms are relatively robust to weighting, we also note that these terms are implicitly scaled through the dynamics by the control timescale Δ​t\\\\Delta t.\nIn future work, we plan to more closely investigate this relationship, especially in the context of hierarchical RL.\nSince control timescales are a natural approach to choosing hierarchical levels (typically, higher levels in a hierarchy will reason on a longer horizon, with a larger Δ​t\\\\Delta t), it may be possible to automatically assign rewards for different tasks to different hierarchy levels based on their relative scaling.\n\nAnother promising avenue in the context of hierarchical RL is to use value functions as PBRS, as originally proposed by \\[ [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.10142#bib.bibx6 \"\")\\].\nAlthough finding a good approximation for a value function is often daunting, in a hierarchy of world models, it may be possible to solve a cascade of problems using a hierarchy of simplified world-models, and use the obtained value function to shape the reward of each successive stage.\n\nTABLE III: Training Environment Hyperparameters\n\n| Hyperparameter | Value |\n| Value loss coefficient | 1.0 |\n| Clipping ϵ\\\\epsilon | 0.2 |\n| Entropy coefficient | 0.1 |\n| Learning rate | 1e-5 (adaptive) |\n| Discount factor | 0.99 |\n| λ\\\\lambda | 0.95 |\n| Steps/env | 24 |\n| Policy network size | \\[256, 256, 256\\] |\n| Critic network size | \\[256, 256, 256\\] |\n| Activation | ELU |"
                }
              ],
