                  "supportingPassage": "{\"@context\":\"https:\\/\\/schema.org\",\"@graph\":[{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#article\",\"isPartOf\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/\"},\"author\":{\"name\":\"mrodin\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/#\\/schema\\/person\\/a4d76fffcd30181801eb34e2584caf90\"},\"headline\":\"Large Behavior Models and Atlas Find New Footing\",\"datePublished\":\"2025-08-20T15:53:19+00:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2025-08-20T18:47:30+00:00\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/\"},\"wordCount\":2293,\"image\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#primaryimage\"},\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/08\\/atlas-tri-blog.jpg\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\"},{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/\",\"name\":\"Large Behavior Models and Atlas Find New Footing | Boston Dynamics\",\"isPartOf\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/#website\"},\"primaryImageOfPage\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#primaryimage\"},\"image\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#primaryimage\"},\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/08\\/atlas-tri-blog.jpg\",\"datePublished\":\"2025-08-20T15:53:19+00:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2025-08-20T18:47:30+00:00\",\"breadcrumb\":{\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#breadcrumb\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\",\"potentialAction\":[{\"@type\":\"ReadAction\",\"target\":[\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/\"]}]},{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#primaryimage\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/08\\/atlas-tri-blog.jpg\",\"contentUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/wp-content\\/uploads\\/2025\\/08\\/atlas-tri-blog.jpg\",\"width\":1530,\"height\":1080,\"caption\":\"The Atlas folds a leg from a Spot robot\"},{\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/blog\\/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing\\/#breadcrumb\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Large Behavior Models and Atlas Find New Footing\"}]},{\"@type\":\"WebSite\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/#website\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/\",\"name\":\"Boston Dynamics\",\"description\":\"Changing your idea of what robots can do.\",\"potentialAction\":[{\"@type\":\"SearchAction\",\"target\":{\"@type\":\"EntryPoint\",\"urlTemplate\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/?s={search_term_string}\"},\"query-input\":{\"@type\":\"PropertyValueSpecification\",\"valueRequired\":true,\"valueName\":\"search_term_string\"}}],\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\"},{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/bostondynamics.com\\/#\\/schema\\/person\\/a4d76fffcd30181801eb34e2584caf90\",\"name\":\"mrodin\",\"image\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\",\"@id\":\"https:\\/\\/secure.gravatar.com\\/avatar\\/a4cacff82401ce3a50e4b86e9dd37c58c3f484002a6ff490dd53020254faba30?s=96&d=mm&r=g\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/secure.gravatar.com\\/avatar\\/a4cacff82401ce3a50e4b86e9dd37c58c3f484002a6ff490dd53020254faba30?s=96&d=mm&r=g\",\"contentUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/secure.gravatar.com\\/avatar\\/a4cacff82401ce3a50e4b86e9dd37c58c3f484002a6ff490dd53020254faba30?s=96&d=mm&r=g\",\"caption\":\"mrodin\"}}]}\n\nWe are excited to share some of our progress on developing [Large Behavior Models (LBMs)](https://toyotaresearchinstitute.github.io/lbm1/) for [Atlas](https://bostondynamics.com/atlas/) ®. This work is part of a [collaboration](https://pressroom.toyota.com/boston-dynamics-and-toyota-research-institute-announce-partnership-to-advance-robotics-research/) between AI research teams at Toyota Research Institute (TRI) and Boston Dynamics. We have been building end-to-end language-conditioned policies that enable Atlas to accomplish long-horizon manipulation tasks.\n\nThese policies are able to take full advantage of the capabilities of the humanoid form factor, including taking steps, precisely positioning its feet, crouching, shifting its center of mass, and avoiding self-collisions, all of which we have found to be critical to solving realistic mobile manipulation tasks.\n\nIn addition to using simulation to benchmark our policy and architecture choices, we also incorporate our simulation as a significant co-training data source for our multi-task and multi-embodiment policies that we deploy on the hardware.\n\nWhile we are encouraged by our results so far, there is still much work to be done. With our established baseline of tasks and performance, we will focus on scaling our data flywheel to increase throughput, quality, task diversity, and difficulty while also exploring new algorithmic ideas."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "architecture-inputs",
                  "citationId": "bd-atlas-lbm-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://bostondynamics.com/blog/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Building upon Toyota Research Institute’s [Large Behavior Models](https://toyotaresearchinstitute.github.io/lbm1/), which scale [Diffusion Policy](https://diffusion-policy.cs.columbia.edu/)-like architectures, our policy uses a 450M parameter Diffusion Transformer-based architecture together with a flow-matching objective. The policy is conditioned on proprioception, images, and also accepts a language prompt that specifies the objective to the robot. Image data comes in at 30 Hz and our network uses a history of observations to predict an action-chunk of length 48 (corresponding to 1.6 seconds) where generally 24 actions (0.8 seconds when running at 1x speed) are executed each time policy inference is run."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "demonstration-training",
                  "citationId": "bd-atlas-lbm-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://bostondynamics.com/blog/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing/",
                  "supportingPassage": "1. Collect embodied behavior data using teleoperation on both the real-robot hardware and in simulation.\n2. Process, annotate, and curate data so that we can easily incorporate it into a machine learning pipeline.\n3. Train a neural-network policy using _all_ of the data across all tasks.\n4. Evaluate the policy using a test suite of tasks.\n\nThese policies were trained on data that the team continuously collected and iterated upon, where high quality demonstrations were a critical part of getting successful policies. We heavily relied upon our quality assurance tooling that allowed us to review, filter, and provide feedback on the data collected."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "control-versus-inference",
                  "citationId": "bd-atlas-lbm-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://bostondynamics.com/blog/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing/",
                  "supportingPassage": "![Our policy maps inputs consisting of images, proprioception and language prompts to actions that control the full Atlas robot at 30Hz. We leverage a diffusion transformer together with a flow matching loss to train our model.](https://bostondynamics.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Atlas-TRI-Blog-Post-Policy-Architecture-Diagram-v2e-1024x372.png)Our policy maps inputs consisting of images, proprioception, and language prompts to actions that control the full Atlas robot at 30Hz. We leverage a diffusion transformer together with a flow matching loss to train our model.\n\nBuilding upon Toyota Research Institute’s [Large Behavior Models](https://toyotaresearchinstitute.github.io/lbm1/), which scale [Diffusion Policy](https://diffusion-policy.cs.columbia.edu/)-like architectures, our policy uses a 450M parameter Diffusion Transformer-based architecture together with a flow-matching objective. The policy is conditioned on proprioception, images, and also accepts a language prompt that specifies the objective to the robot. Image data comes in at 30 Hz and our network uses a history of observations to predict an action-chunk of length 48 (corresponding to 1.6 seconds) where generally 24 actions (0.8 seconds when running at 1x speed) are executed each time policy inference is run.\n\nOne notable feature of our policies is that we can speed up the execution at inference time without requiring any training time changes. Specifically, since our policies predict a trajectory of future actions along with the time at which those actions should be taken, we can adjust this timing to control execution speed. In the video shown below we compare the policy being rolled out at 1x (i.e. the speed at which this task was done during data collection) and also at 2x and 3x speed. In general, we found that we were able to speed up policies by 1.5x-2x without significantly affecting policy performance on both the MTS and full Atlas platforms. While the task dynamics can sometimes preclude this kind of inference-time speedup, it does suggest that, in some cases, we can exceed the speed limits of human teleoperation."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "mpc-interface",
                  "citationId": "bd-atlas-lbm-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://bostondynamics.com/blog/large-behavior-models-atlas-find-new-footing/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Controlling the robot in a fluid, dynamic, and dexterous manner is crucial, and we have invested heavily in our teleoperation system to address these needs. The system is built on Boston Dynamics’s MPC system, which has previously been deployed for use-cases ranging from [parkour](https://bostondynamics.com/blog/flipping-the-script-with-atlas/), [dance](https://youtu.be/fn3KWM1kuAw?feature=shared), and both [practical](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/SFKM-Rxiqzg) and [impractical manipulation](https://bostondynamics.com/blog/picking-up-momentum/). This control system allows us to perform precise manipulation while maintaining balance and avoiding self-collisions, enabling us to push the boundaries of what we can do with the Atlas hardware.\n\nOur neural network policies use the same control interface to the robot as the teleoperation system, which made it easy to reuse model architectures we had developed previously (for policies that didn’t involve locomotion), simply by augmenting the action representation.\n\nThe policy’s observation space for Atlas consists of the images from the robot’s head-mounted cameras along with proprioception. The action space includes the joint positions for the left and right grippers, neck yaw, torso pose, left and right hand pose, and the left and right foot poses."
                }
              ],
