            "note": "Correction reviewed 2026-09-12T19:37:38.912Z by agent:cf70d662/integrator, not a new retrieval. Table 1 gives Ours (rotation) 98.9% on 11,313 filtered AMASS training sequences, distinct from Ours-kp and held-out sets. Section 4 defines failure as mean body-joint deviation over 0.5 m at any time; all methods are simulated. No universal tracking or hardware result is inferred. Section 3.2 starts with the entire dataset, freezes primitives, and successively selects failed subsets; it adds a recovery primitive and trains a multiplicative composer. This is not a growing motion dataset, nor a separate controller for every clip. Sections 3.3/4.1 and Appendix D describe webcam and MDM-generated reference tracking in simulated avatars, with reset-free demonstrations. Highly dynamic motions, jitter, frame-rate mismatch, camera assumptions and recovery jolts are counterexamples to unrestricted robustness. Full original/current cells: humanoid-tracking-original-history-20260912. Source provenance: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-humanoid-tracking-integration-20260912/source-proof.json. No whole-P1/article, venue, licensing, unselected Stat or independent acceptance credit.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "humanoid-tracking-phc-20260908",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "evaluation",
                  "citationId": "phc-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 3.3 Connecting with Motion Estimators\n\nOur PHC is task-agnostic as it only requires the next time-step reference pose 𝒒~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{q}}\\_{t}} or the keypoint 𝒑~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{p}}\\_{t}} for motion tracking. Thus, we can use any off-the-shelf video-based human pose estimator or generator compatible with the SMPL kinematic structure. For driving simulated avatars from videos, we employ HybrIK \\[ [19](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib19 \"\")\\] and MeTRAbs \\[ [41](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib41 \"\"), [40](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib40 \"\")\\], both of which estimate in the metric space with the important distinction that HybrIK outputs joint rotation 𝜽~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}} while MeTRAbs only outputs 3D keypoints 𝒑~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{p}}\\_{t}}. For language-based motion generation, we use the Motion Diffusion Model (MDM) \\[ [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib43 \"\")\\]. MDM generates disjoint motion sequences based on prompts, and we use our controller’s recovery ability to achieve in-betweening.\n\nTable 1: Quantitative results on imitating MoCap motion sequences (\\* indicates removing sequences containing human-object interaction). AMASS-Train\\*, AMASS-Test\\*, and H36M-Motion\\* contains 11313, 140, and 140 high-quality MoCap sequences, respectively.\n\n|  | AMASS-Train\\* | AMASS-Test\\* | H36M-Motion\\* |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Method | RFC | Succ↑\\\\text{Succ}\\\\uparrow | Eg-mpjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Empjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Eacc↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{acc}}\\\\downarrow | Evel↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{vel}}\\\\downarrow | Succ↑\\\\text{Succ}\\\\uparrow | Eg-mpjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Empjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Eacc↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{acc}}\\\\downarrow | Evel↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{vel}}\\\\downarrow | Succ↑\\\\text{Succ}\\\\uparrow | Eg-mpjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Empjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Eacc↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{acc}}\\\\downarrow | Evel↓\\\\text{E}\\_{\\\\text{vel}}\\\\downarrow |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| UHC | ✓ | 97.0 % | 36.4 | 25.1 | 4.4 | 5.9 | 96.4 % | 50.0 | 31.2 | 9.7 | 12.1 | 87.0% | 59.7 | 35.4 | 4.9 | 7.4 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| UHC | ✗ | 84.5 % | 62.7 | 39.6 | 10.9 | 10.9 | 62.6% | 58.2 | 98.1 | 22.8 | 21.9 | 23.6% | 133.14 | 67.4 | 14.9 | 17.2 |\n| Ours | ✗ | 98.9 % | 37.5 | 26.9 | 3.3 | 4.9 | 96.4% | 47.4 | 30.9 | 6.8 | 9.1 | 92.9% | 50.3 | 33.3 | 3.7 | 5.5 |\n| Ours-kp | ✗ | 98.7% | 40.7 | 32.3 | 3.5 | 5.5 | 97.1% | 53.1 | 39.5 | 7.5 | 10.4 | 95.7% | 49.5 | 39.2 | 3.7 | 5.8 |\n\nTable 2: Motion imitation on noisy motion. We use HybrIK\\[ [19](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib19 \"\")\\] to estimate the joint rotations 𝜽~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}} and uses MeTRAbs \\[ [41](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib41 \"\")\\] for global 3D keypoints 𝒑~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{p}}\\_{t}}. HybrIK + MeTRAbs (root): using joint rotations 𝜽~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}} from HybrIK and root position 𝒑~t0{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{p}}\\_{t}}^{0} from MeTRAbs. MeTRAbs (all keypoints): using all keypoints 𝒑~t{\\\\bm{\\\\tilde{p}}\\_{t}} from MeTRAbs, only applicabile to our keypoint-based controller.\n\n|  | H36M-Test-Video\\* |\n| --- | --- |\n| Method | RFC | Pose Estimate | Succ↑\\\\text{Succ}\\\\uparrow | Eg-mpjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow | Empjpe↓E\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}\\\\downarrow |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| UHC | ✓ | HybrIK + MeTRAbs (root) | 58.1% | 75.5 | 49.3 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| UHC | ✗ | HybrIK + MeTRAbs (root) | 18.1% | 126.1 | 67.1 |\n| Ours | ✗ | HybrIK + MeTRAbs (root) | 88.7% | 55.4 | 34.7 |\n| Ours-kp | ✗ | HybrIK + MeTRAbs (root) | 90.0% | 55.8 | 41.0 |\n| Ours-kp | ✗ | MeTRAbs (all keypoints) | 91.9% | 55.7 | 41.1 |\n\n#### Implementation Details\n\nWe uses four primitives (including fail-state recovery) for all our evaluations. PHC can be trained on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU; it takes around a week to train all primitives and the composer. Once trained, the composite policy runs at >30>30 FPS. Physics simulation is carried out in NVIDIA’s Isaac Gym \\[ [26](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib26 \"\")\\]. The control policy is run at 30 Hz, while simulation runs at 60 Hz. For evaluation, we do not consider body shape variation and use the mean SMPL body shape.\n\n#### Datasets\n\nPHC is trained on the training split of the AMASS \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib25 \"\")\\] dataset. We follow UHC \\[ [22](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib22 \"\")\\] and remove sequences that are noisy or involve interactions of human objects, resulting in 11313 high-quality training sequences and 140 test sequences. To evaluate our policy’s ability to handle unseen MoCap sequences and noisy pose estimate from pose estimation methods, we use the popular H36M dataset \\[ [15](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib15 \"\")\\]. From H36M, we derive two subsets H36M-Motion\\* and H36M-Test-Video\\*. H36M-Motion\\* contains 140 high-quality MoCap sequences from the entire H36M dataset. H36M-Test-Video\\* contains 160 sequences of noisy poses estimated from videos in the H36M test split (since SOTA pose estimation methods are trained on H36M’s training split). \\* indicates the removal of sequences containing human-chair interaction.\n\n#### Metrics\n\nWe use a series of pose-based and physics-based metrics to evaluate our motion imitation performance. We report the success rate (Succ) as in UHC \\[ [22](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib22 \"\")\\], deeming imitation unsuccessful when, at any point during imitation, the body joints are on average >0.5​m>0.5m from the reference motion. Succ measures whether the humanoid can track the reference motion without losing balance or significantly lags behind. We also report the root-relative mean per-joint position error (MPJPE) EmpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}} and the global MPJPE Eg-mpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{g-mpjpe}} (in mm), measuring our imitator’s ability to imitate the reference motion both locally (root-relative) and globally. To show physical realism, we also compare acceleration EaccE\\_{\\\\text{acc}} (mm/frame2) and velocity EvelE\\_{\\\\text{vel}} (mm/frame) difference between simulated and MoCap motion. All the baseline and our methods are physically simulated, so we do not report any foot sliding or penetration."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "capacity",
                  "citationId": "phc-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 3.2 Progressive Multiplicative Control Policy\n\nAs training continues, we notice that the performance of the model plateaus as it forgets older sequences when learning new ones. Hard negative mining alleviates the problem to a certain extent, yet suffers from the same issue. Introducing new tasks, such as fail-state recovery, may further degrade imitation performance due to catastrophic forgetting. These effects are more concretely categorized in the Appendix (App. C). Thus, we propose a progressive multiplicative control policy (PMCP), which allocates new subnetworks (primitives 𝓟\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}) to learn harder sequences.\n\n#### Progressive Neural Networks (PNN)\n\nA PNN \\[ [36](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib36 \"\")\\] starts with a single primitive network 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} trained on the full dataset 𝑸^\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}. Once 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} is trained to convergence on the entire motion dataset 𝑸^\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}} using the imitation task, we create a subset of hard motions by evaluating 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} on 𝑸^\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}. We define convergence as the success rate on 𝑸^hard(k)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k)} no longer increases. The sequences that 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} fails on is formed as 𝑸^hard(1)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(1)}. We then freeze the parameters of 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} and create a new primitive 𝓟(2)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(2)} (randomly initialized) along with lateral connections that connect each layer of 𝓟(1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)} to 𝓟(2)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(2)}. For more information about PNN, please refer to our supplementary material. During training, we construct each 𝑸^hard(k)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k)} by selecting the failed sequences from the previous step 𝑸^hard(k−1)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k-1)}, resulting in a smaller and smaller hard subset: 𝑸^hard(k)⊆𝑸^hard(k−1)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k)}\\\\subseteq\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k-1)}. In this way, we ensure that each newly initiated primitive 𝓟(k)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k)} is responsible for learning a new and harder subset of motion sequences, as can be seen in Fig. [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#S3.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Goal Conditioned Motion Imitation with Adversarial Motion Prior ‣ 3 Method ‣ Perpetual Humanoid Control for Real-time Simulated Avatars\"). Notice that this is different from hard-negative mining in UHC \\[ [22](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib22 \"\")\\], as we initialize a new primitive 𝓟(k+1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k+1)} to train. Since the original PNN is proposed to solve completely new tasks (such as different Atari games), a lateral connection mechanism is proposed to allow later tasks to choose between reuse, modify, or discard prior experiences. However, mimicking human motion is highly correlated, where fitting to harder sequences 𝑸^hard(k)\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}\\_{\\\\text{hard}}^{(k)} can effectively draw experiences from previous motor control experiences. Thus, we also consider a variant of PNN where there are no lateral connections, but the new primitives are initialized from the weights of the prior layer. This weight sharing scheme is similar to fine-tuning on the harder motion sequences using a new primitive 𝓟(k+1)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k+1)} and preserve 𝓟(k)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k)}’s ability to imitate learned sequences.\n\n#### Fail-state Recovery\n\nIn addition to learning harder sequences, we also learn new tasks, such as recovering from fail-state. We define three types of fail-state: 1) fallen on the ground; 2) faraway from the reference motion (>0.5​m>0.5m); 3) their combination: fallen and faraway. In these situations, the humanoid should get up from the ground, approach the reference motion in a natural way, and resume motion imitation. For this new task, we initialize a primitive 𝓟(F)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(F)} at the end of the primitive stack. 𝓟(F)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(F)} shares the same input and output space as 𝓟(1)⋯𝓟(k)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)}\\\\cdots\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k)}, but since the reference motion does not provide useful information about fail-state recovery (the humanoid should not attempt to imitate the reference motion when lying on the ground), we modify the state space during fail-state recovery to remove all information about the reference motion except the root. For the reference joint rotation 𝜽^t=\\[𝜽^t0,𝜽^t1,⋯𝜽^tJ\\]{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}}=\\[\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{0},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{1},\\\\cdots\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{J}\\] where 𝜽^ti\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{i} corresponds to the ithi^{\\\\text{th}} joint, we construct 𝜽^t′=\\[𝜽^t0,𝜽t1,⋯𝜽tj\\]{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}}^{\\\\prime}=\\[\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{0},\\\\bm{{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{1},\\\\cdots\\\\bm{{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{j}\\] where all joint rotations except the root are replaced with simulated values (without ⋅^\\\\widehat{\\\\cdot}). This amounts to setting the non-root joint goals to be identity when computing the goal states: 𝒔tg-Fail≜(𝜽^t′⊖𝜽t,𝒑^t′−𝒑t,𝒗^t′−𝒗t,𝝎^t′−𝝎t,𝜽^t′,𝒑^t′)\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g-Fail}}\\_{t}\\\\triangleq(\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}\\\\ominus\\\\bm{\\\\theta}\\_{t},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{p}\\_{t},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{v}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{v}\\_{t},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\omega}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{\\\\omega}\\_{t},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{\\\\theta}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime},\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}). 𝒔tg-Fail\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g-Fail}}\\_{t} thus collapse from an imitation objective to a point-goal \\[ [49](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib49 \"\")\\] objective where the only information provided is the relative position and orientation of the target root. When the reference root is too far (>5​m>5m), we normalize 𝒑^t′−𝒑t\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{p}\\_{t} as 5×(𝒑^t′−𝒑t)‖𝒑^t′−𝒑t‖2\\\\frac{5\\\\times(\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{p}\\_{t})}{\\\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}\\_{t}^{\\\\prime}-\\\\bm{p}\\_{t}\\\\\\|\\_{2}} to clamp the goal position. Once the humanoid is close enough ( _e.g_.<0.5​m<0.5m ), the goal will switch back to full-motion imitation:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 𝒔tg={𝒔tg‖𝒑^t0−𝒑t0‖2≤0.5𝒔tg-Failotherwise.\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g}}\\_{t}=\\\\begin{cases}\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g}}\\_{t}&\\\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\hat{p}}^{0}\\_{t}-\\\\bm{p}^{0}\\_{t}\\\\\\|\\_{2}\\\\leq 0.5\\\\\\<br>\\\\bm{s}^{\\\\text{g-Fail}}\\_{t}&\\\\text{otherwise}.\\\\\\<br>\\\\end{cases} |  | (3) |\n\nTo create fallen states, we follow ASE \\[ [34](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib34 \"\")\\] and randomly drop the humanoid on the ground at the beginning of the episode. The faraway state can be created by initializing the humanoid 2 ∼\\\\sim 5 meters from the reference motion. The reward for fail-state recovery consists of the AMP reward rtampr^{\\\\text{amp}}\\_{t}, point-goal reward rtg-pointr^{\\\\text{g-point}}\\_{t}, and energy penality rtenergyr^{\\\\text{energy}}\\_{t}, calculated by the reward function 𝓡recover\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{R}}^{\\\\text{recover}}:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | rtg-recover=𝓡recover​(𝒔t,𝒒^t)=0.5​rtg-point+0.5​rtamp+0.1​rtenergy,r^{\\\\text{g-recover}}\\_{t}=\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{R}}^{\\\\text{recover}}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}},{\\\\bm{\\\\hat{q}}\\_{t}})=0.5r^{\\\\text{g-point}}\\_{t}+0.5r^{\\\\text{amp}}\\_{t}+0.1r^{\\\\text{energy}}\\_{t}, |  | (4) |\n\nThe point-goal reward is formulated as rtg-point=(dt−1−dt)r^{\\\\text{g-point}}\\_{t}=\\\\left(d\\_{t-1}-d\\_{t}\\\\right) where dtd\\_{t} is the distance between the root reference and simulated root at the time step tt\\[ [49](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib49 \"\")\\]. For training 𝓟(F)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(F)}, we use a handpicked subset of the AMASS dataset named 𝑸loco\\\\bm{Q}^{\\\\text{loco}} where it contains mainly walking and running sequences. Learning using only 𝑸loco\\\\bm{Q}^{\\\\text{loco}} coaxes the discriminator 𝓓\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{D}} and the AMP reward rtampr^{\\\\text{amp}}\\_{t} to bias toward simple locomotion such as walking and running. We do not initialize a new value function and discriminator while training the primitives and continuously fine-tune the existing ones.\n\n#### Multiplicative Control\n\nOnce each primitive has been learned, we obtain {𝓟(1)⋯𝓟(K),𝓟(F)}\\\\{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(1)}\\\\cdots\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(K)},\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(F)}\\\\}, with each primitive capable of imitating a subset of the dataset 𝑸^\\\\bm{\\\\hat{Q}}. In Progressive Networks \\[ [36](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib36 \"\")\\], task switching is performed manually. In motion imitation, however, the boundary between hard and easy sequences is blurred. Thus, we utilize Multiplicative Control Policy (MCP) \\[ [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#bib.bib33 \"\")\\] and train an additional composer 𝓒\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}} to dynamically combine the learned primitives. Essentially, we use the pretrained primitives as a informed search space for the composer 𝓒\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}, and 𝓒\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}} only needs to select which primitives to activate for imitation. Specifically, our composer 𝓒(𝒘t1:K+1\\|𝒔t)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}(\\\\bm{w}^{1:K+1}\\_{t}\\|{\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}}) consumes the same input as the primitives and outputs a weight vector 𝒘1:K+1t∈ℝk+1\\\\bm{w}^{1:K+1}\\_{t}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{k+1} to activate the primitives. Combining our composer and primitives, we have the PHC’s output distribution:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | πPHC​(𝒂t∣𝒔t)=1𝓒⁡(𝒔t)​∏ik𝓟(i)​(𝒂t(i)∣𝒔t)𝓒⁡(𝒔t),𝓒⁡(𝒔t)≥0.\\\\scriptsize{\\\\pi\\_{\\\\text{PHC}}}(\\\\bm{a}\\_{t}\\\\mid{\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}\\\\prod\\_{i}^{k}\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(i)}(\\\\bm{a}^{(i)}\\_{t}\\\\mid{\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})^{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})},\\\\hskip 8.19447pt\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})\\\\geq 0. |  | (5) |\n\nAs each 𝓟(k)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{(k)} is an independent Gaussian, the action distribution:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 𝒩⁡(1∑lk𝓒i​(𝒔t)σlj​(𝒔t)​∑ik𝓒i​(𝒔t)σij​(𝒔t)​μij​(𝒔t),σj​(𝒔t)=(∑ik𝓒i​(𝒔t)σij​(𝒔t))−1),\\\\scriptsize\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sum\\_{l}^{k}\\\\frac{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}\\_{i}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}{\\\\sigma\\_{l}^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}}\\\\sum\\_{i}^{k}\\\\frac{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}\\_{i}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}{\\\\sigma\\_{i}^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}\\\\mu\\_{i}^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}}),\\\\sigma^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})=\\\\left(\\\\sum\\_{i}^{k}\\\\frac{\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{C}}\\_{i}(\\\\bm{s}\\_{t})}{\\\\sigma\\_{i}^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}})}\\\\right)^{-1}\\\\right), |  | (6) |\n\nwhere μij​(𝒔t)\\\\mu\\_{i}^{j}({\\\\bm{s}\\_{t}}) corresponds to the 𝓟(i)\\\\bm{\\\\mathcal{P}}^{\\\\text{(i)}}’s jthj^{\\\\text{th}} action dimension. Unlike a Mixture of Expert policies that only activates one at a time (top-1 MOE), MCP combines the actors’ distribution and activates all actors at the same (similar to top-inf MOE). Unlike MCP, we progressively train our primitives and make the composer and actor share the same input space. Since primitives are independently trained for different harder sequences, we observe that the composite policy sees a significant boost in performance. During composer training, we interleave fail-state recovery training. The training process is described in Alg. [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#algorithm1 \"In Progressive Neural Networks (PNN) ‣ 3.2 Progressive Multiplicative Control Policy ‣ 3 Method ‣ Perpetual Humanoid Control for Real-time Simulated Avatars\") and Fig. [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2305.06456#S3.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Goal Conditioned Motion Imitation with Adversarial Motion Prior ‣ 3 Method ‣ Perpetual Humanoid Control for Real-time Simulated Avatars\")."
