                  "supportingPassage": "## 3 Methods\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851v3/overview-v4.png)Figure 1: An overview of PBHC that includes three core components: (a) motion extraction from videos and multi-steps motion processing, (b) adaptive motion tracking based on the optimal tracking factor, (c) the RL training framework and sim-to-real deployment.\n\nAn overview of PBHC is illustrated in Fig. [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\").\nFirst, raw human videos are processed by a Human Motion Recovery (HMR) model to produce SMPL-format motion sequences. These sequences are filtered via physics-based metrics and corrected using contact masks. The refined motions are then retargeted to the G1 robot. Finally, each resulting trajectory serves as reference motion for training a separate RL policy, which is then deployed on the real G1 robot. In the following, we detail the\nmotion processing pipeline (§ [3.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.SS1 \"3.1 Motion Processing Pipeline ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")), adaptive motion tracking module (§ [3.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.SS2 \"3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")) and RL framework (§ [3.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.SS3 \"3.3 RL Training Framework ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")).\n\n### 3.1 Motion Processing Pipeline\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "exponential-tolerance",
                  "citationId": "kungfubot-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851",
                  "supportingPassage": "#### 3.2.1 Exponential Form Tracking Reward\n\nThe reward function in PBHC, detailed in Appendix [C.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#A3.SS2 \"C.2 Reward Design ‣ Appendix C Algorithm Design ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\"),\ncomprises two components: task-specific rewards, which enforce accurate tracking of reference motions, and regularization rewards, which promote overall stability and smoothness.\n\nThe task-specific rewards include terms for aligning joint states, rigid body state, and foot contact mask. These rewards, except the foot contact tracking term, follow the exponential form as:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | r(x)=exp(−x/σ),r(x)=\\\\exp(-x/\\\\sigma), |  | (4) |\n\nwhere xx represents the tracking error, typically measured as the mean squared error (MSE) of quantities such as joint angles, while σ\\\\sigma controls the tolerance of the error, referred to as the _tracking factor_. This exponential form is preferred over the negative error form because it is bounded, helps stabilize the training process, and provides a more intuitive approach for reward weighting.\n\nFigure 2: Illustration of the effect of tracking factor σ\\\\sigma on the reward value.\n\nIntuitively, when σ\\\\sigma is much larger than the typical range of xx, the reward remains close to 1 and becomes insensitive to changes in xx, while an overly small σ\\\\sigma causes the reward to approach 0 and also reduces its sensitivity, highlighting the importance of choosing σ\\\\sigma appropriately to enhance responsiveness and hence tracking precision. This intuition is illustrated in Fig. [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.1 Exponential Form Tracking Reward ‣ 3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\").\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "adaptive-update",
                  "citationId": "kungfubot-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851",
                  "supportingPassage": "#### 3.2.3 Adaptive Mechanism\n\nFigure 3: Closed-loop adjustment of tracking factor in the proposed adaptive mechanism.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851v3/left_hand_traj_v3-punch1.png)Figure 4: Example of the right hand yy-position for ‘Horse-stance punch’. The adaptive σ\\\\sigma can progressively improve the tracking precision. σpos​\\_​vr\\\\sigma\\_{\\\\mathrm{pos\\\\\\_vr}} is used for tracking the head and hands.\n\nWhile Eq. ( [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.E7 \"In 3.2.2 Optimal Tracking Factor ‣ 3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")) provides a theoretical guidance for determining the tracking factor, the coupling between σ∗\\\\sigma^{\\*} and 𝒙∗\\\\bm{x}^{\\*} creates a circular dependency that prevents direct computation. Additionally, due to the varying quality and complexity of reference motion data, selecting a single, fixed value for the tracking factor that works for all motion scenarios is impractical. To resolve this, we design an adaptive mechanism that dynamically adjusts σ\\\\sigma during training through a feedback loop between error estimation and tracking factor adaptation.\n\nIn this mechanism, we maintain an Exponential Moving Average (EMA) x^\\\\hat{x} of the instantaneous tracking error over environment steps. This EMA serves as an online estimate of the expected tracking error under the current policy, and during training this value should approach the average optimal tracking error\n(∑i=1Nxi∗)/N\\\\left(\\\\sum\\\\nolimits\\_{i=1}^{N}x^{\\*}\\_{i}\\\\right)/N\nunder the current factor σ\\\\sigma.\nAt each step, PBHC updates σ\\\\sigma to the current value of x^\\\\hat{x}, creating a feedback loop where reductions in tracking error lead to tightening of σ\\\\sigma. This closed-loop process drives further policy refinement, and as the tracking error decreases, the system converges to an optimal value of σ\\\\sigma that asymptotically solves Eq. ( [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#A1.E9 \"In Appendix A Derivation of Optimal Tracking Sigma ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")), as illustrated in Fig. [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.F4 \"Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Adaptive Mechanism ‣ 3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\").\n\nTo ensure stability during training, we constrain σ\\\\sigma to be non-increasing and initialize it with a relatively large value, σinit\\\\sigma^{\\\\mathrm{init}}. The update rule is given by Eq. ( [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.E8 \"In 3.2.3 Adaptive Mechanism ‣ 3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\")). As shown in Fig. [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.12851#S3.F4 \"Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Adaptive Mechanism ‣ 3.2 Adaptive Motion Tracking ‣ 3 Methods ‣ KungfuBot: Physics-Based Humanoid Whole-Body Control for Learning Highly-Dynamic Skills\"), this adaptive mechanism allows the policy to progressively improve its tracking precision during training.\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | σ←min⁡(σ,x^).\\\\sigma\\\\leftarrow\\\\min(\\\\sigma,\\\\hat{x}). |  | (8) |\n\n### 3.3 RL Training Framework\n"
