                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "\n###### Abstract\n\nThe ability to track general whole-body motions in the real world is a useful way to build general-purpose humanoid robots.\nHowever, achieving this can be challenging due to the temporal and kinematic diversity of the motions, the policy’s capability, and the difficulty of coordination of the upper and lower bodies.\nTo address these issues, we propose GMT, a general and scalable motion-tracking framework that trains a single unified policy to enable humanoid robots to track diverse motions in the real world. GMT is built upon two core components: an Adaptive Sampling strategy and a Motion Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture.\nThe Adaptive Sampling automatically balances easy and difficult motions during training. The MoE ensures better specialization of different regions of the motion manifold.\nWe show through extensive experiments in both simulation and the real world the effectiveness of GMT, achieving state-of-the-art performance across a broad spectrum of motions using a unified general policy. Videos and additional information can be found at [gmt-humanoid.github.io](https://gmt-humanoid.github.io/ \"\").\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n|  |\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770v2/teaser-v4.png)Figure 1: We deploy the general unified motion tracking policy on a medium-sized humanoid robot.\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "adaptive-sampling",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "\nLeveraging human motion data to develop human-like whole-body behaviors for robots has been extensively studied in character animation. Previous works have achieved high-quality and general motion tracking on simulated characters \\[ [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib1 \"\"), [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib3 \"\"), [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib5 \"\"), [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib4 \"\")\\], and diverse motion skills for various downstream tasks \\[ [30](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib30 \"\"), [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib2 \"\"), [31](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib31 \"\"), [32](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib32 \"\"), [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib33 \"\")\\]. However, due to the partial observability in the real world, developing such whole-body controllers for real robots \\[ [10](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib10 \"\"), [34](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib34 \"\"), [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib9 \"\"), [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib8 \"\"), [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib7 \"\"), [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib11 \"\"), [35](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib35 \"\"), [36](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib36 \"\"), [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib37 \"\")\\] can be challenging. For developing a unified general whole-body motion tracking controller, some works decouple upper-body and lower-body control to compromise between expressiveness and balance \\[ [10](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib10 \"\"), [34](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib34 \"\")\\]. HumanPlus \\[ [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib9 \"\")\\] and OmniH2O \\[ [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib8 \"\")\\] then successfully enabled whole-body motion imitation on a full-sized robot, but with unnatural movements in the lower body. ExBody2 \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib7 \"\")\\] achieved better whole-body tracking performance but with several separate specialist policies. VMP \\[ [35](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib35 \"\")\\] demonstrated high-fidelity reproduction of a wide range of skills on the real robot, but its dependency on the mocap system during deployment limits its applicability in the wild.\n\n## 3 Learning General Motion Tracking Controllers\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770v2/method_v3.png)Figure 3: An overview of GMT. Here 𝒈t{\\\\bm{g}}\\_{t} denotes the motion target frame, 𝒐t{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t} denotes proprioceptive observation, and 𝒆t{\\\\bm{e}}\\_{t} denotes privileged information.\n\nIn this section, we introduce GMT, a framework for developing general and high-quality motion tracking controllers for real-world humanoid robots. GMT follows the two-stage teacher-student training framework similar to prior works \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib7 \"\"), [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib8 \"\")\\], where a privileged teacher policy is first trained using PPO \\[ [38](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib38 \"\")\\], and a student policy is then trained using DAgger\\[ [39](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib39 \"\")\\] by imitating the output of the teacher policy.\nFor the following part, we first introduce the core components of our method, which are Adaptive Sampling strategy and Motion MoE architecture. Then we present some key designs that are also crucial for policy learning, including motion input designs and the dataset curation process.\n\n### 3.1 Adaptive Sampling\n\nAs illustrated in Fig. [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#S1.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GMT: General Motion Tracking for Humanoid Whole-Body Control\"), large motion datasets like AMASS \\[ [6](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib6 \"\")\\] exhibit significant category unbalance. This unbalance can substantially hinder the learning of less frequent and more complex motions. Moreover, longer sequences within such datasets are often composite motions, consisting of a series of skills that include both basic and challenging segments. Using previous sampling strategies \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib5 \"\"), [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib3 \"\")\\], when a policy fails on harder segments, it is still trained on the entire motion sequence — often dominated by easier parts — which results in a low effective sampling rate for the difficult portions.\n\nTo address these issues, we introduce Adaptive Sampling, which consists of two key components:\n\n- •\n\n\nRandom Clipping: Motions longer than 1010 seconds are clipped into several sub-clips, each with a maximum length of 10 seconds. To prevent artifacts at clip transitions, we apply randomized clipping by introducing a random offset of up to 22 seconds. Additionally, all motions are re-clipped periodically during training to further diversify the sampled sub-clips;\n\n- •\n\n\nTracking Performance-based Probabilities: During training, we log the completion level cic\\_{i} of each motion, and terminate episodes when the tracking error exceeds EiE\\_{i}. cic\\_{i} starts at 1010 and decays by multiplying 0.990.99 each time the motion is completed, the minimum value is 11. And Ei=0.25​exp⁡((ci−1)/(10−1)∗log⁡(0.6/0.25))E\\_{i}=0.25\\\\exp((c\\_{i}-1)/(10-1)\\*\\\\log(0.6/0.25)). We then define the sampling level sis\\_{i} of each motion as:\n\n\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | si\\\\displaystyle s\\_{i} | ={(min⁡(Emax\\_key\\_body\\_error/0.15,1))5,ci=1,ci,ci>1.\\\\displaystyle=\\\\begin{cases}\\\\left(\\\\min\\\\left(E\\_{\\\\text{max\\\\\\_key\\\\\\_body\\\\\\_error}}/{0.15},1\\\\right)\\\\right)^{5},&c\\_{i}=1,\\\\\\<br>c\\_{i},&c\\_{i}>1.\\\\end{cases} |  | (1) |\n\n\n\nThe actual sampling probability is obtained by normalizing sis\\_{i}.\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "teacher-soft-moe",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 3.2 Motion Mixture-of-Experts\n\nTo enhance the expressiveness of the model, we incorporate a soft MoE module during the training of the teacher policy. An illustration of our model is shown in Fig. [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#S3.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 3 Learning General Motion Tracking Controllers ‣ GMT: General Motion Tracking for Humanoid Whole-Body Control\"). The policy network consists of a group of expert networks and a gating network. The expert networks take both of the robot state observation and motion targets as input, which output the final action 𝒂t{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t}. The gating network also takes the same input observations and outputs a probability distribution over all experts. The final action output is a combination of actions sampled from each expert’s individual action distributions:\n𝒂=∑i=1npi​𝒂i,{\\\\bm{a}}=\\\\sum\\_{i=1}^{n}p\\_{i}{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{i},\nwhere pip\\_{i} is the probability of each expert output by the gating network and 𝒂i{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{i} is the output of each expert policy.\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "distilled-student",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 6.3 Policy Learning\n\nFollowing previous works \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib7 \"\"), [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib8 \"\")\\], we adopt a two-stage training framework. In the first stage, we train a privileged teacher policy that observes both proprioceptive and privileged information, and outputs joint target actions 𝒂^t\\\\hat{{\\\\bm{a}}}\\_{t}, optimized using PPO \\[ [38](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib38 \"\")\\]. In the second stage, we train a deployable student policy that takes as input a sequence of proprioceptive observation history, and is supervised by the teacher policy through DAgger \\[ [39](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib39 \"\")\\]. The student policy is optimized by minimizing the ℓ2\\\\ell\\_{2} loss between its output 𝒂t{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t} and the teacher’s output 𝒂t^\\\\hat{{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t}} according to ‖𝒂t^−𝒂‖22\\\\\\|\\\\hat{{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t}}-{\\\\bm{a}}\\\\\\|\\_{2}^{2}.\n\n### 6.4 Observations and Actions\n\nFor the teacher policy observations consist of proprioception 𝒐t{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t}, privileged information 𝒆t{\\\\bm{e}}\\_{t}, and motion targets 𝒈{\\\\bm{g}}. 𝒐t{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t} consists of root angular velocity (3 dims), root roll and pitch (2 dims), joint positions (23 dims), joint velocities (23 dims), and last action (23 dims). 𝒆t{\\\\bm{e}}\\_{t} consists of root linear velocity (3 dims), root height (1 dim), key body positions, feet contact mask (2 dims), mass randomization params (6 dim), and motor strength (46 dims). For the student policy, observation consists of proprioception 𝒐t{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t}, proprioception history 𝒐t−20,…,𝒐t{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t-20},\\\\dots,{\\\\bm{o}}\\_{t}, and motion targets 𝒈{\\\\bm{g}}.\n\nThe output action is target joint positions.\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "simulation-comparison",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 4.1 Experimental Setups\n\nWe evaluate GMT in both simulation and real-world settings. For simulation experiments, each policy is trained using approximately 6.86.8 billion samples with domain randomization \\[ [42](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib42 \"\")\\] and action delay \\[ [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib43 \"\")\\], on a filtered subset of the AMASS and LAFAN1 \\[ [40](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib40 \"\")\\] datasets; and evaluated on AMASS test set \\[ [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib3 \"\")\\] and LAFAN1. We use IsaacGym \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib44 \"\")\\] as the simulator and the number of parallel environments is 40964096. For ablation studies and baseline comparisons, we focus on evaluating the performance of the privileged policy, as the deployable student policy is trained solely through imitation of the privileged teacher. For real-world experiments, we deploy our policy on Unitree G1 \\[ [45](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib45 \"\")\\], a medium-sized humanoid robot with 2323 DoFs and a height of 1.321.32 meters.\nThe policy tracking performance is quantitatively evaluated with: 1)EmpkpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpkpe}}, mean per keybody position error, in m​mmm; 2)EmpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}, mean per joint position error, in r​a​drad; 3)EvelE\\_{\\\\text{vel}}, linear velocity error, in m/sm/s; 4)Eyaw velE\\_{\\\\text{yaw vel}}, yaw velocity error, in r​a​d/srad/s.\n\n### 4.2 Baselines\n\nWe compare GMT’s performance with ExBody2 \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib7 \"\")\\] in simulation. We re-implement ExBody2 and train it on our filtered dataset. From table [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#S4.T2 \"Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GMT: General Motion Tracking for Humanoid Whole-Body Control\"), we found that GMT outperforms ExBody2 on both local tracking performance (EmpkpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpkpe}} and EmpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}) and global tracking performance (EvelE\\_{\\\\text{vel}} and Eyaw velE\\_{\\\\text{yaw vel}}).\n\nTable 2: Simulation evaluation of trained policies on AMASS-test and LAFAN dataset. All policies are trained on our filtered AMASS+LAFAN dataset. For all the baseline comparison and ablation studies, we only compare the performance of privileged policies.\n\n|  | AMASS-Test | LAFAN1 |\n| Method | EmpkpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpkpe}}↓\\\\downarrow | EmpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}↓\\\\downarrow | EvelE\\_{\\\\text{vel}}↓\\\\downarrow | Eyaw velE\\_{\\\\text{yaw vel}}↓\\\\downarrow | EmpkpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpkpe}}↓\\\\downarrow | EmpjpeE\\_{\\\\text{mpjpe}}↓\\\\downarrow | EvelE\\_{\\\\text{vel}}↓\\\\downarrow | Eyaw velE\\_{\\\\text{yaw vel}}↓\\\\downarrow |\n| Teacher & Student |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "real-demonstrations",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 4.4 Real-World Deployment\n\nAs shown in Fig. [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#S0.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ GMT: General Motion Tracking for Humanoid Whole-Body Control\"), we successfully deploy our policy on a real-world humanoid robot, reproducing a wide range of human motions — including stylized walking, high kicking, dancing, spinning, crouch walking, soccer kicking, and many others — with high fidelity and state-of-the-art performance. For more detailed deployment results, please refer to the [project website](https://gmt-humanoid.github.io/ \"\").\n\n### 4.5 Applications - Tracking MDM-Generated Motions\n\nWe test our policy with motion diffusion model (MDM) \\[ [46](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib46 \"\")\\] generated motions in MuJoCo \\[ [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib47 \"\")\\] sim-to-sim settings. Results in Fig. [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#S4.F7 \"Figure 7 ‣ 4.5 Applications - Tracking MDM-Generated Motions ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GMT: General Motion Tracking for Humanoid Whole-Body Control\") show that GMT can perform well on motions generated with MDM by text prompts, proving the potential of GMT to be applied to other downstream tasks.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770v2/mdm-snaps.png)Figure 7: Motion tracking on MDM-generated motions.\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "limitations",
                  "citationId": "gmt-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 6 Limitations\n\nWhile GMT achieves state-of-the-art performance as a unified, general motion tracking controller, it still has several limitations:\n\n- •\n\n\nLack of Contact-Rich Skills. Due to the significant additional simulation complexity required to model contact-rich behaviors \\[ [28](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14770#bib.bib28 \"\")\\], along with hardware limitations, our framework does not currently support skills such as getting up from a fallen state or rolling on the ground.\n\n- •\n\n\nLimitations on Challenging Terrains. Our current policy is trained without any terrain observations and is not designed for imitation on challenging terrains like slopes and stairs. In future work, we aim to extend our framework to develop a general and robust controller capable of operating across both flat and challenging terrains.\n"
                }
