            "sourceChecked": "rewards-constraints-2024: fetched primary body and arXiv metadata; exact URLs, timestamps and literals are in the paired plan.",
            "note": "Correction reviewed 2026-09-09T14:08:44.644Z by agent:91cb126e/integrator against retained September 8 responses, not a new fetch. Table I and V-B explicitly give three reward terms and eleven constraints (six probabilistic, five average). The corrected paragraph says reduced tuning, not elimination. The current neutral description contains no abolishes-weights claim and is preserved, not rewritten to an obsolete packet endpoint. VI-B names Raibo, Mini-cheetah, Hound, ANYmal B, ANYmal C, Go1 and Atlas in simulation. These are separately trained controllers, not seven hardware deployments. VI-A and VI-B independently name deployment on Raibo and Mini-cheetah; no other real platform is inferred. V-B requires trial-and-error tuning for the reference robot. Table III and VI-B vary the torque coefficient among three reward coefficients for robot transfer; this is not no-other-design. VI-B changes morphology/size-specific limits and regions, including manual Atlas hip limits. V-B allows description-file or manual limits; robot-specific choices remain explicit. IV-A uses small nonzero probability thresholds and averages; IV-B uses IPO with TRPO and adaptive feasible-region thresholds. Measured real costs 0.007/0.001/0.002 versus 0.025 contradict zero-violation hard-bound shorthand. Body and abs metadata agree on ordered eight-author title. T-RO 2024 is source-supplied acceptance metadata only; unversioned ar5iv body is not independently v4-byte-pinned. Full original/current cells: constrained-reward-original-history-20260909. Source provenance: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-constrained-reward-methods-integration-20260909/source-proof.json. No whole-P1/article, held-original or independent acceptance credit.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "constrained-reward-method-6-20260908",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "formulation",
                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "TABLE I: Reward Functions\n\n| Reward | Expression |\n| Command tracking |\n| rc=−kc​(‖c​m​dvx​y−Vx​y‖2+(c​m​dwz−wz)2)r\\_{c}=-k\\_{c}(\\|\\|cmd\\_{v\\_{xy}}-V\\_{xy}\\|\\|^{2}+(cmd\\_{w\\_{z}}-w\\_{z})^{2}) |\n| Joint torque | rτ=−kτ​‖τ‖2r\\_{\\\\tau}=-k\\_{\\\\tau}\\|\\|\\\\tau\\|\\|^{2} |\n| Action smoothness |\n| rs=−ks​(‖qtd​e​s−qt−1d​e​s‖2+‖qtd​e​s−2​qt−1d​e​s+qt−2d​e​s‖2)r\\_{s}=-k\\_{s}(\\|\\|q^{des}\\_{t}-q^{des}\\_{t-1}\\|\\|^{2}+\\|\\|q^{des}\\_{t}-2q^{des}\\_{t-1}+q^{des}\\_{t-2}\\|\\|^{2}) |\n\nRewards are composed of three terms: command tracking, joint torque, and action smoothness. Command tracking reward and joint torque reward guide the robot to track the given velocity command with minimal torque usage. Action smoothness reward is additionally used to regulate the neural network policy to have a smooth output surface, which is crucial for preventing motor vibration in a real-world deployment. The detailed reward equations are in Table [I](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S5.T1 \"TABLE I ‣ V-B Teacher policy ‣ V Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\"). The final reward given to the robot is r=rc+rτ+rsr=r\\_{c}+r\\_{\\\\tau}+r\\_{s}, where kc,kτ,ksk\\_{c},k\\_{\\\\tau},k\\_{s} are reward coefficients representing relative weights between different reward terms. To make the training framework more generalizable across different robot platforms, we used the linear relationship between the energy usage (i.e., torque usage ‖τ‖2\\|\\|\\\\tau\\|\\|^{2}) and the robot’s mass. To this end, we parameterized the torque reward coefficient kτk\\_{\\\\tau} of a robot with mass mm as kτ=k^τ⋅m¯/m=(sτ⋅k¯τ)⋅m¯/mk\\_{\\\\tau}=\\\\hat{k}\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m=(s\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau})\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m, where k^τ\\\\hat{k}\\_{\\\\tau} is the torque reward coefficient before mass compensation, sτs\\_{\\\\tau} is the scaling factor, k¯τ\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau} and m¯\\\\bar{m} each are the torque reward coefficient and the mass of the reference robot. We first find an adequate torque reward coefficient for the reference robot k¯τ\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau} by trial and error. For the reference robot, sτ=1s\\_{\\\\tau}=1 and m=m¯m=\\\\bar{m}. When training for a different robot, we initialize the torque reward coefficient to kτ=k¯τ⋅m¯/mk\\_{\\\\tau}=\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m, where sτ=1s\\_{\\\\tau}=1, and modify sτs\\_{\\\\tau} as we observe to their resulting motions. In our work, a quadruped robot Raibo \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib9 \"\")\\] is the reference robot.\n\nCost functions are defined based on their constraint type. If the constraint should be satisfied at every time step or is easier to be represented via probability, we formulate it as a probabilistic constraint ( [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E5 \"In IV-A1 Probabilistic Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) and use an indicator function for the cost function ( [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E4 \"In IV-A1 Probabilistic Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")). If not, the constraint is formulated as an average constraint ( [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E7 \"In IV-A2 Average Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) by defining the cost function as the corresponding physical variable ( [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E6 \"In IV-A2 Average Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")).\n\nTo summarize, a total of 11 constraints (6 probabilistic constraints and 5 average constraints) are defined and used to train a control policy for legged robots. To the best of our knowledge, this is the greatest number of constraints ever used in the constrained reinforcement learning literature. Some of the constraints may not be critical during the optimization depending on the robot and the environment. For example, for some robots, the joint velocity constraint may have less of an impact because the desirable range was chosen with enough margin, and so the initial policy already satisfied the desired constraint threshold. However, constraints do not need to be switched on and off according to their usage because of the property of logarithmic barrier functions. Concretely, already satisfied constraints have no effect on policy optimization due to a near-zero gradient of the barrier function. However, the barrier function applies a penalty when the policy tries to exit the desired region during exploration via a steep gradient."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "sim-platforms",
                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "The proposed learning framework was used to train locomotion controllers for diverse legged robots in the physics simulator: Raibo \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib9 \"\")\\], Mini-cheetah \\[ [58](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib58 \"\")\\], Hound \\[ [59](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib59 \"\")\\], Anymal B, Anymal C \\[ [60](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib60 \"\")\\], Unitree Go1, and Atlas (Figure [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S1.F1 \"Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")). Robots used for training vary in both morphology and physical properties. In particular, two robot types (quadruped and bipedal) are studied, with mass ranging from 11kg\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\mathrm{g} to 135kg\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\mathrm{g}, and robot size and leg length ranging from a 0.3m\\\\mathrm{m} tall small-scale dog with a 0.4m\\\\mathrm{m} leg length to a 1.9m\\\\mathrm{m} tall person with a 0.9m\\\\mathrm{m} leg length. Furthermore, suitable PD gains for each robot varied substantially depending on the robot’s motor specification and inertia, and the hind legs design of quadruped robots (Anymal B and Anymal C) differed depending on whether they are bent forward or backward."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "hardware",
                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "The locomotion control policy runs at 100Hz. We deployed the trained controller on two real-world quadruped robots: Raibo \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib9 \"\")\\] and Mini-cheetah \\[ [58](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib58 \"\")\\]. For the deployment, the student policy network was reimplemented with the Eigen library in C++ and forward-passed in the Central Processing Unit (CPU). We recommend readers check the original paper \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib9 \"\"), [58](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib58 \"\")\\] for more details about the robot hardware."
                },
                {
