                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "TABLE I: Reward Functions\n\n| Reward | Expression |\n| Command tracking |\n| rc=−kc​(‖c​m​dvx​y−Vx​y‖2+(c​m​dwz−wz)2)r\\_{c}=-k\\_{c}(\\|\\|cmd\\_{v\\_{xy}}-V\\_{xy}\\|\\|^{2}+(cmd\\_{w\\_{z}}-w\\_{z})^{2}) |\n| Joint torque | rτ=−kτ​‖τ‖2r\\_{\\\\tau}=-k\\_{\\\\tau}\\|\\|\\\\tau\\|\\|^{2} |\n| Action smoothness |\n| rs=−ks​(‖qtd​e​s−qt−1d​e​s‖2+‖qtd​e​s−2​qt−1d​e​s+qt−2d​e​s‖2)r\\_{s}=-k\\_{s}(\\|\\|q^{des}\\_{t}-q^{des}\\_{t-1}\\|\\|^{2}+\\|\\|q^{des}\\_{t}-2q^{des}\\_{t-1}+q^{des}\\_{t-2}\\|\\|^{2}) |\n\nRewards are composed of three terms: command tracking, joint torque, and action smoothness. Command tracking reward and joint torque reward guide the robot to track the given velocity command with minimal torque usage. Action smoothness reward is additionally used to regulate the neural network policy to have a smooth output surface, which is crucial for preventing motor vibration in a real-world deployment. The detailed reward equations are in Table [I](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S5.T1 \"TABLE I ‣ V-B Teacher policy ‣ V Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\"). The final reward given to the robot is r=rc+rτ+rsr=r\\_{c}+r\\_{\\\\tau}+r\\_{s}, where kc,kτ,ksk\\_{c},k\\_{\\\\tau},k\\_{s} are reward coefficients representing relative weights between different reward terms. To make the training framework more generalizable across different robot platforms, we used the linear relationship between the energy usage (i.e., torque usage ‖τ‖2\\|\\|\\\\tau\\|\\|^{2}) and the robot’s mass. To this end, we parameterized the torque reward coefficient kτk\\_{\\\\tau} of a robot with mass mm as kτ=k^τ⋅m¯/m=(sτ⋅k¯τ)⋅m¯/mk\\_{\\\\tau}=\\\\hat{k}\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m=(s\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau})\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m, where k^τ\\\\hat{k}\\_{\\\\tau} is the torque reward coefficient before mass compensation, sτs\\_{\\\\tau} is the scaling factor, k¯τ\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau} and m¯\\\\bar{m} each are the torque reward coefficient and the mass of the reference robot. We first find an adequate torque reward coefficient for the reference robot k¯τ\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau} by trial and error. For the reference robot, sτ=1s\\_{\\\\tau}=1 and m=m¯m=\\\\bar{m}. When training for a different robot, we initialize the torque reward coefficient to kτ=k¯τ⋅m¯/mk\\_{\\\\tau}=\\\\bar{k}\\_{\\\\tau}\\\\cdot\\\\bar{m}/m, where sτ=1s\\_{\\\\tau}=1, and modify sτs\\_{\\\\tau} as we observe to their resulting motions. In our work, a quadruped robot Raibo \\[ [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib9 \"\")\\] is the reference robot.\n\nCost functions are defined based on their constraint type. If the constraint should be satisfied at every time step or is easier to be represented via probability, we formulate it as a probabilistic constraint ( [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E5 \"In IV-A1 Probabilistic Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) and use an indicator function for the cost function ( [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E4 \"In IV-A1 Probabilistic Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")). If not, the constraint is formulated as an average constraint ( [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E7 \"In IV-A2 Average Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) by defining the cost function as the corresponding physical variable ( [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E6 \"In IV-A2 Average Constraints ‣ IV-A Constraint Types ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")).\n\nTABLE III: Reward coefficients for several legged robots\n\n|  | kck\\_{c} | k^τ\\\\hat{k}\\_{\\\\tau} | ksk\\_{s} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Raibo | 10. | 0.005 | 0.06 |\n| Hound | 10. | 0.0025 | 0.06 |\n| Go1 | 10. | 0.025 | 0.06 |\n| Mini-cheetah | 10. | 0.03 | 0.06 |\n| Anymal B | 10. | 0.005 | 0.06 |\n| Anymal C | 10. | 0.003 | 0.06 |\n| Atlas | 10. | 0.001 | 0.06 |\n\nRegardless of these variations, robust locomotion controllers for individual robots could be trained successfully using our framework with minimal parameter modifications. As shown in Figure [3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.F3 \"Fig. 3 ‣ VI-B Framework Evaluation ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")-A and Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\"), policies gradually learned to maximize the designed rewards while satisfying all of the constraints. Scaling factor sτs\\_{\\\\tau}, which defines the torque reward coefficient kτk\\_{\\\\tau}, was only adjusted among the reward coefficients for different robots (Table [III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.T3 \"TABLE III ‣ VI-B Framework Evaluation ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")). This is reasonable because energy usage is a complex function that cannot be solely modeled by mass; it is also influenced by factors like link inertia and actuator characteristics.\n\nIn summary, locomotion controllers for various legged robots were trained with our framework that utilized only three rewards and generalizable constraints. A single locomotion learning framework could train control policies for N robots with a minimal amount of engineering. Specifically, only a single reward coefficient was adjusted among the three designed reward terms for robot transfer. If the robot’s training is solely guided by a composition of abundant reward terms, the interdependency between them makes the additional engineering process difficult and cumbersome. More analysis about the reward-only framework is provided in Sec. [VI-D](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.SS4 \"VI-D Comparison with the Reward-only Framework ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\"). There were some modifications for the constraints, but this can be considered a much more intuitive process for the control engineer compared to the reward coefficient tuning because the constraint bounds all have exact physical meanings. Our extensive real-world experiments show that dynamic and agile controllers trained in simulation can be successfully transferred to the real world in zero-shot and show highly robust performance in various harsh terrains."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "manual-constraint-changes",
                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "Constraint limits and desirable region S for some probabilistic constraints were adjusted based on the robot’s morphology and size. S for the joint angle constraints were set semantically the same for all quadruped robots. In the case of Atlas, a bipedal robot, the desirable joint angle ranges S for the hip joints were manually set in the joint angle constraint based on the related online video footage (e.g., the robot’s locomotion videos) to prevent the robot from spreading its legs too far to the sides while walking. In the gait pattern constraint, the size of the initial phase offset parameter Φ0\\\\Phi\\_{0} for quadrupedal robots was four (e.g., \\[0, pi, pi, 0\\]), while it was two (e.g., \\[0, pi\\]) for bipedal robots. This is necessary because each robot has a different number of legs. Thresholds for the COM, foot clearance, and foot height limit constraints were adjusted based on the size of the robot. For instance, the 7cm foot clearance limit used for Anymal C is too large for a small-scale robot like Mini-cheetah. The contact velocity constraint threshold was set the same for all quadruped robots but was slightly increased for Atlas. This was necessary because the foot’s collision body of the quadruped robots was a sphere, whereas that of Atlas was a box. Desirable regions for joint velocity and joint torque constraints were set automatically from the robot description file (e.g., URDF files). Other constraint limits, desirable regions S, and reward functions were set exactly the same for all robots. Specific limits for each constraint are provided in Table [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.T2 \"TABLE II ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\").\n\nThe desirable range S defining the indicator cost functions of joint angle, joint velocity, and joint torque constraints can be retrieved automatically from the robot description file (e.g., URDF files) if available, or manually determined by the engineer based on the desired motion to obtain or prior knowledge of the system to control. The constraint limits for the above constraints, except gait pattern constraint cg​pc\\_{gp}, are set to a fairly small value to restrict the policy from entering the undesirable region."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "optimizer-limits",
                  "citationId": "rewards-constraints-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517",
                  "supportingPassage": "In reinforcement learning, stochastic policies are often used \\[ [48](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib48 \"\"), [49](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib49 \"\")\\] for better exploration. Thus, constraints for the policies should be defined in a probabilistic manner, which is the biggest difference from deterministic constraints commonly used in numerical optimization problems. We use two types of constraints: probabilistic and average constraints. Each constraint can be customized in a form that suits the engineer’s intent.\n\n#### IV-A1 Probabilistic Constraints\n\nA probabilistic constraint is used to limit the probability of an undesirable event. It can be set by defining the cost function as an indicator function as below:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Ck​(s,a,s′)={0,if ​(s,a,s′)∈𝐒1,otherwise,C\\_{k}(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})=\\\\begin{cases}0,&\\\\text{if }(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\in\\\\mathbf{S}\\\\\\<br>1,&\\\\text{otherwise,}\\\\end{cases} |  | (4) |\n\nwhere S is the robot’s desirable event space.\nThen the constraint is written as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Prob⁡((s,a,s′)∉𝐒)=𝔼ρ,π,P​\\[Ck​(s,a,s′)\\]≤Dk,\\\\mathrm{Prob}((s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\notin\\\\mathbf{S})=\\\\underset{\\\\rho,\\\\pi,P}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[C\\_{k}(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\right\\]\\\\leq D\\_{k}, |  | (5) |\n\nwhere Dk∈\\[0,1\\]D\\_{k}\\\\in\\[0,1\\] is the probability threshold for the kkth constraint. If DkD\\_{k} is set to zero, the policy is trained to completely stay away from encountering the undesirable event. Consequently, the probabilistic constraint can be employed to establish constraints for the undesirable region that the robot should avoid at every time step. For example, a robot’s center of mass position should remain within a specific distance threshold from the ground to pass through the overhanging obstacles. We can define the desired area S for the center of mass and set the limit of the probability constraint to zero. However, satisfying the constraint of Dk=0D\\_{k}=0 in stochastic MDP settings is challenging. Thus, in our work, DkD\\_{k} is instead set to a fairly small value.\n\n#### IV-A2 Average Constraints\n\nAn average constraint is used to restrict the average of some physical variables of the robot to be below a desired threshold. It can be set by defining the cost function as below:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Ck​(s,a,s′)=f⁡(s,a,s′),C\\_{k}(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})=f(s,a,s^{\\\\prime}),\\\\\\ |  | (6) |\n\nwhere f⁡(s,a,s′)f(s,a,s^{\\\\prime}) is the corresponding physical variable.\nThen the constraint is written as\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 𝔼ρ,π,P​\\[f⁡(s,a,s′)\\]=𝔼ρ,π,P​\\[Ck​(s,a,s′)\\]≤Dk,\\\\underset{\\\\rho,\\\\pi,P}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[f(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\right\\]=\\\\underset{\\\\rho,\\\\pi,P}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[C\\_{k}(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\right\\]\\\\leq D\\_{k}, |  | (7) |\n\nwhere DkD\\_{k} is the value threshold for the kkth constraint. This type is appropriate when the goal is to limit the average value rather than to enforce constraints at every step. For instance, it is preferable for legged robots to have low foot velocities when making contact with the ground to avoid slippage. Because the foot velocity may not be zero on slippery surfaces, it is undesirable to use a probabilistic constraint. Instead, we can specify a threshold below which the average foot contact velocity should be.\n\n### IV-BPolicy optimization\n\nThe probabilistic and average constraints are handled as discounted cumulative constraints during the policy optimization as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | JCk​(π):=𝔼ρ,π,P​\\[∑t=0∞γt​Ck​(st,at,st+1)\\]≤dk,J\\_{C\\_{k}}(\\\\pi):=\\\\underset{\\\\rho,\\\\pi,P}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[\\\\sum\\_{t=0}^{\\\\infty}\\\\gamma^{t}C\\_{k}(s\\_{t},a\\_{t},s\\_{t+1})\\\\right\\]\\\\leq d\\_{k}, |  | (8) |\n\nwhere dk=Dk/(1−γ)d\\_{k}=D\\_{k}/(1-\\\\gamma) is the modified constraint limit. This has been done in previous works \\[ [42](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib42 \"\"), [43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib43 \"\")\\] to efficiently optimize the policy in CMDP formulation ( [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S3.E2 \"In III-A Constrained Markov Decision Process ‣ III Background ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) with a near-constraint satisfaction guarantee \\[ [42](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib42 \"\")\\].\n\nWe use the Interior-point Policy Optimization (IPO) \\[ [43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib43 \"\")\\] to solve the constrained reinforcement learning problem ( [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S3.E2 \"In III-A Constrained Markov Decision Process ‣ III Background ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")). IPO converts the constrained problem into an unconstrained problem using logarithmic barrier functions as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | maximizeπ∈Πθ​J​(π)+∑k=1Klog⁡(dk−JCk​(π))/t,\\\\underset{\\\\pi\\\\in\\\\Pi\\_{\\\\theta}}{\\\\mathrm{maximize}}\\\\;J(\\\\pi)+\\\\sum\\_{k=1}^{K}\\\\log{\\\\left(d\\_{k}-J\\_{C\\_{k}}(\\\\pi)\\\\right)}/t, |  | (9) |\n\nwhere t>0t>0 is the hyperparameter that determines the steepness of the logarithmic barrier functions. With some approximations ( [3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S3.E3 \"In III-A Constrained Markov Decision Process ‣ III Background ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")), the IPO objective ( [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E9 \"In IV-B Policy optimization ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) is modified as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | maximizeπ∈Πθ​𝔼s∼dπia∼π​\\[Aπi​(s,a)\\]+∑k=1Klog(dk−(JCk(πi)+11−γ𝔼s∼dπia∼π\\[AπiCk(s,a)\\]))/ts.t. D¯K​L(π\\|\\|πi)≤δ.\\\\begin{gathered}\\\\underset{\\\\pi\\\\in\\\\Pi\\_{\\\\theta}}{\\\\mathrm{maximize}}\\\\;\\\\underset{\\\\begin{subarray}{c}s\\\\sim d^{\\\\pi\\_{i}}\\\\\\<br>a\\\\sim\\\\pi\\\\end{subarray}}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[A^{\\\\pi\\_{i}}(s,a)\\\\right\\]\\\\\\<br>+\\\\sum\\_{k=1}^{K}\\\\log(d\\_{k}-(J\\_{C\\_{k}}(\\\\pi\\_{i})+\\\\frac{1}{1-\\\\gamma}\\\\underset{\\\\begin{subarray}{c}s\\\\sim d^{\\\\pi\\_{i}}\\\\\\<br>a\\\\sim\\\\pi\\\\end{subarray}}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[A^{\\\\pi\\_{i}}\\_{C\\_{k}}(s,a)\\\\right\\]))/t\\\\\\<br>\\\\text{s.t. }\\\\bar{D}\\_{KL}(\\\\pi\\|\\|\\\\pi\\_{i})\\\\leq\\\\delta.\\\\end{gathered} |  | (10) |\n\nIn the original paper \\[ [43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib43 \"\")\\], the IPO objective ( [10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S4.E10 \"In IV-B Policy optimization ‣ IV Learning Framework ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\")) is optimized with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) \\[ [49](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib49 \"\")\\]. However, we utilized Trust Region Policy Optimization (TRPO) \\[ [48](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib48 \"\")\\] because the optimizer’s explicit line search allows for more stable policy improvement and constraint feasibility checking 111\n\n\n\nWe also experimented with the PPO\\[ [49](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib49 \"\")\\] optimizer and found several algorithmic advantages, such as ease of implementation and the availability to utilize recurrent neural networks, albeit with the trade-off of training stability.. The advantage functions AπA^{\\\\pi} and ACkπA^{\\\\pi}\\_{C\\_{k}} are computed using Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) \\[ [50](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib50 \"\")\\] and neural network value functions. Each advantage function then undergoes additional standardization and zero-mean normalization before computing the policy gradients.\n\nBecause we are utilizing constrained reinforcement learning to train controllers for complex robotic systems, the method should be capable of dealing with a large number of constraints. However, earlier publications in the literature, such as CPO and IPO, had limits when it came to explicitly addressing these issues. To that purpose, we incorporate two modifications to the IPO to make it scalable for the number of constraints.\n\n#### IV-B1 Adaptive constraint thresholding\n\nIPO requires the policy to be located in the feasible region during the training because it uses logarithmic barrier functions. However, this setting is not practical in situations where policies are randomly initialized. For these circumstances, a naive approach \\[ [45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#bib.bib45 \"\")\\] can be utilized, in which one of the violated constraints is chosen and the policy is adjusted until it enters the feasible region by directly reducing the cost advantage functions. This, however, makes the training inefficient because updating the violated constraints one at a time becomes difficult as the number of constraints increases.\n\nIn this work, we widen the feasible region by adaptively adjusting the constraint limit based on the performance of the current policy πi\\\\pi\\_{i}. Specifically, the kkth constraint limit for the iith policy update dkid^{i}\\_{k} is set as\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | dki=m​a​x​(dk,JCk​(πi)+α⋅dk),d^{i}\\_{k}=max(d\\_{k},J\\_{C\\_{k}}(\\\\pi\\_{i})+\\\\alpha\\\\cdot d\\_{k}),\\\\\\ |  | (11) |\n\nwhere JCk​(πi)=𝔼ρ,πi,P​\\[Ck​(s,a,s′)\\]/(1−γ)J\\_{C\\_{k}}(\\\\pi\\_{i})={\\\\underset{\\\\rho,\\\\pi\\_{i},P}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[C\\_{k}(s,a,s^{\\\\prime})\\\\right\\]}/(1-\\\\gamma) and α\\\\alpha is the hyperparameter that determines how much the feasible region will be enlarged.\n\nThis makes the problem always feasible due to zero-mean normalization done for the cost advantage function ACkπA^{\\\\pi}\\_{C\\_{k}} and brings a similar effect as scale normalization by parameterizing the enlarging range with dkd\\_{k}. Furthermore, the steep inclination of a barrier function gradually guides the policy toward the desired constraint region defined with threshold dkd\\_{k}.\n\nReal-world constraint satisfaction was also verified for those that can be directly observed by sensors (i.e., joint angle, joint velocity, joint torque). For verification, we collected approximately 25 minutes of real-world robot sensor data with the Raibo robot in the outdoor experiment routes shown in Figure [4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2308.12517#S6.F4 \"Fig. 4 ‣ VI-B Framework Evaluation ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Not Only Rewards But Also Constraints: Applications on Legged Robot Locomotion\"). Joint angle, joint velocity, and joint torque constraints are all probabilistic constraints, and their cost values were 0.007, 0.001, and 0.002, respectively, indicating constraint satisfaction given that the thresholds were all 0.025."
