                  "partId": "optimizer-and-choices",
                  "citationId": "stagewise-cmorl-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755",
                  "supportingPassage": "According to Kim et al. \\[ [14](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib14 \"\")\\], convergence to a CP optimal policy can be achieved by aggregating the advantage functions of rewards and costs through a weighted summation, where the weights satisfy specific conditions, and updating the policy using TRPO \\[ [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib33 \"\")\\] with the aggregated advantage.\nIt can be written as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | πt+1=\\\\displaystyle\\\\pi\\_{t+1}= | argmax𝜋​𝔼τ∼πt​\\[π⁡(a\\|s)πt​(a\\|s)​Aπt​(s,a)\\]\\\\displaystyle\\\\underset{\\\\pi}{\\\\mathrm{argmax}}\\\\underset{\\\\tau\\\\sim\\\\pi\\_{t}}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\left\\[\\\\frac{\\\\pi(a\\|s)}{\\\\pi\\_{t}(a\\|s)}A^{\\\\pi\\_{t}}(s,a)\\\\right\\] |  | (2) |\n|  |  | 𝐬.𝐭.𝔼τ∼πt\\[DKL(πt(⋅\\|s)\\|\\|π(⋅\\|s))\\]≤ϵ,\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathbf{s.t.}\\\\;\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{\\\\tau\\\\sim\\\\pi\\_{t}}\\\\left\\[D\\_{\\\\mathrm{KL}}(\\\\pi\\_{t}(\\\\cdot\\|s)\\|\\|\\\\pi(\\\\cdot\\|s))\\\\right\\]\\\\leq\\\\epsilon, |  |\n\nwhere Aπt​(s,a):=∑iνt,i​ARiπt​(s,a)−∑jλt,j​ACjπt​(s,a)A^{\\\\pi\\_{t}}(s,a):=\\\\sum\\_{i}\\\\nu\\_{t,i}A\\_{R\\_{i}}^{\\\\pi\\_{t}}(s,a)-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\lambda\\_{t,j}A\\_{C\\_{j}}^{\\\\pi\\_{t}}(s,a), ϵ\\\\epsilon is a trust region size, and DKLD\\_{\\\\mathrm{KL}} is the KL divergence.\nFor the condition on the weights, ν\\\\nu and λ\\\\lambda, please refer to Theorem 4.2 in \\[ [14](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib14 \"\")\\].\n\nIn order to properly determine the weights, ν\\\\nu and λ\\\\lambda, we use 1) reward normalization and 2) standard deviation of advantage functions.\nFirst, in the CMORL setting, each reward function operates at a different scale, making it essential to adjust them to a consistent level.\nTo this end, we apply reward normalization for each reward and stage, and train the value functions using the normalized rewards. This approach automatically adjusts the ratio of each objective to a consistent level.\nNext, it is important to maintain consistency not only in the scale of the rewards but also in the ratio between objectives and constraints.\nWithout a consistent ratio, the policy may be updated to over-maximizing objectives rather than satisfying constraints, potentially destabilizing the training process.\nTo resolve this, we normalize the reward and cost advantages by their respective standard deviations, ensuring the policy to be updated with a consistent ratio of objectives to constraints.\nAs a result, the proposed rule for advantage aggregation as follows:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Aπ=ARπStd⁡\\[ARπ\\]−η​∑jACjπStd⁡\\[ACjπ\\]​𝟏(JCi​(π)>di),A^{\\\\pi}=\\\\frac{A\\_{R}^{\\\\pi}}{\\\\mathrm{Std}\\[A\\_{R}^{\\\\pi}\\]}-\\\\eta\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\frac{A\\_{C\\_{j}}^{\\\\pi}}{\\\\mathrm{Std}\\[A\\_{C\\_{j}}^{\\\\pi}\\]}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{(J\\_{C\\_{i}}(\\\\pi)>d\\_{i})}, |  | (3) |\n\nwhere Std\\\\mathrm{Std} denotes the standard deviation, ARπ:=∑iωi​A^RiπA\\_{R}^{\\\\pi}:=\\\\sum\\_{i}\\\\omega\\_{i}\\\\hat{A}\\_{R\\_{i}}^{\\\\pi} with a given preference ω\\\\omega, A^Riπ\\\\hat{A}\\_{R\\_{i}}^{\\\\pi} is the advantage function calculated from the normalized rewards, and the hyper-parameter η\\\\eta serves as the ratio of constraints, as done in \\[ [16](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib16 \"\")\\].\nWith the aggregated advantage functions, the policy can be updated using ( [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#S4.E2 \"In IV-B CMORL Policy Update ‣ IV PROPOSED METHOD ‣ Stage-Wise Reward Shaping for Acrobatic Robots: A Constrained Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning Approach\")).\nHowever, to simplify the implementation, we apply a PPO update, which is formulated as follows \\[ [15](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib15 \"\")\\]:\n\n|     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | πt+1=argmax𝜋​𝔼τ∼πt​\\[min⁡(rt​Aπt,clip⁡(rt,1−ϵ,1+ϵ)​Aπt)\\],\\\\pi\\_{t+1}\\\\!\\\\!=\\\\underset{\\\\pi}{\\\\mathrm{argmax}}\\\\!\\\\underset{\\\\tau\\\\sim\\\\pi\\_{t}}{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\!\\\\left\\[\\\\min(r\\_{t}A^{\\\\pi\\_{t}},\\\\mathrm{clip}(r\\_{t},1-\\\\epsilon,1+\\\\epsilon)A^{\\\\pi\\_{t}})\\\\right\\], |  |\n\nwhere rt:=π⁡(a\\|s)/πt​(a\\|s)r\\_{t}:=\\\\pi(a\\|s)/\\\\pi\\_{t}(a\\|s), and ϵ\\\\epsilon is a hyper-parameter.\n\nThese estimates are aggregated to calculate the overall advantage, as detailed in Sec. [IV-B](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#S4.SS2 \"IV-B CMORL Policy Update ‣ IV PROPOSED METHOD ‣ Stage-Wise Reward Shaping for Acrobatic Robots: A Constrained Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning Approach\"), which is subsequently used for policy updates. Additionally, a stage scheduler updates the current stage based on a user-defined rule for stage transitions.\n\nTABLE I: Example of reward and cost definitions for the back-flip task.\nThe reward and cost functions are defined in the first five lines and the following five lines, respectively.\nIn the velocity reward, 𝟏turn\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\\\mathrm{turn}} means 11 if the robot completes a turn and 00 otherwise.\nIn the balance reward, z^B\\\\hat{z}\\_{B} and z^W\\\\hat{z}\\_{W} are the zz-axis unit vectors of the base and world frame, respectively.\nIn the style reward, qjdefaultq^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j} represents the default position of the jjth joint.\nIn the contact-related costs, ICspecifiedI\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{specified}} denotes the set of indices of specified links that make contact.\nDuring stages other than the jump stage, the foot contact cost outputs the predefined threshold value as the cost is undefined in these phases.\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| stage | stand | sit | jump | air | land | threshold |\n| reward functions |\n| base height | −\\|pz−0.35\\|-\\|p\\_{z}-0.35\\| | −\\|pz−0.2\\|-\\|p\\_{z}-0.2\\| | 𝟏pz≤0.5⋅pz\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{p\\_{z}\\\\leq 0.5}\\\\cdot p\\_{z} | 𝟏pz≤0.5⋅pz\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{p\\_{z}\\\\leq 0.5}\\\\cdot p\\_{z} | −\\|pz−0.35\\|-\\|p\\_{z}-0.35\\| | - |\n| base velocity | −(vx2+vy2+ωz2)-(v\\_{x}^{2}+v\\_{y}^{2}+\\\\omega\\_{z}^{2}) | −(vx2+vy2+ωz2)-(v\\_{x}^{2}+v\\_{y}^{2}+\\\\omega\\_{z}^{2}) | −𝟏turn⋅ωy-\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\\\mathrm{turn}}\\\\cdot\\\\omega\\_{y} | −s⋅ωy-s\\\\cdot\\\\omega\\_{y} | −(vx2+vy2+ωz2)-(v\\_{x}^{2}+v\\_{y}^{2}+\\\\omega\\_{z}^{2}) | - |\n| base balance | −∠⁡(z^B,z^W)-\\\\angle(\\\\hat{z}\\_{B},\\\\hat{z}\\_{W}) | −∠⁡(z^B,z^W)-\\\\angle(\\\\hat{z}\\_{B},\\\\hat{z}\\_{W}) | −\\|∠⁡(y^B,z^W)−π/2\\|-\\|\\\\angle(\\\\hat{y}\\_{B},\\\\hat{z}\\_{W})-\\\\pi/2\\| | −\\|∠⁡(y^B,z^W)−π/2\\|-\\|\\\\angle(\\\\hat{y}\\_{B},\\\\hat{z}\\_{W})-\\\\pi/2\\| | −∠⁡(z^B,z^W)-\\\\angle(\\\\hat{z}\\_{B},\\\\hat{z}\\_{W}) | - |\n| energy | −∑jτj2-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\tau\\_{j}^{2} | −∑jτj2-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\tau\\_{j}^{2} | −∑jτj2-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\tau\\_{j}^{2} | −∑jτj2-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\tau\\_{j}^{2} | −∑jτj2-\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\tau\\_{j}^{2} | - |\n| style | −∑j(qj−qjdefault)2-\\\\sum\\_{j}(q\\_{j}-q^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j})^{2} | −∑j(qj−qjdefault)2-\\\\sum\\_{j}(q\\_{j}-q^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j})^{2} | −∑j(qj−qjdefault)2-\\\\sum\\_{j}(q\\_{j}-q^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j})^{2} | −∑j(qj−qjdefault)2-\\\\sum\\_{j}(q\\_{j}-q^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j})^{2} | −∑j(qj−qjdefault)2-\\\\sum\\_{j}(q\\_{j}-q^{\\\\mathrm{default}}\\_{j})^{2} | - |\n| cost functions |\n| foot contact | - | - | 𝟏\\|ICfoot,rear\\|=0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{foot,rear}}\\|=0} | - | - | 0.250.25 |\n| body contact | 𝟏\\|ICbody\\|>0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{body}}\\|>0} | 𝟏\\|ICbody\\|>0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{body}}\\|>0} | 𝟏\\|ICbody\\|>0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{body}}\\|>0} | 𝟏\\|ICbody\\|>0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{body}}\\|>0} | 𝟏\\|ICbody\\|>0\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|I\\_{C}^{\\\\mathrm{body}}\\|>0} | 0.0250.025 |\n| joint position | 1J∑j𝟏qj>qjmax\\|\\|qj<qjmin\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{q\\_{j}>q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}\\|\\|q\\_{j}<q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}} | 1J∑j𝟏qj>qjmax\\|\\|qj<qjmin\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{q\\_{j}>q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}\\|\\|q\\_{j}<q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}} | 1J∑j𝟏qj>qjmax\\|\\|qj<qjmin\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{q\\_{j}>q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}\\|\\|q\\_{j}<q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}} | 1J∑j𝟏qj>qjmax\\|\\|qj<qjmin\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{q\\_{j}>q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}\\|\\|q\\_{j}<q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}} | 1J∑j𝟏qj>qjmax\\|\\|qj<qjmin\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{q\\_{j}>q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}\\|\\|q\\_{j}<q\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}} | 0.0250.025 |\n| joint velocity | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|q˙j\\|>q˙jmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}\\|>\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|q˙j\\|>q˙jmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}\\|>\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|q˙j\\|>q˙jmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}\\|>\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|q˙j\\|>q˙jmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}\\|>\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|q˙j\\|>q˙jmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}\\|>\\\\dot{q}\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 0.0250.025 |\n| joint torque | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|τj\\|>τjmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\tau\\_{j}\\|>\\\\tau\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|τj\\|>τjmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\tau\\_{j}\\|>\\\\tau\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|τj\\|>τjmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\tau\\_{j}\\|>\\\\tau\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|τj\\|>τjmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\tau\\_{j}\\|>\\\\tau\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 1J​∑j𝟏\\|τj\\|>τjmax\\\\frac{1}{J}\\\\sum\\_{j}\\\\mathbf{1}\\_{\\|\\\\tau\\_{j}\\|>\\\\tau\\_{j}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}} | 0.0250.025 |\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755v1/img/stage_transition.png)Fig. 3: Example of stage transitions for the back-flip. The task start in the stand stage.\nUpon receiving a back-flip command, the robot attempts to sit.\nWhen the base height drops below 0.25​m0.25\\\\;m, it transitions to the jump stage. During this stage, the robot attempts to jump, transitioning to the air stage once all feet detach from the ground. After completing the aerial motion, the robot transitions to the landing stage as soon as at least one foot makes contact with the ground."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "evaluation-split",
                  "citationId": "stagewise-cmorl-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755",
                  "supportingPassage": "We use the Isaac Gym simulator \\[ [28](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib28 \"\")\\] due to its effectiveness in sim-to-real transfer and its flexibility in creating a wide range of tasks across various robotic platforms.\nIn simulation experiments, we employ two types of robots from Unitree Robotics: Go1, a quadrupedal robot, and H1, a humanoid \\[ [35](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#bib.bib35 \"\")\\].\n\nThe quadrupedal robot comprises 23 body links and 12 motors, while the humanoid robot features 20 body links and 19 motors.\nIn real-world experiments, we deploy the quadruped robot, Go1, through sim-to-real techniques as discussed in Section [IV-C](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#S4.SS3 \"IV-C Sim-to-Real Techniques ‣ IV PROPOSED METHOD ‣ Stage-Wise Reward Shaping for Acrobatic Robots: A Constrained Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning Approach\").\n\nThe state representation includes body orientation, joint positions and velocities, commands, as well as the previous action.\nFor the teacher policy, privileged information—such as linear and angular velocities, height, and foot contact, which are difficult to access in the real-world but available in simulation—is additionally used.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755v1/img/backflip_joint_position.png)\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755v1/img/sideroll_joint_position.png)\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755v1/img/twohand_joint_position.png)\n\nFig. 6: Sim-to-real experimental results.\nThe graph shows the position changes of three joints—hip, thigh, and knee—in the left front leg, along with the body orientation over time for each task.\nThe solid line represents the real-world data, while the dotted line indicates the simulation results.\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755v1/img/ablation_result.png)Fig. 7: Comparison with RL and constrained RL algorithms.\nThe first five graphs show the proportion of time the robot remains in each stage during an episode, while the last shows the success rate.\nAll results are obtained by training algorithms with five random seeds.\nBold lines represent the mean, and shaded areas indicate the standard deviation of the results.\n\n### V-BTask Details\n\nWe have designed four acrobatic tasks: _back-flip, side-flip, side-roll_, and _two-hand walk_.\nThe back-flip and two-hand walk tasks are implemented on both types of robots, while the side-roll and side-flip tasks are designated for the quadruped.\nSnapshots of each task are shown in Fig. [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#S0.F1 \"Fig. 1 ‣ Stage-Wise Reward Shaping for Acrobatic Robots: A Constrained Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning Approach\") and Fig. [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15755#S4.F4 \"Fig. 4 ‣ IV PROPOSED METHOD ‣ Stage-Wise Reward Shaping for Acrobatic Robots: A Constrained Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning Approach\"), and the following are descriptions of each task.\n\nFor real-world evaluation, the side-roll, back-flip, and two-hand walk tasks were successfully demonstrated using a quadrupedal robot."
                },
