                  "supportingPassage": "To deal with this challenge, constrained RL emerges as a technique to enforce the constraints through mathematical formulations describing the desired characteristics of robot behaviors \\[ [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#bib.bib9 \"\")\\]. This technique can be divided into model-based and model-free approaches. The model-based approach relies on accurate models for learning, making model-free more practical. Without an environment model, model-free constrained RL represents undesired behaviors using penalty terms (Ri​tR\\_{it}), resulting in the following reward function (RtR\\_{t}):\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Rt=λ0​tR0​t−∑λi​tRi​t,\\\\text{$R\\_{t}$}=\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{0t}$}\\\\text{$R\\_{0t}$}-\\\\sum\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{it}$}\\\\text{$R\\_{it}$}, |  | (1) |\n\nwhere RtR\\_{t}, R0​tR\\_{0t}, and Ri​tR\\_{it} denote the total reward, primary reward (i.e., the weighted summation of all positive reward terms), and it​hi^{th} semi-positive definite constraint penalty terms at time tt, while λ0​t\\\\lambda\\_{0t} and λi​t\\\\lambda\\_{it} denote the corresponding weighting gains. This approach can be categorized further into two groups: fixed-weighting, where the gains are pre-tuned and fixed; and adaptive weighting, where the gains are adapted or learned.\n\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | λ0​t\\\\lambda\\_{0t} | =1−Δt,\\\\displaystyle=1-\\\\Delta\\_{t}, |  | (2) |\n|  | λi​t\\\\lambda\\_{it} | =rλi​t​Δt,\\\\displaystyle=\\\\text{$r\\_{\\\\lambda\\_{i}t}$}\\\\Delta\\_{t}, |  | (3) |\n\nwhere Δt\\\\Delta\\_{t} denotes the clipped summation of constraint penalties, preventing λ0​t<0\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{0t}$}<0 and ensuring that λ0​t+Σ​λi​t=1\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{0t}$}+\\\\Sigma\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{it}$}=1, and rλi​tr\\_{\\\\lambda\\_{i}t} denotes the constraint contribution ratio of the it​hi^{th} constraint penalty term. Δt\\\\Delta\\_{t} and rλi​tr\\_{\\\\lambda\\_{i}t} are computed from the ratio of the estimated constraint penalties (R~i​t\\\\tilde{R}\\_{it}) to the constraint thresholds (τi\\\\tau\\_{i}) as:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Δt=min⁡{∑j​(R~j​t/τj)2,1.0},\\\\Delta\\_{t}=\\\\operatorname{min}\\\\left\\\\{\\\\text{$\\\\sum\\_{j}$}(\\\\text{$\\\\tilde{R}\\_{jt}$}/\\\\tau\\_{j})^{2},1.0\\\\right\\\\}, |  | (4) |\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | rλi​t=(R~i​t/τi)2∑j(R~j​t/τj)2,\\\\text{$r\\_{\\\\lambda\\_{i}t}$}=\\\\frac{(\\\\text{$\\\\tilde{R}\\_{it}$}/\\\\tau\\_{i})^{2}}{\\\\sum\\_{j}(\\\\text{$\\\\tilde{R}\\_{jt}$}/\\\\tau\\_{j})^{2}}, |  | (5) |\n\nwhere τi\\\\tau\\_{i} and τj\\\\tau\\_{j} are selected intuitively based on the physical properties of the system, e.g., maximum hardware limits, together forming a safe region into which the robot should be bounded, as shown by the dashed lines in Figure [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S2.F3 \"Fig. 3 ‣ II Reward-Oriented Gains via Embodied Regulation (ROGER) ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\"), while R~i​t\\\\tilde{R}\\_{it} is a statistical estimate of the ithi^{\\\\text{th}} penalty at time tt: R~i​t=average​\\[Ri​t\\]−kσ​std​\\[σi​t\\]\\\\text{$\\\\tilde{R}\\_{it}$}=\\\\text{average}\\[\\\\text{${R}\\_{it}$}\\]-k\\_{\\\\sigma}\\\\text{std}\\[\\\\text{$\\\\sigma\\_{it}$}\\]. A preliminary result when varying kσk\\_{\\\\sigma} is presented in the Appendix.\n\nThis adaptation strategy is also proven to be partially stable in key conditions (i.e., near the constraint thresholds and convergence), while the expected primary reward is guaranteed to increase, as detailed in Appendix \\[ [23](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#bib.bib23 \"\")\\]. As a result, if an optimal solution exists far from the constraint thresholds, such as body orientation in robot locomotion, ROGER will converge to that solution; otherwise, it chooses a safe alternative.\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759v2/figure/adaptationsurface0.jpg)(a)\n\n![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759v2/figure/adaptationsurface1.jpg)(b)\n\nFig. 3:\nThis adaptation strategy is also proven to be partially stable in key conditions (i.e., near the constraint thresholds and convergence), while the expected primary reward is guaranteed to increase, as detailed in Appendix. As a result, if an optimal solution exists far from the constraint thresholds, such as body orientation in robot locomotion, ROGER will converge to that solution; otherwise, it chooses a safe alternative.\n\nIntuitively, the robot initially maximizes the primary reward since all penalty terms and their gains are zero, i.e., Δt=0\\\\Delta\\_{t}=0 and λ0​t=1\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{0t}$}=1, as illustrated in Figure [3(a)](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S2.F3.sf1 \"In Fig. 3 ‣ II Reward-Oriented Gains via Embodied Regulation (ROGER) ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\"). As the robot approaches a constraint threshold, the Ri​tR\\_{it} terms increase, causing Δt\\\\Delta\\_{t} to grow, reducing λ0​t\\\\lambda\\_{0t} and increasing λi​t\\\\lambda\\_{it}, thereby distributing more and more attention to the penalty terms. This setup prevents the robot from approaching the constraint thresholds. However, when near a constraint threshold, i.e., some Ri​tR\\_{it}≈τi\\\\approx\\\\tau\\_{i}, Δt\\\\Delta\\_{t} saturates at 1.0, making λ0​t≈0\\\\text{$\\\\lambda\\_{0t}$}\\\\approx 0 and Rt≈−Ri​t\\\\text{$R\\_{t}$}\\\\approx-\\\\text{$R\\_{it}$}. This setup neglects the primary reward, immediately enforcing the reduction of the penalty terms and ensuring constraint satisfaction.\n\nSimultaneously, the penalty weighting gains are distributed according to the ratio of Ri​tR\\_{it}, as illustrated in Figure [3(b)](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S2.F3.sf2 \"In Fig. 3 ‣ II Reward-Oriented Gains via Embodied Regulation (ROGER) ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\"). As the first penalty term (R1​tR\\_{1t}) increases, it receives more weight and higher optimization priority. However, as other terms, e.g., R2​tR\\_{2t}, rise, the weighting gains are redistributed, lowering the priority of the first penalty term to increase those of the others. Thus, this setup automatically balances the contributions between different penalty terms online during locomotion learning. In total, ROGER111\n\nThe total reward function in Equation [1](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S1.E1 \"In I Introduction ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\") includes a forward velocity reward (R0​t=vfwd\\\\text{$R\\_{0t}$}=v\\_{\\\\text{fwd}}, encouraging forward movement) and orientation penalties for roll (R1​t=\\|αt\\|\\\\text{$R\\_{1t}$}=\\|\\\\alpha\\_{t}\\|, penalizing sideways oscillation) and pitch (R2​t=\\|βt\\|\\\\text{$R\\_{2t}$}=\\|\\\\beta\\_{t}\\|, penalizing longitudinal oscillation). The constraint thresholds, e.g., τj\\\\tau\\_{j} in Equations [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S2.E4 \"In II Reward-Oriented Gains via Embodied Regulation (ROGER) ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\") and [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10759#S2.E5 \"In II Reward-Oriented Gains via Embodied Regulation (ROGER) ‣ Gain Tuning Is Not What You Need: Reward Gain Adaptation for Constrained Locomotion Learning\"), are selected as 0.2 rad, or approximately 10∘. To include 99.9% of the exploration uncertainty in the process, ROGER recomputes the reward weighting gains using kσ=3k\\_{\\\\sigma}=3. In other state-of-the-art methods, R~i​t\\\\tilde{R}\\_{it} is used as summarized in Appendix."
