                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01672",
                  "supportingPassage": "# Title:RDA: Reward Design Agent for Reinforcement Learning\n\nAuthors: [Hojoon Lee](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lee,+H), [Ajay Subramanian](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Subramanian,+A), [Ben Abbatematteo](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Abbatematteo,+B), [Vijay Veerabadran](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Veerabadran,+V), [Pedro Matias](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Matias,+P), [Karl Ridgeway](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ridgeway,+K), [Nitin Kamra](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kamra,+N)\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled RDA: Reward Design Agent for Reinforcement Learning, by Hojoon Lee and 6 other authors\n\n[View PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.01672) [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.01672v1)\n\n> Abstract:Reinforcement learning has enabled the acquisition of impressive robotic skills, but typically requires hand-crafted reward functions that are slow to design and difficult to align with human intentions. Recent work, such as Eureka, automates reward design by using an LLM to iteratively generate and refine reward code from task descriptions. However, they rely on coarse feedback signals such as success rate, which provide little semantic insight into the learned behavior. As a result, their trained policies achieve the final goal but are frequently poorly aligned with task instructions. We introduce the Reward Design Agent (RDA), a VLM-based agentic framework that injects semantic understanding into reward design. RDA decomposes tasks, visually evaluates trajectories, summarizes failure modes, and iteratively revises reward code to better align with task instructions. Across 12 tabletop manipulation tasks from ManiSkill and 4 whole-body manipulation tasks from HumanoidBench, RDA produces policies substantially more instruction-aligned than those of other baselines, while achieving comparable task success rates. Videos and the generated reward code are available on [this https URL](https://nitinkamra1992.github.io/reward-design-agent).\n\n|     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n| Comments: | Accepted to RLC'26 |\n| Subjects: | Machine Learning (cs.LG) |\n| Cite as: | [arXiv:2606.01672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01672) \\[cs.LG\\] |\n|  | (or [arXiv:2606.01672v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01672v1) \\[cs.LG\\] for this version) |\n|  | [https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.01672](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.01672)<br>Focus to learn more<br>arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite |"
