                  "supportingPassage": "Massively multitask world models. To demonstrate that our Table 1. Training cost. Ap-\nproposed improvements facilitate scaling of world models, we proximate TD-MPC2 training cost\nevaluate the performance of 5 multitask models ranging from on the 80-task dataset, reported in\n1M to 317M parameters on a collection of 80 diverse tasks that GPU days on a single NVIDIA\nspan multiple task domains and vary greatly in objective, em- GeForce RTX 3090 GPU. We also\nbodiment, and action space. Models are trained on a dataset list the normalized score achieved\nof 545M transitions obtained from the replay buffers of 240 by each model at end of training.\nsingle-task TD-MPC2 agents, and thus contain a wide variety\n                                                                Params (M) GPU days Score\nof behaviors ranging from random to expert policies. The task\nset consists of all 50 Meta-World tasks, as well as 30 DMCon-        1         3.7      16.0\ntrol tasks. The DMControl task set includes 19 original DM-          5         4.2      49.5\nControl tasks, as well as 11 new tasks. For completeness, we        19         5.3      57.1\ninclude a separate set of scaling results on the 30-task DMCon-     48         12       68.0\n                                                                   317         33       70.6\ntrol subset (345M transitions) as well. Due to our careful de-\nsign of the TD-MPC2 algorithm, scaling up is straightforward:\nto improve rate of convergence we use a 4× larger batch size (1024) compared to the single-task\nexperiments, but make no other changes to hyperparameters.\n\n[… noncontiguous retained passage …]\n\nScaling TD-MPC2 to 317M parameters. Our scaling results are                           Finetuning\nshown in Figure 7. To summarize agent performance with a single              60\n                                                                                        10 tasks\n                  