            "note": "Abstract prints verbatim: 'We describe a new physics engine tailored to model-based control.'; 'Models are specified using either a high-level C++ API or an intuitive XML file format.'; 'The engine can compute both forward and inverse dynamics.'; 'To facilitate optimal control applications and in particular sampling and finite differencing, the dynamics can be evaluated for different states and controls in parallel.' These supply the physics-engine, hand-specified-model, and state-and-control forward-dynamics predicates. 'Explicit state-transition model' and the world-model boundary verdict are the article's framing (current note already discloses this); the abstract prints no 'not learned' clause - the span's 'simply not learned' rests on the printed API/XML model-specification mechanism. Abstract page proves only what the abstract prints; full text remains paywalled.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "taxonomy-19-mujoco-20260916h",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "t19-mujoco-physics-engine-model-based-control",
                  "citationId": "mujoco-2012",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2012.6386109",
                  "supportingPassage": "We describe a new physics engine tailored to model-based control. Multi-joint dynamics are represented in generalized coordinates and computed via recursive algorithms. Contact responses are computed via efficient new algorithms we have developed, based on the modern velocity-stepping approach which avoids the difficulties with spring-dampers. Models are specified using either a high-level C++ API or an intuitive XML file format. A built-in compiler transforms the user model into an optimized data structure used for runtime computation. The engine can compute both forward and inverse dynamics. The latter are well-defined even in the presence of contacts and equality constraints. The model can include tendon wrapping as well as actuator activation states (e.g. pneumatic cylinders or muscles). To facilitate optimal control applications and in particular sampling and finite differencing, the dynamics can be evaluated for different states and controls in parallel."
                },
