                  "supportingPassage": "###### Assumption 1 (Regularity)\n\nIn each evaluation trial, the initial state s0s\\_{0} and the observation o0o\\_{0} are drawn in an independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) fashion from the underlying distribution 𝒟s0,o0\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{s\\_{0},o\\_{0}}333Note that this is a standard assumption in statistical testing. A discussion of practical methods by which to approximately satisfy this condition in robotic evaluation is included in \\[ [28](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10966#bib.bib28 \"\")\\].. We assume access to samples from 𝒟s0,o0\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{s\\_{0},o\\_{0}}, but do not assume 𝒟s0,o0\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{s\\_{0},o\\_{0}} itself to be known.\n\nUnder Assumption 1, the nthn^{\\\\text{th}} evaluation trial involves making an i.i.d. draw of an environment from 𝒟s0,o0\\\\mathcal{D}\\_{s\\_{0},o\\_{0}} and running the policy π1\\\\pi\\_{1} in this environment. This yields a binary success/failure outcome z1,nz\\_{1,n} corresponding to an i.i.d. draw from a Bernoulli random variable with mean p1p\\_{1}, which is the true performance (success rate) of the policy π1\\\\pi\\_{1} on the task:\n\n|     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | z1,n∼Ber​(p1).z\\_{1,n}\\\\sim\\\\text{Ber}(p\\_{1}). |  | (2) |\n\n"
