                  "partId": "risk-schedule",
                  "citationId": "optimal-stopping-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10966",
                  "supportingPassage": "### IV-DPower Adaptivity to Varying Difficulty: Risk Budgets\n\nAs discussed in [SectionIV-B](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10966#S4.SS2 \"IV-B Decision Regions ‣ IV Methodology ‣ Is Your Imitation Learning Policy Better than Mine? Policy Comparison with Near-Optimal Stopping\"), we must appropriately adjust the temporal rate of risk accumulation.\nTo formally define this notion of risk, we introduce a non-negative scalar function f⁡(n)f(n) for n∈{1,⋯,Nmax}n\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\cdots,N\\_{\\\\max}\\\\}, which determines the maximum allowable Type-I Error under any null hypothesis at each step nn. We impose a constraint ∑n=0Nmaxf⁡(n)=α∗\\\\sum\\_{n=0}^{N\\_{\\\\text{max}}}f(n)=\\\\alpha^{\\*} to globally bound the Type-I error by α∗\\\\alpha^{\\*}.\nThis risk “budget” can be interpreted as encoding the evaluator’s competing objectives: to reject the null and stop the evaluation quickly in easier cases (front-loading the risk accumulation) against the desire to wait longer to achieve a significant decision in harder instances (delaying risk accumulation until more data is collected). Importantly, _any risk budget ff that is nonnegative everywhere and sums to some r∈\\[0,α∗\\]r\\\\in\\[0,\\\\alpha^{\\*}\\] maintains Type-I error control at level α∗\\\\alpha^{\\*}_. However, the shape of the risk budget will significantly influence the power of the resulting procedure, and thus represents an important component in solving (near-optimally) [Eq.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10966#S3.E4 \"In III Problem Formulation ‣ Is Your Imitation Learning Policy Better than Mine? Policy Comparison with Near-Optimal Stopping\"). With this said, in the following experiments we fix the risk budget to be uniform in order to focus on optimizing the decision regions (described in [SectionIV-E](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.10966#S4.SS5 \"IV-E Tractable Optimization ‣ IV Methodology ‣ Is Your Imitation Learning Policy Better than Mine? Policy Comparison with Near-Optimal Stopping\")); to be explicit: this means the risk budget is f⁡(n)=α∗Nmaxf(n)=\\\\frac{\\\\alpha^{\\*}}{N\\_{\\\\text{max}}} for each scenario. This selection is heuristically reasonable, but leaves potential for further improvements in future work.\n"
