                  "supportingPassage": "[PDF extracted-text page 1; layout text may interleave columns]\n CHOMP: Gradient Optimization Techniques for Efficient Motion Planning\n                                                            –PREPRINT–\n\n\n             Nathan Ratliff1           Matt Zucker1            J. Andrew Bagnell1                 Siddhartha Srinivasa2\n                                  1                                              2\n                                    The Robotics Institute                      Intel Research\n                                Carnegie Mellon University                     Pittsburgh, PA\n                           {ndr, mzucker, dbagnell}@cs.cmu.edu         siddhartha.srinivasa@intel.com\n\n\n   Abstract— Existing high-dimensional motion planning algo-\nrithms are simultaneously overpowered and underpowered. In\ndomains sparsely populated by obstacles, the heuristics used by\nsampling-based planners to navigate “narrow passages” can be\nneedlessly complex; furthermore, additional post-processing is\nrequired to remove the jerky or extraneous motions from the\npaths that such planners generate. In this paper, we present\nCHOMP, a novel method for continuous path refinement that\nuses covariant gradient techniques to improve the quality of\nsampled trajectories. Our optimization technique converges\nover a wider range of input paths and is able to optimize higher-\norder dynamics of trajectories than previous path optimization      Fig. 1. Experimental robotic platforms: Boston Dynamics’s LittleDog (left),\nstrategies. As a result, CHOMP can be used as a standalone          and Barrett Technology’s WAM arm (right).\nmotion planner in many real-world planning queries. The\neffectiveness of our proposed method is demonstrated in ma-\nnipulation planning for a 6-DOF robotic arm as well as in           collision free. As a result, CHOMP can often transform a\ntrajectory generation for a walking quadruped robot.\n                                                                    naı̈ve initial guess into a trajectory suitable for execution on\n                      I. I NTRODUCTION                              a robotic platform without invoking a separate motion plan-\n   In recent years, sampling-based planning algorithms have         ner. A covariant gradient update rule ensures that CHOMP\nmet with widespread success due to their ability to rapidly         quickly converges to a locally optimal trajectory.\ndiscover the connectivity of high-dimensional configuration            In many respects, CHOMP is related to optimal control of\nspaces. Planners such as Probabilistic Roadmap (PRM) and            robotic systems. Instead of merely finding feasible paths, our\nRapidly-exploring Random Tree (RRT) algorithms, along               goal is to directly construct trajectories which optimize over\nwith their descendents, are now used in a multitude of              a variety of dynamic and task-based criteria. Few current\nrobotic applications [15], [16]. Both algorithms are typically      approaches to optimal control are equipped to handle obsta-\ndeployed as part of a two-phase process: first find a feasible      cle avoidance, though. Of those that do, many approaches\npath, and then optimize it to remove redundant or jerky             require some description of configuration space obstacles,\nmotion.                                                             which can be prohibitive to create for high-dimensional\n   Perhaps the most prevalent method of path optimization           manipulators [25]. Many optimal controllers which do handle\nis the so-called “shortcut” heuristic, which picks pairs of         obstacles are framed in terms of mixed integer programming,\nconfigurations along the path and invokes a local planner to        which is known to be an NP-hard problem [24], [9], [17],\nattempt to replace the intervening sub-path with a shorter          [27]. Approximate