                  "supportingPassage": "[PDF extracted-text page 1; layout text may interleave columns]\n        Finding Locally Optimal, Collision-Free\n   Trajectories with Sequential Convex Optimization\n             John Schulman, Jonathan Ho, Alex Lee, Ibrahim Awwal, Henry Bradlow and Pieter Abbeel\n\n\n    Abstract—We present a novel approach for incorporating\ncollision avoidance into trajectory optimization as a method of\nsolving robotic motion planning problems. At the core of our\napproach are (i) A sequential convex optimization procedure,\nwhich penalizes collisions with a hinge loss and increases the\npenalty coefficients in an outer loop as necessary. (ii) An efficient\nformulation of the no-collisions constraint that directly considers\ncontinuous-time safety and enables the algorithm to reliably solve\nmotion planning problems, including problems involving thin and\ncomplex obstacles.\n    We benchmarked our algorithm against several other mo-\ntion planning algorithms, solving a suite of 7-degree-of-freedom\n(DOF) arm-planning problems and 18-DOF full-body planning\nproblems. We compared against sampling-based planners from\nOMPL, and we also compared to CHOMP, a leading approach\nfor trajectory optimization. Our algorithm was faster than the\nalternatives, solved more problems, and yielded higher quality\npaths.\n    Experimental evaluation on the following additional problem\ntypes also confirmed the speed and effectiveness of our approach:\n(i) Planning foot placements with 34 degrees of freedom (28 joints      Fig. 1. Several problem settings were we have used our algorithm for motion\n+ 6 DOF pose) of the Atlas humanoid robot as it maintains               planning. Top left: planning an arm trajectory for the PR2 in simulation, in a\nstatic stability and has to negotiate environmental constraints.        benchmark problem. Top right: PR2 opening a door with a full-body motion.\n(ii) Industrial box picking. (iii) Real-world motion planning for       Bottom left: industrial robot picking boxes, obeying an orientation constraint\nthe PR2 that requires considering all degrees of freedom at the         on the end effector. Bottom right: humanoid robot model (DRC/Atlas) ducking\n                                                                        underneath an obstacle while obeying static stability constraints.\nsame time.\n\n                       I. I NTRODUCTION\n   Trajectory optimization algorithms have two roles in robotic       part2,of\n                                                         Saturday, February 13 configuration space, which can be directly incorporated\n\nmotion planning. First, they can be used to smooth and shorten        into  the  convex optimization problem that is solved at each\ntrajectories generated by some other method. Second, they can         iteration  of the optimization.\nbe used to plan from scratch: one initializes with a trajectory           The first advantage of our approach is speed. Our imple-\nthat contains collisions and perhaps violates constraints, and mentation solves typical arm planning problems in around\none hopes that the optimization converges to a high-quality 100 − 200 ms and solves problems involving many more\ntrajectory satisfying constraints. Using an optimization algo- degrees of freedom in under a second. This is largely enabled\nrithm to plan from scratch is an especially attractive option by our novel formulation of the the collision penalty, which\nin problems with many degrees of freedom (DOF), since the guarantees safety in continuous time by considering swept-\ncomputation time scales favorably with the number of DOF.             out volumes. This cost formulation has little computational\n   Two of the key ingredients in trajectory optimization for overhead in collision checking and allows us to use a sparsely\nmotion planning are (1) the numerical optimization method, sampled trajectory. The second advantage of our approach\nand (2) 