                  "supportingPassage": "Grounding DINO is built upon the DETR-like model DINO \\[ [57](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib57 \"\")\\], which is an end-to-end Transformer-based detector.\nDETR was first proposed in \\[ [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib2 \"\")\\] and then has been improved from many directions \\[ [64](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib64 \"\"), [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib33 \"\"), [13](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib13 \"\"), [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib5 \"\"), [48](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib48 \"\"), [17](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib17 \"\"), [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib4 \"\")\\] in the past few years.\nDAB-DETR \\[ [31](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib31 \"\")\\] introduces anchor boxes as DETR queries for more accurate box prediction. DN-DETR \\[ [24](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib24 \"\")\\] proposes a query-denoising approach to stabilizing the bipartite matching.\nDINO \\[ [57](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib57 \"\")\\] further develops several techniques including contrastive de-noising and sets a new record on the COCO object detection benchmark.\nHowever, such detectors mainly focus on closed-set detection and are difficult to generalize to novel classes because of the limited pre-defined categories.\n\n"
                },
