                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "Under this principle, we design three feature fusion approaches in the neck, query initialization, and head phases. More specifically, we design a feature enhancer by stacking self-attention, text-to-image cross-attention, and image-to-text cross-attention as the neck module. We then develop a language-guided query selection method to initialize queries for the detection head. We also design a cross-modality decoder for the head phase with image and text cross-attention layers to boost query representations."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "perception-37-category-and-referring-inputs",
                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "Grounding DINO outputs multiple pairs of object boxes and noun phrases for a given (Image, Text) pair. For example, as shown in Fig. [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#S2.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Grounding DINO: Marrying DINO with Grounded Pre-Training for Open-Set Object Detection\"), the model locates a cat and a table from the input image and extracts word cat and table from the input text as corresponding labels. Both object detection and REC tasks can be aligned with the pipeline. Following GLIP \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\")\\], we concatenate all category names as input texts for object detection tasks. REC requires a bounding box for each text input. We use the output object with the largest scores as the output for the REC task."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "perception-37-rec-training-boundary",
                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "Nevertheless, both GLIP and Grounding DINO perform not well without REC data. More training data like caption data or larger models help the final performance, but quite minor. After injecting RefCOCO/+/g data into training, Grounding DINO obtains significant gains. The results reveal that most nowadays open-set object detectors need to pay more attention for a more fine-grained detection.\n"
                }
              ],
