                {
                  "partId": "perception-38-coco-setting-and-category-overlap",
                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "We compare Grounding DINO with GLIP and DINO in Table [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#S3.T2 \"Table 2 ‣ 3.5 Loss Function ‣ 3 Grounding DINO ‣ Grounding DINO: Marrying DINO with Grounded Pre-Training for Open-Set Object Detection\").\nWe pre-train models on large-scale datasets and directly evaluate our model on the COCO benchmark. As the O365 dataset \\[ [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib43 \"\")\\] has (nearly444\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIt is not an exact mapping between O365 and COCO categories. We made some approximations during evaluation. ) covered all categories in COCO, we evaluate an O365 pre-trined DINO on COCO as a zero-shot baseline. The result shows that DINO performs better on the COCO zero-shot transfer than DyHead.\nGrounding DINO outperforms all previous models on the zero-shot transfer setting, with +0.5+0.5AP and +1.8+1.8AP compared with DINO and GLIP under the same setting. Grounding data is still helpful for Grounding DINO, introducing more than 11AP (48.1 vs. 46.7) on the zero-shot transfer setting.\nWith stronger backbones and larger data, Grounding DINO sets a new record of 52.552.5 AP on the COCO object detection benchmark without seeing any COCO images during training.\nGrounding DINO obtains a 62.662.6 AP on COCO minival, outperforming DINO’s 62.562.5 AP.\nWhen enlarging the input images by 1.5×1.5\\\\times, the benefits reduce. We suspect that the text branch enlarges the gap between models with different input images. Even though the performance plateaus with larger input size, Grounding DINO gets an impressive 63.063.0 AP on COCO test-dev with fine-tuning on the COCO dataset(See the number in brackets of Table [2](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#S3.T2 \"Table 2 ‣ 3.5 Loss Function ‣ 3 Grounding DINO ‣ Grounding DINO: Marrying DINO with Grounded Pre-Training for Open-Set Object Detection\")).\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "perception-38-data-identity-and-version",
                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "## Appendix 0.B Data Usage\n\nWe use three types of data in our model pre-train.\n\n1. 1.\n\n\nDetection data. Following GLIP \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\")\\], we reformulate the object detection task to a phrase grounding task by concatenating the category names into text prompts. We use COCO \\[ [29](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib29 \"\")\\], O365 \\[ [43](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib43 \"\")\\], and OpenImage(OI) \\[ [19](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib19 \"\")\\] for our model pretrain. To simulate different text inputs, we randomly sampled category names from all categories in a dataset on the fly during training.\n\n2. 2.\n\n\nGrounding data. We use the GoldG and RefC data as grounding data. Both GoldG and RefC are preprocessed by MDETR \\[ [18](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib18 \"\")\\]. These data can be fed into Grounding DINO directly. GoldG contains images in Flickr30k entities \\[ [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib37 \"\"), [38](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib38 \"\")\\] and Visual Genome \\[ [20](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib20 \"\")\\]. RefC contains images in RefCOCO, RefCOCO+, and RefCOCOg.\n\n3. 3.\n\n\nCaption data. To enhance the model performance on novel categories, we feed the semantic-rich caption data to our model. Following GLIP, we use the pseudo-labeled caption data for model training. In our experiments, we use the same data with GLIP under comparable settings. More specifically, we use GLIP-T annotated caption data for Grounding DINO T, while GLIP-L annotated caption data for Grounding DINO L.\n\n\nThere are two versions of the O365 dataset, which we termed O365v1 and O365v2, respectively. O365v1 is a subset of O365v2. O365v1 contains about 600K images, while O365v2 contains about 1.7M images. Following previous works \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\"), [52](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib52 \"\")\\], we pre-train the Grounding DINO T on O365v1 for a fair comparison. The Grounding DINO L is pre-trained on O365v2 for a better result.\n\n"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "perception-38-other-regimes-not-interchangeable",
                  "citationId": "grounding-dino-2024",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499",
                  "supportingPassage": "Table 2: Zero-shot domain transfer and fine-tuning on COCO. \\* The results in brackets are trained with 1.5×\\\\times image sizes, i.e., with a maximum image size of 2000. †The models map a subset of O365 categories to COCO for zero-shot evaluations. ‡This is not a real zero-shot performance as we add COCO data during model training. We list the result here for a reference.\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Model | Backbone | Pre-Training Data | Zero-Shot | Fine-Tuning |\n| 2017val | 2017val/test-dev |\n| Faster R-CNN | RN50-FPN | - | - | 40.2 / - |\n| Faster R-CNN | RN101-FPN | - | - | 42.0 / - |\n| DyHead-T \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] | Swin-T | - | - | 49.7 / - |\n| DyHead-L \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] | Swin-L | - | - | 58.4 / 58.7 |\n| DyHead-L \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] | Swin-L | O365,ImageNet21K | - | 60.3 / 60.6 |\n| SoftTeacher \\[ [50](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib50 \"\")\\] | Swin-L | O365,SS-COCO | - | 60.7 / 61.3 |\n| DINO(Swin-L) \\[ [57](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib57 \"\")\\] | Swin-L | O365 | - | 62.5 / - |\n| DyHead-T†\\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] | Swin-T | O365 | 43.6 | 53.3 / - |\n| GLIP-T (B) \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\")\\] | Swin-T | O365 | 44.9 | 53.8 / - |\n| GLIP-T (C) \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\")\\] | Swin-T | O365,GoldG | 46.7 | 55.1 / - |\n| GLIP-L \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib25 \"\")\\] | Swin-L | FourODs,GoldG,Cap24M | 49.8 | 60.8 / 61.0 |\n| DINO(Swin-T)†\\[ [57](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib57 \"\")\\] | Swin-T | O365 | 46.2 | 56.9 / - |\n| Grounding DINO T (Ours) | Swin-T | O365 | 46.7 | 56.9 / - |\n| Grounding DINO T (Ours) | Swin-T | O365,GoldG | 48.1 | 57.1 / - |\n| Grounding DINO T (Ours) | Swin-T | O365,GoldG,Cap4M | 48.4 | 57.2 / - |\n| Grounding DINO L (Ours) | Swin-L | O365,OI\\[ [19](https://arxiv.org/html/2303.05499#bib.bib19 \"\")\\],GoldG | 52.5 | 62.6 / 62.7 (63.0 / 63.0)\\* |\n| Grounding DINO L (Ours) | Swin-L | O365,OI,GoldG,Cap4M,COCO,RefC | 60.7‡ | 62.6 / - |\n\n"
