                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 5 Segment Anything Dataset\n\nOur dataset, SA-1B, consists of 11M diverse, high-resolution, licensed, and privacy protecting images and 1.1B high-quality segmentation masks collected with our data engine. We compare SA-1B with existing datasets and analyze mask quality and properties. We are releasing SA-1B to aid future development of foundation models for computer vision. We note that SA-1B will be released under a favorable license agreement for certain research uses and with protections for researchers.\n\n#### Images\n\n. We licensed a new set of 11M images from a provider that works directly with photographers. These images are high resolution (3300×{\\\\times}4950 pixels on average), and the resulting data size can present accessibility and storage challenges. Therefore, we are releasing downsampled images with their shortest side set to 1500 pixels. Even after downsampling, our images are significantly higher resolution than many existing vision datasets ( _e.g_., COCO \\[ [66](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib66 \"\")\\] images are ∼\\\\scriptstyle\\\\sim480×{\\\\times}640 pixels). Note that most models today operate on much lower resolution inputs. Faces and vehicle license plates have been blurred in the released images.\n\n#### Masks\n\n. Our data engine produced 1.1B masks, 99.1% of which were generated fully automatically. Therefore, the quality of the automatic masks is centrally important. We compare them directly to professional annotations and look at how various mask properties compare to prominent segmentation datasets. Our main conclusion, as borne out in the analysis below and the experiments in § [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7 \"7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\"), is that our automatic masks are high quality and effective for training models. Motivated by these findings, SA-1B _only includes automatically generated masks._\n\n#### Mask quality.\n\nTo estimate mask quality, we randomly sampled 500 images (∼\\\\scriptstyle\\\\sim50k masks) and asked our professional annotators to improve the quality of all masks in these images. Annotators did so using our model and pixel-precise “brush” and “eraser” editing tools. This procedure resulted in pairs of automatically predicted and professionally corrected masks. We computed IoU between each pair and found that 94% of pairs have greater than 90% IoU (and 97% of pairs have greater than 75% IoU). For comparison, prior work estimates inter-annotator consistency at 85-91% IoU \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib44 \"\"), [60](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib60 \"\")\\]. Our experiments in § [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7 \"7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") confirm by human ratings that mask quality is high relative to a variety of datasets and that training our model on automatic masks is nearly as good as using all masks produced by the data engine."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "default-training",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments\n\nIn this section, we present _zero-shot transfer_ experiments with SAM, the Segment Anything Model. We consider five tasks, four of which differ significantly from the promptable segmentation task used to train SAM. These experiments evaluate SAM on datasets and tasks that were not seen during training (our usage of “zero-shot transfer” follows its usage in CLIP \\[ [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib82 \"\")\\]). The datasets may include novel image distributions, such as underwater or ego-centric images ( _e.g_. Fig. [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F8 \"Figure 8 ‣ Task. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\")) that, to our knowledge, do not appear in SA-1B.\n\nOur experiments begin by testing the core goal of promptable segmentation: producing a valid mask from any prompt. We emphasize the challenging scenario of a _single_ foreground point prompt, since it is more likely to be ambiguous than other more specific prompts. Next, we present a sequence of experiments that traverse low, mid, and high-level image understanding and roughly parallel the historical development of the field. Specifically, we prompt SAM to (1) perform edge detection, (2) segment everything, _i.e_. object proposal generation, (3) segment detected objects, _i.e_. instance segmentation, and (4), as a proof-of-concept, to segment objects from free-form text. These four tasks differ significantly from the promptable segmentation task that SAM was trained on and are implemented via prompt engineering. Our experiments conclude with an ablation study.\n\n#### Implementation.\n\nUnless otherwise specified: (1) SAM uses an MAE \\[ [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib47 \"\")\\] pre-trained ViT-H \\[ [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib33 \"\")\\] image encoder and (2) SAM was trained on SA-1B, noting that this dataset includes only automatically generated masks from the final stage of our data engine. For all other model and training details, such as hyperparameters, refer to § [A](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A1 \"Appendix A Segment Anything Model and Task Details ‣ Segment Anything\")."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "sampled-training",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "#### Training algorithm.\n\nFollowing recent approaches \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\"), [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib37 \"\")\\], we simulate an interactive segmentation setup during training. First, with equal probability either a foreground point or bounding box is selected randomly for the target mask. Points are sampled uniformly from the ground truth mask. Boxes are taken as the ground truth mask’s bounding box, with random noise added in each coordinate with standard deviation equal to 10% of the box sidelength, to a maximum of 20 pixels. This noise profile is a reasonable compromise between applications like instance segmentation, which produce a tight box around the target object, and interactive segmentation, where a user may draw a loose box.\n\nAfter making a prediction from this first prompt, subsequent points are selected uniformly from the error region between the previous mask prediction and the ground truth mask. Each new point is foreground or background if the error region is a false negative or false positive, respectively. We also supply the mask prediction from the previous iteration as an additional prompt to our model. To provide the next iteration with maximal information, we supply the unthresholded mask logits instead of the binarized mask. When multiple masks are returned, the mask passed to the next iteration and used to sample the next point is the one with the highest predicted IoU.\n\nWe find diminishing returns after 8 iteratively sampled points (we have tested up to 16). Additionally, to encourage the model to benefit from the supplied mask, we also use two more iterations where no additional points are sampled. One of these iterations is randomly inserted among the 8 iteratively sampled points, and the other is always at the end. This gives 11 total iterations: one sampled initial input prompt, 8 iteratively sampled points, and two iterations where no new external information is supplied to the model so it can learn to refine its own mask predictions. We note that using a relatively large number of iterations is possible because our lightweight mask decoder requires less than 1% of the image encoder’s compute and, therefore, each iteration adds only a small overhead. This is unlike previous interactive methods that perform only one or a few interactive steps per optimizer update \\[ [70](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib70 \"\"), [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib9 \"\"), [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib37 \"\"), [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\")\\].\n\n#### Training recipe.\n\nWe use the AdamW \\[ [68](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib68 \"\")\\] optimizer (β1=0.9\\\\beta\\_{1}=0.9, β2=0.999\\\\beta\\_{2}=0.999) and a linear learning rate warmup \\[ [42](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib42 \"\")\\] for 250 iterations and a step-wise learning rate decay schedule. The initial learning rate ( _lr_), after warmup, is 8​e−4{8}\\\\mathrm{e}^{-4}. We train for 90k iterations (∼\\\\scriptstyle\\\\sim2 SA-1B epochs) and decrease the _lr_ by a factor of 10 at 60k iterations and again at 86666 iterations. The batch size is 256 images. To regularize SAM, we set weight decay ( _wd_) to 0.1 and apply drop path \\[ [53](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib53 \"\")\\] ( _dp_) with a rate of 0.4. We use a layer-wise learning rate decay \\[ [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib5 \"\")\\] ( _ld_) of 0.8. No data augmentation is applied. We initialize SAM from an MAE \\[ [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib47 \"\")\\] pre-trained ViT-H. We distribute training across 256 GPUs, due to the large image encoder and 1024×{\\\\times}1024 input size. To limit GPU memory usage, we train with up to 64 randomly sampled masks per GPU. Additionally, we find that lightly filtering SA-1B masks to discard any that cover more than 90% of the image qualitatively improves results.\n\nFor ablations and others variations on training ( _e.g_., text-to-mask § [D.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.SS5 \"D.5 Zero-Shot Text-to-Mask ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\")), we deviate from the default recipe above as follows. When training with data from the first and second data engine stages only, we augment the input with large-scale jitter \\[ [40](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib40 \"\")\\] with a scale range of \\[0.1, 2.0\\]. Intuitively, data augmentation may be helpful when training data is more limited. To train ViT-B and ViT-L, we use 180k iterations with batch size 128 distributed across 128 GPUs. We set _lr_ = 8​e−4{8}\\\\mathrm{e}^{-4}/4​e−4{4}\\\\mathrm{e}^{-4}, _ld_ = 0.6/0.8, _wd_ = 0.1, and _dp_ = 0.6/0.4 for ViT-B/L, respectively."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "prompt-model-and-time",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 3 Segment Anything Model\n\nWe next describe the Segment Anything Model (SAM) for promptable segmentation. SAM has three components, illustrated in Fig. [4](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S2.F4 \"Figure 4 ‣ Discussion. ‣ 2 Segment Anything Task ‣ Segment Anything\"): an image encoder, a flexible prompt encoder, and a fast mask decoder. We build on Transformer vision models \\[ [14](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib14 \"\"), [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib33 \"\"), [20](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib20 \"\"), [62](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib62 \"\")\\] with specific tradeoffs for (amortized) real-time performance. We describe these components at a high-level here, with details in § [A](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A1 \"Appendix A Segment Anything Model and Task Details ‣ Segment Anything\").\n\n#### Image encoder.\n\nMotivated by scalability and powerful pre-training methods, we use an MAE \\[ [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib47 \"\")\\] pre-trained Vision Transformer (ViT) \\[ [33](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib33 \"\")\\] minimally adapted to process high resolution inputs \\[ [62](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib62 \"\")\\]. The image encoder runs once per image and can be applied prior to prompting the model.\n\n#### Prompt encoder.\n\nWe consider two sets of prompts: _sparse_ (points, boxes, text) and _dense_ (masks). We represent points and boxes by positional encodings \\[ [95](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib95 \"\")\\] summed with learned embeddings for each prompt type and free-form text with an off-the-shelf text encoder from CLIP \\[ [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib82 \"\")\\]. Dense prompts ( _i.e_., masks) are embedded using convolutions and summed element-wise with the image embedding.\n\n#### Mask decoder.\n\nThe mask decoder efficiently maps the image embedding, prompt embeddings, and an output token to a mask. This design, inspired by \\[ [14](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib14 \"\"), [20](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib20 \"\")\\], employs a modification of a Transformer decoder block \\[ [103](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib103 \"\")\\] followed by a dynamic mask prediction head. Our modified decoder block uses prompt self-attention and cross-attention in two directions (prompt-to-image embedding and vice-versa) to update _all_ embeddings. After running two blocks, we upsample the image embedding and an MLP maps the output token to a dynamic linear classifier, which then computes the mask foreground probability at each image location.\n\n#### Resolving ambiguity.\n\nWith one output, the model will average multiple valid masks if given an ambiguous prompt. To address this, we modify the model to predict multiple output masks for a single prompt (see Fig. [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S2.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ Zero-shot transfer. ‣ 2 Segment Anything Task ‣ Segment Anything\")). We found 3 mask outputs is sufficient to address most common cases (nested masks are often at most three deep: whole, part, and subpart). During training, we backprop only the minimum loss \\[ [15](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib15 \"\"), [45](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib45 \"\"), [64](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib64 \"\")\\] over masks. To rank masks, the model predicts a confidence score ( _i.e_., estimated IoU) for each mask.\n\n#### Efficiency.\n\nThe overall model design is largely motivated by efficiency. Given a precomputed image embedding, the prompt encoder and mask decoder run in a web browser, on CPU, in ∼\\\\scriptstyle\\\\sim50ms. This runtime performance enables seamless, real-time interactive prompting of our model.\n\n#### Losses and training.\n\nWe supervise mask prediction with the linear combination of focal loss \\[ [65](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib65 \"\")\\] and dice loss \\[ [73](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib73 \"\")\\] used in \\[ [14](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib14 \"\")\\]. We train for the promptable segmentation task using a mixture of geometric prompts (for text prompts see § [7.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.SS5 \"7.5 Zero-Shot Text-to-Mask ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\")). Following \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\"), [37](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib37 \"\")\\], we simulate an interactive setup by randomly sampling prompts in 11 rounds per mask, allowing SAM to integrate seamlessly into our data engine."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "point-protocol",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation\n\n#### Task.\n\nWe evaluate segmenting an object from a _single_ foreground point. This task is ill-posed as one point can refer to multiple objects. Ground truth masks in most datasets do not enumerate _all_ possible masks, which can make automatic metrics unreliable. Therefore, we supplement the standard mIoU metric ( _i.e_., the mean of all IoUs between predicted and ground truth masks) with a human study in which annotators rate mask quality from 1 (nonsense) to 10 (pixel-perfect). See § [D.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.SS1 \"D.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\"), § [E](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A5 \"Appendix E Human Study Experimental Design ‣ Segment Anything\"), and § [G](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A7 \"Appendix G Annotation Guidelines ‣ Segment Anything\") for additional details.\n\nBy default, we sample points from the “center” of ground truth masks (at a maximal value of the mask’s interior distance transform), following the standard evaluation protocol in interactive segmentation \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\")\\]. Since SAM is capable of predicting multiple masks, we evaluate only the model’s most confident mask by default. The baselines are all single-mask methods. We compare mainly to RITM \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\")\\], a strong interactive segmenter that performs best on our benchmark compared to other strong baselines \\[ [67](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib67 \"\"), [18](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib18 \"\")\\].\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |     |     |     |"
                },
                {
                  "partId": "point-results",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "#### Datasets.\n\nWe use a newly compiled suite of 23 datasets with diverse image distributions. Fig. [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F8 \"Figure 8 ‣ Task. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") lists the datasets and shows a sample from each one (see appendix Table [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.T7 \"Table 7 ‣ D.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\") for more details). We use all 23 datasets for mIoU evaluation. For the human study, we use the subset listed in Fig. [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F9 \"Figure 9 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") b (due to the resource requirements of such studies). This subset includes both datasets for which SAM outperforms and underperforms RITM according to automatic metrics.\n\n|     |\n| --- |\n| -200+20+40IoU delta at 1 center pointGTEA \\[ [34](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib34 \"\"), [63](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib63 \"\")\\]TrashCan \\[ [52](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib52 \"\")\\]DRAM \\[ [24](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib24 \"\")\\]PIDRay \\[ [104](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib104 \"\")\\]Cityscapes \\[ [25](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib25 \"\")\\]WoodScape \\[ [112](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib112 \"\")\\]IBD \\[ [17](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib17 \"\")\\]EgoHOS \\[ [113](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib113 \"\")\\]Plittersdorf \\[ [46](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib46 \"\")\\]VISOR \\[ [28](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib28 \"\"), [27](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib27 \"\")\\]NDISPark \\[ [22](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib22 \"\"), [23](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib23 \"\")\\]Hypersim \\[ [86](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib86 \"\")\\]OVIS \\[ [81](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib81 \"\")\\]ADE20K \\[ [117](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib117 \"\")\\]iShape \\[ [111](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib111 \"\")\\]ZeroWaste-f \\[ [6](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib6 \"\")\\]STREETS \\[ [91](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib91 \"\")\\]LVIS \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib44 \"\")\\]NDD20 \\[ [100](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib100 \"\")\\]TimberSeg \\[ [38](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib38 \"\")\\]DOORS \\[ [80](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib80 \"\")\\]BBBC038v1 \\[ [12](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib12 \"\")\\]PPDLS \\[ [74](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib74 \"\")\\]-21.4-15.0-6.5-5.8-2.0-0.6-0.3+0.8+1.5+1.8+2.7+6.1+7.0+7.8+8.8+9.1+17.3+18.5+21.1+28.9+41.1+44.7+46.9 |\n| (a) SAM _vs_. RITM \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\")\\] on 23 datasets |\n\n|     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |\n| (b) Mask quality ratings by human annotators |\n| |     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |\n| (c) Center points (default) | (d) Random points | |\n\nFigure 9: Point to mask evaluation on 23 datasets. (a) Mean IoU of SAM and the strongest single point segmenter, RITM \\[ [92](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib92 \"\")\\]. Due to ambiguity, a single mask may not match ground truth; circles show “oracle” results of the most relevant of SAM’s 3 predictions. (b) Per-dataset comparison of mask quality ratings by annotators from 1 (worst) to 10 (best). All methods use the ground truth mask center as the prompt. (c, d) mIoU with varying number of points. SAM significantly outperforms prior interactive segmenters with 1 point and is on par with more points. Low absolute mIoU at 1 point is the result of ambiguity.\n\n#### Results.\n\nFirst, we look at automatic evaluation on the full suite of 23 datasets using mIoU. We compare per-dataset results in Fig. [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F9 \"Figure 9 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") a against RITM. SAM yields higher results on 16 of the 23 datasets, by as much as ∼\\\\scriptstyle\\\\sim47 IoU. We also present an “oracle” result, in which the most relevant of SAM’s 3 masks is selected by comparing them to the ground truth, rather than selecting the most confident mask. This reveals the impact of ambiguity on automatic evaluation. In particular, with the oracle to perform ambiguity resolution, SAM outperforms RITM on _all_ datasets.\n\nResults of the human study are presented in Fig. [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F9 \"Figure 9 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") b. Error bars are 95% confidence intervals for mean mask ratings (all differences are significant; see § [E](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A5 \"Appendix E Human Study Experimental Design ‣ Segment Anything\") for details). We observe that the annotators consistently rate the quality of SAM’s masks substantially higher than the strongest baseline, RITM. An ablated, “ambiguity-unaware” version of SAM with a single output mask has consistently lower ratings, though still higher than RITM. SAM’s mean ratings fall between 7 and 9, which corresponds to the qualitative rating guideline: “ _A high score (7-9): The object is identifiable and errors are small and rare (e.g., missing a small, heavily obscured disconnected component, …)._” These results indicate that SAM has learned to segment valid masks from a single point. Note that for datasets like DRAM and IBD, where SAM is worse on automatic metrics, _it receives consistently higher ratings in the human study_.\n\nFig. [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F9 \"Figure 9 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") c shows additional baselines, SimpleClick \\[ [67](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib67 \"\")\\] and FocalClick \\[ [18](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib18 \"\")\\], which obtain lower single point performance than RITM and SAM. As the number of points increases from 1 to 9, we observe that the gap between methods decreases. This is expected as the task becomes easier; also, SAM is not optimized for the very high IoU regime. Finally, in Fig. [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F9 \"Figure 9 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 7.1 Zero-Shot Single Point Valid Mask Evaluation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") d we replace the default center point sampling with random point sampling. We observe that the gap between SAM and the baselines grows and SAM is able to achieve comparable results under either sampling method."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "text-model",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 7.5 Zero-Shot Text-to-Mask\n\n#### Approach.\n\nFinally, we consider an even higher-level task: segmenting objects from free-form text. This experiment is a proof-of-concept of SAM’s ability to process text prompts. While we used the exact same SAM in all prior experiments, for this one SAM’s training procedure is modified to make it text-aware, but in a way that does not require new text annotations. Specifically, for each manually collected mask with area larger than 1002\\\\textrm{100}^{\\\\textrm{2}} we extract the CLIP _image_ embedding. Then, during training, we prompt SAM with the extracted CLIP image embeddings as its first interaction. The key observation here is that because CLIP’s _image_ embeddings are trained to align with its _text_ embeddings, we can train with image embeddings, but use text embeddings for inference. That is, at inference time we run text through CLIP’s text encoder and then give the resulting text embedding as a prompt to SAM (see § [D.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.SS5 \"D.5 Zero-Shot Text-to-Mask ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\") for details).\n\n|     |     |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/a_wheel.png)✓ | ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/beaver_tooth_grille.png)✓ |\n| ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/a_wiper.png)✗ | ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/a_wiper_click.png)✓ |\n| ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/wipers.png)✗ | ![Refer to caption](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643v1/wipers_click.png)✓ |\n\nFigure 12: Zero-shot text-to-mask. SAM can work with simple and nuanced text prompts. When SAM fails to make a correct prediction, an additional point prompt can help.\n\n#### Results.\n\nWe show qualitative results in Fig. [12](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F12 \"Figure 12 ‣ Approach. ‣ 7.5 Zero-Shot Text-to-Mask ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\"). SAM can segment objects based on simple text prompts like “a wheel” as well as phrases like “beaver tooth grille”. When SAM fails to pick the right object from a text prompt only, an additional point often fixes the prediction, similar to \\[ [31](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib31 \"\")\\]."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "limitations",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "## 8 Discussion\n\n#### Foundation models.\n\nPre-trained models have been adapted to downstream tasks since the early days of machine learning \\[ [99](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib99 \"\")\\]. This paradigm has become increasingly important in recent years with a growing emphasis on scale, and such models have recently been (re-)branded as “foundation models”: _i.e_. models that are “trained on broad data at scale and are adaptable to a wide range of downstream tasks” \\[ [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib8 \"\")\\]. Our work correlates well with this definition, though we note that a foundation model for image segmentation is an inherently limited scope, since it represents an important, yet fractional, subset of computer vision. We also contrast one aspect of our approach with \\[ [8](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib8 \"\")\\], which emphasizes the role of _self-supervised_ learning in foundation models. While our model is initialized with a self-supervised technique (MAE \\[ [47](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib47 \"\")\\]), the vast majority of its capabilities come from large-scale _supervised_ training. In cases where data engines can scale available annotations, like ours, supervised training provides an effective solution.\n\n#### Compositionality.\n\nPre-trained models can power new capabilities even beyond ones imagined at the moment of training. One prominent example is how CLIP \\[ [82](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib82 \"\")\\] is used as a _component_ in larger systems, such as DALL⋅\\\\cdotE \\[ [83](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib83 \"\")\\]. Our goal is to make this kind of composition straightforward with SAM. We aim to achieve this by requiring SAM to predict a valid mask for a wide range of segmentation prompts. The effect is to create a reliable interface between SAM and other components. For example, MCC \\[ [106](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib106 \"\")\\] can easily use SAM to segment an object of interest and achieve strong generalization to unseen objects for 3D reconstruction from a single RGB-D image. In another example, SAM can be prompted with gaze points detected by a wearable device, enabling new applications. Thanks to SAM’s ability to generalize to new domains like ego-centric images, such systems work without need for additional training.\n\n#### Limitations.\n\nWhile SAM performs well in general, it is not perfect. It can miss fine structures, hallucinates small disconnected components at times, and does not produce boundaries as crisply as more computationally intensive methods that “zoom-in”, _e.g_.\\[ [18](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib18 \"\")\\]. In general, we expect dedicated interactive segmentation methods to outperform SAM when many points are provided, _e.g_.\\[ [67](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib67 \"\")\\]. Unlike these methods, SAM is designed for generality and breadth of use rather than high IoU interactive segmentation. Moreover, SAM can process prompts in real-time, but nevertheless SAM’s overall performance is not real-time when using a heavy image encoder. Our foray into the text-to-mask task is exploratory and not entirely robust, although we believe it can be improved with more effort. While SAM can perform many tasks, it is unclear how to design simple prompts that implement semantic and panoptic segmentation. Finally, there are domain-specific tools, such as \\[ [7](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib7 \"\")\\], that we expect to outperform SAM in their respective domains."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "detector-composition",
                  "citationId": "segment-anything-2023",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643",
                  "supportingPassage": "### 7.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation\n\n#### Approach.\n\nMoving to higher-level vision, we use SAM as the segmentation module of an instance segmenter. The implementation is simple: we run a object detector (the ViTDet used before) and prompt SAM with its output boxes. This illustrates _composing_ SAM in a larger system.\n\n#### Results.\n\nWe compare the masks predicted by SAM and ViTDet on COCO and LVIS in Table [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.T5 \"Table 5 ‣ Results. ‣ 7.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\"). Looking at the mask AP metric we observe gaps on both datasets, where SAM is reasonably close, though certainly behind ViTDet. By visualizing outputs, we observed that SAM masks are often qualitatively better than those of ViTDet, with crisper boundaries (see § [D.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.SS4 \"D.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\") and Fig. [16](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#A4.F16 \"Figure 16 ‣ Method. ‣ D.3 Zero-Shot Object Proposals ‣ Appendix D Experiment Implementation Details ‣ Segment Anything\")). To investigate this observation, we conducted an additional human study asking annotators to rate the ViTDet masks and SAM masks on the 1 to 10 quality scale used before. In Fig. [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F11 \"Figure 11 ‣ Results. ‣ 7.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\") we observe that SAM consistently outperforms ViTDet in the human study.\n\n|     |     |     |     |     |     |     |     |     |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | COCO \\[ [66](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib66 \"\")\\] | LVIS v1 \\[ [44](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib44 \"\")\\] |\n| method | AP | APS | APM | APL | AP | APS | APM | APL |\n| ViTDet-H \\[ [62](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#bib.bib62 \"\")\\] | 51.0 | 32.0 | 54.3 | 68.9 | 46.6 | 35.0 | 58.0 | 66.3 |\n| _zero-shot transfer methods (segmentation module only):_ |\n| SAM | 46.5 | 30.8 | 51.0 | 61.7 | 44.7 | 32.5 | 57.6 | 65.5 |\n\nTable 5: Instance segmentation results. SAM is prompted with ViTDet boxes to do zero-shot segmentation. The fully-supervised ViTDet outperforms SAM, but the gap shrinks on the higher-quality LVIS masks. Interestingly, SAM outperforms ViTDet according to human ratings (see Fig. [11](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.F11 \"Figure 11 ‣ Results. ‣ 7.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\")).Figure 11: Mask quality rating distribution from our human study for ViTDet and SAM, both applied to LVIS ground truth boxes. We also report LVIS and COCO ground truth quality. The legend shows rating means and 95% confidence intervals. Despite its lower AP (Table [5](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02643#S7.T5 \"Table 5 ‣ Results. ‣ 7.4 Zero-Shot Instance Segmentation ‣ 7 Zero-Shot Transfer Experiments ‣ Segment Anything\")), SAM has higher ratings than ViTDet, suggesting that ViTDet exploits biases in the COCO and LVIS training data.\n\nWe hypothesize that on COCO, where the mask AP gap is larger and the ground truth quality is relatively low (as borne out by the human study), ViTDet learns the specific biases of COCO masks. SAM, being a zero-shot method, is unable to exploit these (generally undesirable) biases. The LVIS dataset has higher quality ground truth, but there are still specific idiosyncrasies ( _e.g_., masks do not contain holes, they are simple polygons by construction) and biases for modal _vs_. amodal masks. Again, SAM is not trained to learn these biases, while ViTDet can exploit them."
                }
