                  "supportingPassage": "We use self-supervised pixelwise contrastive loss, as developed in [7, 8]. This learns a dense visual descriptor\nmapping which maps a full-resolution RGB image, RW ×H×3 to a dense descriptor space, RW ×H×D ,\n\n\n                                                       2\n\fwhere for each pixel we have a D-dimensional descriptor vector. Training is performed in a Siamese\nfashion, where a pair of RGB images, Ia and Ib are sampled from one RGBD video, and many pixel\nmatches and non-matches are generated from the pair of images. A pixel ua ∈R2 from image Ia is a match\nwith pixel ub from image Ib if they correspond to the same vertex of the dense 3D reconstruction (Figure\n1 (c-f)). The dense descriptor mapping is trained via pixelwise contrastive loss. The loss function aims\nto minimize the distance between descriptors corresponding to a match, while descriptors corresponding to\na non-match should be at least a distance M apart, where M is a margin parameter.\n\nFor our network we use 34-layer, stride-8 ResNet (pretrained on ImageNet), and then bilinearly upsample\nto produce a full resolution 640x480 image."
