                  "supportingPassage": "I-identity-basic (printed p. 82):\nVisual Servo Control\nPart I: Basic Approaches\nBY FRANÇOIS CHAUMETTE AND SETH HUTCHINSON\n\nhis article is the first of a two-part series on the topic\nof visual servo control—using computer vision data\nin the servo loop to control the motion of a robot. In\nthe present article, we describe the basic techniques that are\nby now well established in the field. We first give a general\noverview of the formulation of the visual servo control problem. We then describe the two archetypal visual servo control\nschemes: image-based and position-based visual servo control.\nFinally, we discuss performance and stability issues that pertain\nto these two schemes, motivating the second article in the\nseries, in which we consider advanced techniques.\n\nT\n\nIntroduction\n\nI-publication-imprint (printed p. 82):\n1070-9932/06/$20.00©2006 IEEE\n\nDECEMBER 2006\n\nI-local-stability (printed p. 89):\nwhich is known to be locally asymptotically stable in a neighborhood of e = e∗ = 0 if\n+ L > 0,\n\u0001\nL\ne e\n\n(20)\n\n+ L ∈ R6×6 . Indeed, only the linearized\n\u0001\nwhere L\ne e\n\u0001\n+ L e\u0001 has to be considered if we are inter\u0001\nsystem ė = −λL\ne e\nested in the local asymptotic stability [20].\nOnce again, if the features are chosen and the control\n+ are of full rank 6, then\n\u0001\nscheme designed so that Le and L\ne\ncondition (20) is ensured if the approximations involved in\n+ are not too coarse.\n\u0001\nL\ne\nTo end the demonstration of local asymptotic stability,"
