            "supportingPassage": "",
            "evidenceFailures": [],
            "sourceChecked": "imap-2021 (arXiv:2103.12352 abs, verbatim: \"a multilayer perceptron (MLP) can serve as the only scene representation in a real-time SLAM system for a handheld RGB-D camera... trained in live operation without prior data\")",
            "note": "Original four-cell tuple (JSON): [\"iMAP made a single multilayer perceptron the entire scene representation of a real-time SLAM system, trained online from a live RGB-D stream\",\"imap-2021 (arXiv:2103.12352 abs, verbatim: \\\"a multilayer perceptron (MLP) can serve as the only scene representation in a real-time SLAM system for a handheld RGB-D camera... trained in live operation without prior data\\\")\",\"V\",\"\"]. Original tuple SHA256: 40754e56b47c5d0d871d51a36f4ba21c07f3c9c9138378b9d26d85046c55be51. Historical source label preserved as a lead, not source proof. Current source-auditor review 2026-09-08T19:00:12.934Z, session 0a9a5dc1. iMAP original GET https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.12352v2, 2026-09-08T11:57:31.632923Z–11:57:34.154653Z, HTTP200, zero redirects,8480607 bytes; printed v2 dated13September2021. CPU/GPU model not specified. Current offline extraction equals preserved text; this review is not a new fetch or current-liveness check. Introduction and Section3 describe random initialization and live RGB-D fitting without prior training data; this does not exclude network architectural priors. Section3.2 maps position to colour and volume density without view direction; Section3.3 explicitly uses intrinsics K for rendering. No calibration-free or complete-geometry claim. Section3.4 separates current-pose optimization against a fixed scene network from joint network and selected-keyframe-pose mapping. Sections3.4–3.6 retain RGB-D keyframes, their poses and a copied network snapshot for information-gain selection and replay. The MLP is the dense map, not the entire memory state. Section4.4 and abstract report 10Hz tracking and 2Hz mapping on the stated desktop CPU/GPU; Table4 uses six/ten concurrent tracking/mapping iterations, 200 samples and window five. No exact hardware model, sensor rate or robot control rate is inferred. Section4.1 drops intermediate live frames but processes every captured frame in reported experiments; Section4.4 distinguishes seconds for coarse geometry from minutes for detail. Online operation is not instantaneous fine reconstruction. Conclusion and Replica/real-camera setup establish room-scale demonstrations; Section4.1 explicitly places mesh extraction outside SLAM. Neither claimed completeness nor a safety/collision guarantee is adopted. Both originals38/39 and their shared paragraph are one atomic correction; excluded ORB26/27 and31/33 remain held, including the orb-slam-2015 specific-distribution obligation. No whole-P1, article-date, registryURL, source-certification or acceptance claim. Josef's September7 'fix it all!' is routine correction authority, not personal certification. Evidence: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-neural-slam-integration-20260908/source-proof.json and extraction-verification.json.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "scene-representation-38-neural-slam-20260908",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "initialization",
                  "citationId": "imap-2021",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.12352v2",
                  "supportingPassage": "Our\nrandomly-initialised network is trained in live operation and\nwe do not require any prior training data. Our iMAP system\nis designed with a keyframe structure and multi-processing\ncomputation flow reminiscent of PTAM [11]. In a tracking\nprocess, running at over 10 Hz, we align live RGB-D observations with rendered depth and colour predictions from the\nMLP scene map. In parallel, a mapping process selects and\nmaintains a set of historic keyframes whose viewpoints span\nthe scene, and uses these to continually train and improve\nthe MLP, while jointly optimising the keyframe poses.\nIn both tracking and mapping, we dynamically sample\nthe most informative RGB-D pixels to reduce geometric\nuncertainty, achieving real-time speed. Our system runs\nin Python, and all optimisation is via a standard PyTorch\nframework [20] on a single desktop CPU/GPU system."
