                  "supportingPassage": "Artificial Intelligence researchers have been trying to get robot arms and hands to carry out manipulation of objects for over 65 years; since just a few years after the term Artificial Intelligence first appeared [in a proposal](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/idocs/DartmouthProposal.pdf) for a 1956 “Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence”. By 1961 Heinrich Ernst had produced a [PhD thesis](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/idocs/ErnstPhD.pdf) describing a computer controlled arm and hand that he had connected to the TX-0 computer at MIT, and had it picking up blocks and stacking them, and stunningly there is a [video](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/idocs/Ernst.mp4). His advisor was [Claude Shannon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon), and he also thanked [Marvin Minsky](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvin_Minsky) for his guidance, thus naming two of the four authors of the Dartmouth AI proposal."
