            "note": "Original four-cell tuple (JSON): {\"claim\":\"\\\"...front ends, CNNs and MFCCs, that baked in the priors the network supposedly did not need\\\"\",\"sourceChecked\":\"A Better Lesson + Brooks dexterity essay (both fetched, grepped)\",\"verdict\":\"C\",\"note\":\"Neither essay uses \\\"MFCC\\\". The speech-front-end argument (Mel filter banks, telephone-circuit heritage) appears in the 2025 dexterity essay, which never abbreviates it. Reworded to the sources' own terms with both citations. Same gloss in lib/competing-theses.ts fixed.\"}. Original tuple SHA256: 053dd66397d54b123cb134fed25f06745b2fbf154748223376d88e94bb965a29. Integrator source review 2026-09-13T17:34:04.420477+00:00: The first numbered objection explicitly identifies a human-designed CNN front end for translational invariance. The correction attributes that argument to Brooks, not a measured universal architectural or computational result. The later essay explicitly says the earlier reply did not make the speech argument and then develops it in section3.1. Both essays remain required for the combined historical attribution. Section3.1 traces the preprocessing to telephone transmission and lists FFTs, Mel filter banks and other techniques. Its immediately following paragraph says different implementations use different selections. These are examples in Brooks’s account, not a universal pipeline. MFCC lexical absence establishes no technical equivalence. The section3 opening explicitly calls leaving as much as possible to learning critical to the speech/image successes. This acknowledgement must accompany the criticism rather than turning Brooks into an opponent of learning gains. Supporting source record: tool-rendered canonical essay text returned tool-reported status 200 on 2026-09-13; origin transport and publication day were not independently observed. Both 2019 image and 2025 speech evidence are required (AND), with implementation variability and acknowledged learning gains. MFCC/MFCCs is absent from the retained author-text bodies; that lexical check does not define equivalence. The neighboring tactile claim and mixed table row 25 remain unadjudicated. Zero new retrieval. Exact provenance: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-brooks-theses-integration-20260913/source-proof.json. Native review by source-auditor 519d4fc5, not independent acceptance or owner source certification. Whole P1, publication day, chronology, article date and unselected originals remain unaccepted and unchanged.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "competing-theses-brooks-5-20260913",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "cnn",
                  "citationId": "brooks-better-lesson-2019",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/a-better-lesson/",
                  "supportingPassage": "1. One of the most celebrated successes of Deep Learning is image labeling, using CNNs, Convolutional Neural Networks, but the very essence of CNNs is that the front end of the network is designed by humans to manage translational invariance, the idea that objects can appear anywhere in the frame. To have a Deep Learning network also have to learn that seems pedantic to the extreme, and will drive up the computational costs of the learning by many orders of magnitude."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "extension",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Most importantly I pointed out that the image labeling case was not end-to-end starting with images and ending with labels. Instead it uses a convolutional network as a front end to structure the way the learning algorithm has access to the images. While I did not make a similar argument for speech to text, nor the as-then-yet-unannounced LLMs, I will make the the case that all three succeeded due to engineers building case specific pre-processing that relied on directly simulating (without learning) parts of human physiology.\n\nHere are the accommodations made to learning, in each of the three cases, in terms of hard coding front end processing of data."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "speech",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "There are many ways the sound signal could get into the computer for this learning. We could take the analog output of a microphone and digitize the loudness of the signal many tens of thousands of times a second and have that as the input to the learning. But in practice that is not how it is done.\n\nInstead it relies on a technology developed for practical spoken communication over world wide telephone networks in the 20th century where the signals were compressed for individual voice circuits so that more calls could fit on a single wire. This work determined aspects of the signal that had to be preserved so that a human could understand what had been said by the distant speaker. And if a human could understand such compressed signals that says that all the information that is necessary to understand speech is still in that signal.\n\nThe inputs to various speech to text learning systems vary, but here are some of the common pre-processing steps taken. The analog input signal is sampled at a fixed frequency, perhaps 16kHz, then a high pass filter boosts higher frequencies as they are important for detecting consonants, then the signal is cut into frames, say 25ms long with 10ms overlap, for instance, and then each frame is conditioned so that subsequent Fast Fourier Transforms (FFTs) will not be compromised by the shortness of the window. Somewhere along the way there may be some noise reduction. Then the signal is subdivided into frequency bands using one or more methods such as FFT, Mel filter banks, logarithm of outputs, and cosine transforms. In some implementations there is initial training on just the frames so that language dependent frame signatures can be recognized early in the deep network.\n\nDifferent implementations use different selections of these and other techniques, but the point is that **after** all this, **then** end-to-end learning is let loose on the output of all this input signal transformation.\n\nFurther, all that signal transformation was originally developed so that human speech could be stored and the listened to in distant places and times. The important thing about those transformations was that they allowed the human listening mechanism for understanding speech to be used with no change to the human."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "success",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "In the last two decades speech to text, image labeling, and fluid language generated by Large Language Models (LLMs) have all been transformed, in spectacular ways, by end-to-end learning, using linear threshold neural models.\n\nFor the speech and image cases the new methods showed radical increases in performances. In both cases leaving as much as possible to the learning methods was critical for that success. That meant, for speech, getting rid of explicit models for phonemes (which are very language dependent), which had dominated all previously recent approaches. For image labeling it meant getting rid of any notion of line (boundary) finding, shape, shading, or color constancy, all of which had dominated recent work on image understanding.\n\nLLMs showed proficiency with language and answering general questions (with a strong tendency, still today, for rabid confabulations) that was beyond anything anyone was expecting was around the corner. And they had done it by eliminating any references or direct experience in the world of anything other than language. They were self-contained language machines, with none of the long expected grounding in experience in the real world, that everyone had expected, the believed to be [_symbol grounding problem_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol_grounding_problem). \\[\\[Even Alan Turing had brought this up in his brilliant [Intelligent M](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/idocs/IntelligentMachinery.pdf) [achinery](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/idocs/IntelligentMachinery.pdf), written in 1948 but not published until 1970, in Machine Intelligence 5, edited by Bernard Meltzer and Donald Michie. There (on page 13 of that volume) Turing had said that the sure way to get to an intelligent machine was to “take a man as a whole and to try replace all parts of him by machinery”. Today we might say “build a humanoid”; prescient! As for the grounding in real world experience he went on to say: “In order that the machine should have a chance of finding things out for itself it should be allowed to roam the countryside, and _the danger to the ordinary citizen would be serious_” (my emphasis). He concluded that it was too hard to do with the technology of the day. Two more instances of prescience.\\]\\]\n\nThese were radical changes and head spinning for most researchers, including me. But the new methods undeniably worked much better than anything we had seen in the past."
                }
              ],
              "structuralFailures": [],
              "adjudicationFailures": []
            },
            "legacyPointer": {
              "kind": "locator",
              "value": "https://rodneybrooks.com/a-better-lesson/"
            }
          },
