            "sourceChecked": "A Better Lesson – Rodney Brooks — https://rodneybrooks.com/a-better-lesson/ (web fetch tool-rendered author text; result 2026-09-13T15:12:19.623Z; tool status 200)",
            "note": "Original four-cell tuple (JSON): {\"claim\":\"\\\"his rebuttal quotes the essay at length\\\"\",\"sourceChecked\":\"A Better Lesson (fetched)\",\"verdict\":\"C\",\"note\":\"the rebuttal contains no extended quotation; it paraphrases (\\\"In his post he argues...\\\") and closes \\\"This review... is seventy six words shorter than Sutton's post.\\\" Corrected.\"}. Original tuple SHA256: 571bddf59e9631b268af656ee0cad64e377b67b94441a1a2148a5941ed646f8e. Integrator source review 2026-09-13T17:34:04.420477+00:00: The opening says In his post he argues, then describes Sutton in Brooks’s own narrative and explicitly disagrees. This supports attribution as a paraphrase, not an extended Sutton quotation or independent verification of Sutton. The complete closing sentence explicitly says This review, including this comment, is seventy six words shorter. Retain it as Brooks’s self-description; neither an independent count nor a chronology finding follows. Supporting source record: tool-rendered canonical essay text returned tool-reported status 200 on 2026-09-13; origin transport and publication day were not independently observed. Self-described length only; no Sutton retrieval, independent word count, or chronology-row completion. Distinct continued-section identity is retained. Zero new retrieval. Exact provenance: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-brooks-theses-integration-20260913/source-proof.json. Native review by source-auditor 519d4fc5, not independent acceptance or owner source certification. Whole P1, publication day, chronology, article date and unselected originals remain unaccepted and unchanged.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "competing-theses-brooks-10-20260913",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "paraphrase",
                  "citationId": "brooks-better-lesson-2019",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/a-better-lesson/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Just last week Rich Sutton published a very short blog post titled [The Bitter Lesson](http://www.incompleteideas.net/IncIdeas/BitterLesson.html). I’m going to try to keep this review shorter than his post. Sutton is well known for his long and sustained contributions to reinforcement learning.\n\nIn his post he argues, using many good examples, that over the 70 year history of AI, more computation and less built in knowledge has always won out as the best way to build Artificial Intelligence systems. This resonates with a current mode of thinking among many of the newer entrants to AI that it is better to design learning networks and put in massive amounts of computer power, than to try to design a structure for computation that is specialized in any way for the task. I must say, however, that at a two day work shop on Deep Learning last week at the National Academy of Science, the latter idea was much more in vogue, something of a backlash against exactly what Sutton is arguing.\n\nI think Sutton is wrong for a number of reasons."
                },
                {
