            "note": "Verified against the arXiv abstract page fetched this session: the paper's own framing is grounding “by means of pretrained skills” with the language model supplying “high-level knowledge about the procedures” while “value functions associated with these skills provide the grounding”. This supports the article's characterization of the 2022 architecture as a separate LLM over a fixed (predefined) skill set. Claim unchanged; evidence fields completed.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "competing-theses-ct13-saycan-20260915",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "ct13-pretrained-skills",
                  "citationId": "saycan-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.01691",
                  "supportingPassage": "We propose to provide real-world grounding by means of pretrained skills, which are used to constrain the model to propose natural language actions that are both feasible and contextually appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "ct13-llm-highlevel",
                  "citationId": "saycan-2022",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.01691",
                  "supportingPassage": "We show how low-level skills can be combined with large language models so that the language model provides high-level knowledge about the procedures for performing complex and temporally-extended instructions, while value functions associated with these skills provide the grounding necessary to connect this knowledge to a particular physical environment."
