                  "supportingPassage": "Two partial paths exist for reducing dependence on expensive real-world collection. The first is [world models](https://www.bvp.com/atlas/can-world-models-unlock-general-purpose-robotics)—neural networks trained on internet-scale video that learn physics through observation. The results are promising: Meta's V-JEPA 2, trained on over a million hours of internet video, achieved 80% zero-shot pick-and-place success on real robot arms with just 62 hours of additional robot-specific data. But world models are themselves expensive to train—NVIDIA's Cosmos required 10,000 H100 GPUs over three months. This is not a free shortcut. It is a capital-intensive bet that physics learned from video generalizes to physical manipulation. The early results are encouraging; full generalization remains unproven.\n\nThe second path is simulation and reinforcement learning. Sim-to-real transfer works well for locomotion, but manipulation is harder. How a robot hand interacts with a soft object, how fabric slides between fingers, how liquids behave — these require a fidelity that current simulators don't achieve. Sim-to-real for manipulation is an open research problem, not a solved one."
                }
              ],
