            "sourceChecked": "Why Today’s Humanoids Won’t Learn Dexterity – Rodney Brooks — https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/ (web fetch tool-rendered author text; result 2026-09-13T15:12:43.252Z; tool status 200)",
            "note": "Original four-cell tuple (JSON): {\"claim\":\"Humanoid/task-specific thesis: \\\"pure fantasy thinking\\\"; \\\"no human-like robot hand has survived real-world deployment at industrial durability\\\"; Brooks's Robust.AI builds warehouse robots\",\"sourceChecked\":\"Brooks essay (fetched)\",\"verdict\":\"V\",\"note\":\"\\\"believing that this will happen any time within decades is pure fantasy thinking\\\"; \\\"No one has managed to get articulated fingers... robust enough... for real industrial applications\\\" + \\\"none have inspired designs that have made it into deployment in real world applications\\\"; \\\"At my company we build robots that are deployed in warehouses\\\"\"}. Original tuple SHA256: 7bc69a08f323a346de676a65e478ebe709ab59534ea62c7e40070dd6460586c4. Integrator source review 2026-09-13T17:34:04.420477+00:00: The prolog’s pure fantasy thinking phrase is explicitly In my opinion and refers to plug-compatible replacement of human manual jobs at lower prices and just as well. It does not address every humanoid or every specialized deployment forever. Section2.1 joins robustness, sufficient force and lifetime for industrial articulated fingers. All three remain attributed assessments, not our mechanical measurements or independently established industry absence. The author says human-like hand designs have not reached real-world applications and have little general dexterity. Preserve his date-scoped assessment without turning it into our census or a claim that no humanoid robot has been deployed. The prolog says At my company we build robots that are deployed in warehouses. This supports his first-person warehouse account, without a new ownership or commercial performance assertion. The same body explicitly discloses that Brooks and Holson work closely at Robust.AI. Paired with the distinct warehouse paragraph this supports the affiliation context, not unobserved corporate particulars. Section2 names Baxter and Sawyer and says They were deployed in factories around the world. Keeping that author report prevents a false no-humanoid-ever interpretation of the prolog. Section6 forecasts changing forms, specialized jobs and continued humanoid naming over the next fifteen years. It qualifies the earlier forecast and is not a rolling fifteen-year forecast made by this wiki. Supporting source record: tool-rendered canonical essay text returned tool-reported status 200 on 2026-09-13; origin transport and publication day were not independently observed. Keep the plug-compatible target, personal/date scope, articulated-finger conjunction, warehouse/Robust.AI context and earlier/future humanoid qualifications together. No universal impossibility, independent durability census, current forecast, video-derived test or external physiology certification. Zero new retrieval. Exact provenance: validation/brand-v2-editorial/source-recovery-20260906/convergence-brooks-theses-integration-20260913/source-proof.json. Native review by source-auditor 519d4fc5, not independent acceptance or owner source certification. Whole P1, publication day, chronology, article date and unselected originals remain unaccepted and unchanged.",
            "compound": {
              "planId": "competing-theses-brooks-24-20260913",
              "evidence": [
                {
                  "partId": "humanoid-opinion",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "For both these companies and probably several others the general plan is that humanoid robots will be “plug compatible” with humans and be able to step in and do the manual things that humans do at lower prices and just as well. In my opinion, believing that this will happen any time within decades is pure fantasy thinking. But many are predicting that it will happen in as soon as two years, and the more conservative hypenotists believe it will have significant economic impact within five years."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "industrial-fingers",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Schunk, a German company, [sells](https://schunk.com/de/en/gripping-systems/parallel-gripper/c/PUB_8295) over 1,000 varieties of parallel jaw grippers, both electric and pneumatic (using compressed air), for robot arms. It also sells some three fingered radially symmetric hands and a few other specialized grippers. No one has managed to get _articulated fingers_ (i.e., fingers with joints in them) that are robust enough, have enough force, nor enough lifetime, for real industrial applications."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "deployment",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "No human-like robot hands have demonstrated much in the way of dexterity, in any general sense. And none have inspired designs that have made it into deployment in real world applications. The approaches to dexterity have been very mathematical and geometrical, and they have just not produced anything like human dexterity."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "warehouse",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "At my company we build robots that are deployed in warehouses. They have a new fangled locomotion system based on “wheels” (and yes our locomotion system is actually new fangled and did not exist at all or anywhere just two years ago).  I have had VCs to whom we have pitched (as is the parlance in startup land) for funding to scale to meet our customer pull, question why we would possibly do that as _everyone knows_ that two legged, and two armed, humanoid robots will take over most human jobs in two years."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "robust",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "You might see pretty videos of human-like robot hands doing one particular task, but they do not generalize at all well beyond that task. In a light hearted, but very insightful, [recent blog post](https://generalrobots.substack.com/p/benjies-humanoid-olympic-games), Benjie Holson (full disclosure: Benjie and I work together closely at Robust.AI) lays out fifteen tasks that any eight year old human can do, in a proposed humanoid robot Olympics. With medals. For instance, one challenge is for a humanoid robot folding laundry to hang a men’s dress shirt which starts with one sleeve inside out, and to have at least one button buttoned. Another is to clean peanut butter off its own hand. And you can’t say “Oh, that would be better done by a different kind of robot mechanism.” No, it is central to the case for humanoid robots that they can do all the tasks that humans can do. Once you see Benjie’s fifteen challenge tasks, it is pretty easy to come up with another fifteen or thirty more dexterous tasks which have very little in common with any of his, but which all of us humans can do without a second thought. And then there are the hard things that we can all do if we have to."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "earlier-deployment",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Besides the early work in Japan on humanoid robots there have been many academic groups across the world that have worked on robots with human form, with and without legs, and with and without arms. My own research group at MIT started building the humanoid Cog in 1992, and we developed seven different platforms, and then I founded Rethink Robotics in 2008, and we sold thousands of two models of humanoids, Baxter and Sawyer. They were deployed in factories around the world. Some of my former post-docs returned to Italy and started the RoboCub open source humanoid project, which has enabled many tens of humanoid robots to be built in AI Labs all over the world."
                },
                {
                  "partId": "future",
                  "citationId": "brooks-dexterity-2025",
                  "sourceUrl": "https://rodneybrooks.com/why-todays-humanoids-wont-learn-dexterity/",
                  "supportingPassage": "Following that pattern, what it means to be a _humanoid robot_ will change over time.\n\nBefore too long (and we already start to see this) humanoid robots will get wheels for feet, at first two, and later maybe more, with nothing that any longer really resembles human legs in gross form. But they will still be called _humanoid robots_.\n\nThen there will be versions which variously have one, two, and three arms. Some of those arms will have five fingered hands, but a lot will have two fingered parallel jaw grippers. Some may have suction cups. But they will still be called _humanoid robots_.\n\nThen there will be versions which have a lot of sensors that are not passive cameras, and so they will have eyes that see with active light, or in non-human frequency ranges, and they may have eyes in their hands, and even eyes looking down from near their crotch to see the ground so that they can locomote better over uneven surfaces. But they will still be called _humanoid robots_.\n\nThere will be many, many robots with different forms for different specialized jobs that humans can do. But they will all still be called _humanoid robots_.\n\nAnd a lot of money will have disappeared, spent on trying to squeeze performance, any performance, from today’s _humanoid robots_. But those robots will be long gone and mostly conveniently forgotten.\n\nThat is the next fifteen years for you."
                }
              ],
